    "description": "Official poverty line rises to  Rs. 17,592 in June",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/Official-poverty-line-rises-to-Rs-17-592-in-June/56-795898",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1786494333.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-12-dailyftlk-df3ea7a5-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "28376a4a2128548edc2918d4575a1c37",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1662,
    "chunk_text": "Official poverty line rises to  Rs. 17,592 in June\n\nGampaha highest at Rs. 18,579; Moneragala lowest at Rs. 16,821\n\nNational threshold rises Rs. 277 from May and Rs. 1,108 from year earlier\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s national official poverty line increased to Rs. 17,592 per person per month in June 2026 from Rs. 17,315 in May, according to the latest estimates released by the Department of Census and Statistics (DCS).\n\nThe official poverty line, which represents the minimum monthly expenditure per person required to meet basic needs, rose by Rs. 277, or 1.6%, from the previous month.\n\nCompared with June 2025, when the national official poverty line stood at Rs. 16,484, the latest estimate represents an increase of Rs. 1,108, or 6.7%, over the past year.\n\nDistrict-level estimates continued to show differences in minimum expenditure requirements. Gampaha recorded the highest official poverty line in June at Rs. 18,579 per person per month, marginally above Colombo at Rs. 18,575, followed by Nuwara Eliya at Rs. 18,500.\n\nAt the other end, Moneragala recorded the lowest threshold at Rs. 16,821, followed by Kilinochchi at Rs. 16,995 and Hambantota at Rs. 17,092.\n\nThe DCS said the national official poverty line for June was Rs. 17,592. District poverty lines are re-estimated to account for price differences using the National Consumer Price Index (NCPI), with the June increase reflecting the higher NCPI compared with the preceding month.\n\nThe measure, however, is frequently criticised by Opposition politicians who contend that an individual cannot meet basic needs on around Rs. 17,600 a month amid higher food, utility, transport and other living costs."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-12-dailyftlk-df3ea7a5",
    "num": "dailyftlk-df3ea7a5",
    "date_str": "2026-08-12",
    "description": "Official poverty line rises to  Rs. 17,592 in June",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/Official-poverty-line-rises-to-Rs-17-592-in-June/56-795898",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1786494333.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-12-dailyftlk-df3ea7a5-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e5587fd6f05155f57cfe2b8347b8deca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1014,
    "chunk_text": "The measure, however, is frequently criticised by Opposition politicians who contend that an individual cannot meet basic needs on around Rs. 17,600 a month amid higher food, utility, transport and other living costs.\n\nThere is, however, a legitimate question over the underlying benchmark. The DCS notes that the official poverty line is updated based on the 2012/13 Household Income and Expenditure Survey, with subsequent price movements incorporated through the NCPI.\n\nA consumption basket anchored to household spending patterns more than a decade ago may not fully capture changes in what households now consume or regard as essential.\n\nTherefore, criticism that the poverty threshold appears low is not without basis. But portraying Rs. 17,592 as the DCS claiming that this amount is sufficient for an individual to live normally would be misleading. The more pertinent debate is whether the basket and methodology underpinning the threshold still capture the minimum needs of Sri Lankan households in 2026."
