    "md5": "e05a3dfd0cc9738b0198f983a510337d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1040,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka has witnessed numerous infrastructure developments that ultimately took a toll on the environment. Construction of road networks via elephant migratory routes, constructing commercial establishments and human settlements along elephant corridors have aggravated the conflict further. It was very much later that government officials realised the need to have wildlife underpasses at least as a pilot project to mitigate the conflict.\n\n\u2018Bringing the world together to help elephants\u2019 is the theme for World Elephant Day which was commemorated on Wednesday (August 12). World Elephant Day was co-founded on August 12, 2012 by Canadian Patricia Sims and the Elephant Reintroduction Foundation of Thailand, an initiative of HM Queen Sirikit of Thailand.\n\nSri Lanka is still trying to find middle ground to help her elephants, a species protected by the Fauna and Flora Protection Ordinance. But if this middle ground is not reached in the immediate future, Sri Lanka\u2019s jungles will lose one of its endangered and iconic species."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
