    "md5": "c82163b326845c6fa5982f5bb8f18835",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1612,
    "chunk_text": "Apart from objections based on religious grounds, patriarchal attitudes and conventional social and cultural norms harmful to women dominated the debate within parliament and drowned out the opposition outside parliament. The fear that decriminalisation would open the \u2018floodgates\u2019, encourage promiscuity and indecency, especially among the young, and lead to the breakdown of traditional power structures within the family and generally impose severe consequences on \u2018our culture\u2019 prevailed.\n\nOne of the significant challenges to legal reform is a lack of consensus and a coordinated approach on the issue. The abortion debate in Sri Lanka is shaped by religious opposition, deep-rooted patriarchy that regulates female morality and sexuality and political inertia. The recent study on public attitudes and perceptions, particularly among young people, regarding abortion, conducted by the Family Planning Association of Sri Lanka in 2023, reinforces some of these arguments, also highlighting self-imposed restrictions by women based on religious and moral convictions. Opinions among physicians and healthcare providers also differ.\n\nTherefore, since its inception, the abortion debate has been centred on two grounds: decriminalisation and legalisation of abortion, recognising women\u2019s right to bodily autonomy and improving public and maternal health by managing maternal deaths arising out of abortions or expanding family planning and reproductive health programmes to prevent unwanted pregnancies. The outcome is a piecemeal approach to legal and health reforms that undermines both objectives."
  },
