    "date_str": "2026-08-13",
    "description": "More competition  better  prices \u2013 why the CEP 4 tender risks wasting public money",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/news-features/More-competition-better-prices-why-the-CEP-4-tender-risks-wasting-public-money/131-348078",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1786642217.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-38563a93-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9813d8546d163f49f4332b2e649fb2bf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1981,
    "chunk_text": "More competition  better  prices \u2013 why the CEP 4 tender risks wasting public money\n\nSenior engineer questions whether stringent criteria could limit bidders and push project costs higher\n\nThe senior engineer pointed out that the fundamental issue is not whether the Road Development Authority (RDA) has followed procurement procedures, but whether the conditions set for the tender will actually create sufficient competition\n\nAnother issue raised by the engineer is the decision to restrict the tender to local bidders and not permit foreign contractors or local\u2013foreign joint ventures\n\nAmong the questions that should be answered, he said, were how many contractors the RDA expects to qualify, why foreign contractors have been excluded, why local\u2013foreign joint ventures have not been permitted and what measures have been taken to increase competition\n\nThe Government is preparing to award contracts worth a staggering Rs. 235.5 billion for the Kurunegala to Dambulla section of the Central Expressway, but a senior civil engineer has raised concerns that the tender conditions could result in too few contractors competing for the work, potentially putting billions of rupees of public money at risk.\n\nAccording to the senior engineer, the fundamental issue is not whether the Road Development Authority (RDA) has followed procurement procedures, but whether the conditions set for the tender will actually create sufficient competition.\n\n\u201cThe basic rule of public procurement is simple. The more qualified contractors competing for a project, the greater the pressure on them to offer competitive prices. If you have only a handful of contractors competing for a large number of packages, that pressure disappears,\u201d the engineer told the Daily Mirror.\n\nThe RDA published the Invitation for Bids for Central Expressway Project Section 4 (CEP 4), Kurunegala to Dambulla, on July 30, covering 12 contract packages with a combined estimated value of Rs. 235.529 billion."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-38563a93",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-38563a93",
    "date_str": "2026-08-13",
    "description": "More competition  better  prices \u2013 why the CEP 4 tender risks wasting public money",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/news-features/More-competition-better-prices-why-the-CEP-4-tender-risks-wasting-public-money/131-348078",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1786642217.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-38563a93-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "28495c1633f7da1b27d26a8c31ceb8ee",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1947,
    "chunk_text": "The RDA published the Invitation for Bids for Central Expressway Project Section 4 (CEP 4), Kurunegala to Dambulla, on July 30, covering 12 contract packages with a combined estimated value of Rs. 235.529 billion.\n\nThe engineer said the concerns become more significant when the CEP 4 criteria are compared with the recently tendered CEP 3 Section 2, where only six local contractors reportedly qualified for seven packages.\n\n\u201cWhat happened with CEP 3 should be a warning. There were seven packages but only six qualified contractors. That means the number of contractors was already smaller than the number of contracts available. When you then increase the value of the project to Rs. 235 billion and introduce criteria that are, in several respects, more demanding, the obvious question is how many contractors will actually qualify,\u201d the engineer said.\n\nUnder CEP 3 Section 2, the seven packages had a combined estimated value of around Rs. 93.6 billion and required a minimum CIDA grade of CS1.\n\nThe senior engineer pointed out that CEP 4 is significantly larger, with 12 packages worth around Rs. 235.5 billion.\n\nHowever, six of the CEP 4 packages require a CS2 grade, while the remaining six require CS1 together with additional qualifications relating to bridges and, in some cases, buildings.\n\nThe prior similar-contract value required under CEP 4 also ranges from Rs. 7.1 billion to Rs. 9.2 billion, compared to around Rs. 5.8 billion to Rs. 7.7 billion under CEP 3 Section 2.\n\nAnnual turnover requirements for CEP 4 range from Rs. 4.9 billion to Rs. 9 billion, while financial resource requirements range from Rs. 670 million to Rs. 1.25 billion, depending on the package.\n\n\u201cIf six contractors were able to qualify for CEP 3 under criteria that were less demanding in several respects, how many contractors will qualify for CEP 4? That is something the RDA should be able to answer publicly before the bids are opened,\u201d he said."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
