    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6b1e86dae2ebc14710561306c19e45d3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1786,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cIf six contractors were able to qualify for CEP 3 under criteria that were less demanding in several respects, how many contractors will qualify for CEP 4? That is something the RDA should be able to answer publicly before the bids are opened,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cI am not saying that the RDA has broken the procurement rules. That is not the point. You can follow every procedure correctly and still end up with a tender that does not provide sufficient competition. Procedure and value for money are two different things,\u201d he said.\n\nAccording to him, the ultimate test of a tender should be whether enough qualified companies are able to compete.\n\n\u201cThe purpose of procurement is not simply to tick boxes and say the procedure was followed. The purpose is to get the best possible value for the taxpayer. If you have 12 packages and only six companies qualify, you have a fundamental competition\n\nHe argued that contractors competing against one another would normally have a stronger incentive to sharpen their prices, potentially bringing bids closer to or below the Engineer\u2019s Estimate.\n\nConversely, a limited field of bidders could reduce that pressure.\n\n\u201cWhen contractors know there are only a few companies capable of bidding, the competitive pressure is naturally reduced. On a Rs. 235 billion project, even a relatively small difference between the Engineer\u2019s Estimate and the final contract prices can translate into billions of rupees,\u201d the engineer said.\n\nAnother issue raised by the engineer is the decision to restrict the tender to local bidders and not permit foreign contractors or local\u2013foreign\n\nHe questioned whether opening the tender to international companies, particularly through partnerships with local contractors, could widen the pool of bidders."
  },
