    "chunk_size_bytes": 1570,
    "chunk_text": "A regulatory gap has persisted since the early 1990s, with the apex investment agency bypassing the national environmental regulator to issue its own compliance certificates. According to official audit responses contained in the report, \u201cSince 1992, the practice for issuing Environmental Protection Licenses (EPLs) to industries located within the designated zones has been, that the Board of Investment (BOI) issues EPLs directly, without obtaining concurrence from the Central Environmental Authority (CEA).\u201d\n\nThe CEA has since pushed back against the three-decade practice, seeking formal oversight over the Katunayake, Biyagama, and Koggala Export Processing Zones. The BOI confirmed that \u201cthe CEA has now informed that their concurrence is required for the issuance of EPLs to industries in these three zones as well,\u201d prompting talks between the two agencies. The BOI noted that \u201cthis matter was discussed with the Director General of the CEA on 22.01.2025, during which it was agreed to formalize the arrangement through a Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) between the BOI and CEA.\u201d A draft agreement was submitted to the CEA in April 2025 and remains pending a response.\n\nBeyond the licensing dispute, the audit and regulatory records point to compliance issues at individual zones. In the Kandy Industrial Park, authorities flagged a factory over the quality of its treated wastewater, compounded by an expired environmental license; the BOI said the park\u2019s zonal EPL \u201cwill also be considered accordingly\u201d once the factory secures a renewal."
