    "md5": "7c7eae30bd8ab2b6c29e749edaf4958c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1943,
    "chunk_text": "UNFPA and NYSC empower 100 youth to lead local disaster preparedness and response\n\nOn International Youth Day 2026, the United Nations Population Fund (UNFPA), in partnership with the National Youth Services Council (NYSC) and under the auspices of the Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports, held a national, simulation-based program titled \u2018The Resilience Lab: Local Youth Action in Crisis Response.\u2019\n\nOrganised around the global theme \u2018Different Contexts, Shared Aspirations,\u2019 the initiative gathered over 100 youth leaders representing Sri Lanka\u2019s island-wide Youth Club network at the NYSC Auditorium in Maharagama. The event aimed to shift the national disaster management paradigm from reactive post-disaster response toward proactive pre-disaster mitigation by engaging the youth in terms of early warning dissemination, and community resilience.\n\nDrawing lessons from recent crises such as Cyclone Ditwah, which affected 2.2 million people, claimed over 600 lives, and damaged nearly 115,000 homes, the program highlighted the vital role of youth as first responders, community mobilisers, and early warning communicators.\n\nThrough realistic tabletop simulations covering multi-hazard scenarios (floods and landslides) and the epidemic of dengue, participants practiced making critical preparedness decisions. The exercises emphasised reaching the \u2018last person\u2019, including pregnant women, older persons, and persons with disabilities, while integrating Sexual and Reproductive Health and Rights (SRHR) and gender considerations into emergency response.\n\nThe initiative was delivered in close technical collaboration with the Disaster Management Centre (DMC), the Sri Lanka Red Cross Society (SLRC), and the Family Planning Association of Sri Lanka (FPASL).\n\n\u25cf 4.78 million youth: According to 2024 census data, young people aged 15\u201329 account for nearly 22% of Sri Lanka\u2019s population (roughly 1 in every 5 citizens)."
  },
