    "md5": "7093b677201498bcc6e1a5cb21766e96",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1809,
    "chunk_text": "The initiative was delivered in close technical collaboration with the Disaster Management Centre (DMC), the Sri Lanka Red Cross Society (SLRC), and the Family Planning Association of Sri Lanka (FPASL).\n\n\u25cf 4.78 million youth: According to 2024 census data, young people aged 15\u201329 account for nearly 22% of Sri Lanka\u2019s population (roughly 1 in every 5 citizens).\n\nNational Youth Services Council (NYSC) Chairman and Youth Affairs Director General Attorney-at-Law Supun Wijerathna said: \u201cWe need to have a clear vision of what we are trying to build. Cyclone Ditwah was one of the most severe natural disasters Sri Lanka has experienced, leaving large numbers of people displaced and affected. In the aftermath of this disaster, there was an urgent need for the National Youth Services Council to intervene swiftly. The NYSC has a strong network of young people who have the time, flexibility, skills, energy and commitment to actively contribute when such a disaster strikes. A key objective of this programme is therefore to build proactive youth leadership, not simply young people who respond after a disaster occurs, but young leaders who can help disseminate early warnings, organise communities and take active steps to protect lives.\u201d\n\nUNFPA Sri Lanka Assistant Representative Dr. Dayanath Ranatunga said: \u201cYouth are not merely passive recipients of disaster relief; they are active communicators, first responders, and community mobilisers. The success of an early warning system lies in reaching the last person, the elderly living alone, a person with a disability, or a pregnant mother needing healthcare. By equipping our youth network with technical skills and integrating gender and SRHR considerations, we transform youth enthusiasm into an organised, life-saving national asset.\u201d"
  },
