    "md5": "ac2a88ae7308fc95b52f5ed0abe2c723",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2010,
    "chunk_text": "These are not simply public-relations numbers, although in a sense they can serve as some sort of positive propaganda. In fact, they are the physical statistics of peace \u2013 where once the terrific tolls of a war-riven nation dominated the news and public concern indices.\n\nThere is even a beautiful irony for those willing to accommodate an alternative spirit of peace-building: because more than 3,000 people have worked in Sri Lanka\u2019s mine-action sector, including many from communities directly affected by the conflict as well as former combatants. About a third of the mine-clearing workforce is female. And MAG has also pioneered a staff-transition program because the people who have spent years making Sri Lanka safe will themselves need livelihoods when there are finally no mines left to clear.\n\nThat target does not tantamount to reconciliation per se, perhaps you might say. But it is peace-building with its sleeves rolled up for the serious work of transitioning from a post-war to a post-conflict society. And de-mining is one of the most concrete enabling activities on the long tough roads to reconciliation; so hopefully it will one day, sooner than later, herald justice with a smile beginning to crease her more often than not furrowed brow.\n\nIt is also why MAG\u2019s intervention should not be dismissed as celebrity public relations. Of course, it is public relations of a sort\u2026 for Rosamund Pike\u2019s ambassadorial job is precisely to make people look, listen and preferably contribute money to fund the peace.\n\nThe question skeptics may well ask as a matter of form, almost, is whether all of this type of publicity would produce something useful. And a fatal error would be to conflate MAG\u2019s actual operational impact with Rosamund Pike\u2019s arguably marginal celebrity-driven contribution to the cause. But in this case, perhaps, there is a strong inclination to suggest (and also, hope) that it will. Yet only time will tell if that hope sprang as fruitlessly as a pike on a hook."
  },
