    "md5": "f90cfb254b4b2f018a687f555e24ab0a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1977,
    "chunk_text": "The question skeptics may well ask as a matter of form, almost, is whether all of this type of publicity would produce something useful. And a fatal error would be to conflate MAG\u2019s actual operational impact with Rosamund Pike\u2019s arguably marginal celebrity-driven contribution to the cause. But in this case, perhaps, there is a strong inclination to suggest (and also, hope) that it will. Yet only time will tell if that hope sprang as fruitlessly as a pike on a hook.\n\nSo Pike\u2019s visit comes at exactly the right moment. It is a gentle reminder that the last lap matters. But there is arguably another reason why her visit is peculiarly timely \u2014 and that is, or could be, considerably less comfortable to sundry stakeholders in State sectors not amenable to the project.\n\nWhile MAG\u2019s de-miners are removing the physical remnants of the war from Sri Lankan soil, the ongoing excavation at Chemmani is doing something that is almost the opposite. It is dredging up the human remnants of the war and bagging it for inspection by the national consciousness.\n\nBy 9 August, at least 515 sets of human remains had been identified at Chemmani-Siththupathi, of which 506 were fully exhumed. Children and infants are among these grisly remains. Personal effects include a feeding bottle, bangles, footwear, schoolbag, toys and other objects. A recently discovered skeleton reportedly had its hands bound and its head separated from its body.\n\nThe numbers and the circumstantial evidence are appalling. But here truth requires restraint. The grim facts of Chemmani do not yet prove that 515 people were murdered by the Sri Lankan security forces. It does not establish that all the dead were Tamil civilians, although the excavation has revived the longstanding allegation that hundreds of Tamils who disappeared after the military recaptured Jaffna in 1995 were killed and buried there. It does not yet identify the dead. And it does not yet establish criminal responsibility."
  },
