    "md5": "c2ac0a353326ec5ef700c2788ecc9370",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1470,
    "chunk_text": "When the guns fell silent in 2009, our responsibilities did not diminish; they expanded. Today, our maritime responsibilities extend far beyond conventional defence to encompass IUU fishing, illegal migration, narcotics smuggling, human trafficking, maritime terrorism, environmental crime, humanitarian assistance, and regional cooperation.\n\nThe Indian Ocean has once again become a principal arena where commerce, influence, technology, and strategic competition converge. Nearly two-thirds of the world\u2019s seaborne petroleum passes through this ocean, while almost half of global container traffic traverses these waters. Regional naval investment is accelerating rapidly. Geography has blessed Sri Lanka, but geography guarantees nothing; strategic relevance belongs not merely to those well placed, but to those well prepared.\n\nOur future cannot be secured merely by becoming a larger Navy; arithmetic alone has never produced strategic advantage. We must become a more intelligent Navy: one that anticipates before reacting, innovates without abandoning tradition, and masters emerging technologies without surrendering professional judgement.\n\nAgility: The discipline to recognise change before change compels a response.\n\nCredibility: Earned through professionalism, patrol by patrol, exercise by exercise, and decision by decision.\n\nStrategic Relevance: History records navies that disappeared not because they were defeated, but because they ceased to matter."
  },
