    "md5": "3d1a578bd6a0a2c8d30f74337743c692",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1763,
    "chunk_text": "Cargills Bank 2Q profit slips despite stronger core income\n\nCargills Bank PLC saw its second-quarter profit edge lower despite a sharp improvement in core banking income, as higher operating costs, impairment charges and taxes absorbed gains from stronger lending and fee-based business.\n\nProfit for the three months ended June 30, 2026 fell 3 percent year-on-year to Rs. 75.8 million from Rs. 78.0 million, even as profit before tax rose 23 percent to Rs. 177.0 million from Rs. 144.2 million. Earnings per share declined to Rs. 0.06 from Rs. 0.09.\n\nTaxes on financial services jumped 54 percent to Rs. 161.4 million, while income tax expense rose 53 percent to Rs. 101.2 million.\n\nNet interest income rose 20 percent to Rs. 1.17 billion, while net fee and commission income surged 45 percent to Rs. 268.2 million. That helped lift total operating income 21 percent to Rs. 1.47 billion despite a 39 percent decline in other income.\n\nHowever, impairment charges increased 40 percent to Rs. 75.6 million, reversing part of the benefit from income growth. Total operating expenses climbed 15 percent to Rs. 1.06 billion, with personnel expenses up 18 percent and depreciation and amortisation rising 34 percent.\n\nThe quarter therefore points to improving earnings from Cargills Bank\u2019s core intermediation business, although cost growth, credit charges and taxation continued to constrain the conversion of that income growth into bottom-line profit.\n\nFor the first half, profit after tax fell 25 percent year-on-year to Rs. 180.7 million, while profit before tax declined 22 percent to Rs. 362.4 million. The weaker headline result, however, masks a substantial shift in the composition of earnings away from treasury-related gains and towards banking operations."
  },
