    "md5": "8c1b4c1a72f6bbc3f8819662fdf1e90e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1728,
    "chunk_text": "As disagreements rose among the EPRLF leadership, Kethesh left the EPRLF in the mid-1990s and sought a career first in journalism, and then as a political analyst on the conflict. It is during this time that he wrote an important book on the search for a political solution in Sri Lanka titled, \u2018Lost Opportunities\u2019. He was well positioned to write that book, because he was a participant during one of the early negotiations towards a political solution in 1985, facilitated by India between the Government of Sri Lanka and a number of Tamil militant groups and political parties in Thimpu. Even though the negotiations failed, Kethesh was central to formulating the Tamil nationalist position now famously known as the Thimpu Principles.\n\nA year later, by mid-2003, when the LTTE\u2019s assassination of Tamil dissidents accelerated, and when his former comrade in the EPRLF, Thambirajah Subathiran known as Robert was assassinated in Jaffna, Kethesh came to the position that for all purposes the peace process was dead. By then he had also become very critical of the role played by Norway, which was increasingly taking the position of appeasing the LTTE, and sought to ensure a deal between the Ranil Wickremesinghe Government and LTTE, even at the cost of human rights, democracy and inclusion of all communities.\n\nWhile the Colombo establishment, including most NGOs were aligned with the Norwegian approach, Kethesh was one of the few progressive voices in Colombo to challenge the internationalised peace process backed by the US, EU and Japan. This was a context in which Kethesh also had to worry about his safety, and indeed many of us feared for his life, as most Tamil critics had rarely been spared by the LTTE."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
