    "chunk_text": "CSE opens week mixed as large-cap strength offsets broad selling pressure\n\nThe Colombo Stock Exchange started the week on a mixed note on Monday, 17 August 2026, with the All Share Price Index edging lower while the S&P Sri Lanka 20 Index extended its positive momentum, highlighting continued strength among selected large-cap counters.\n\nThe ASPI declined marginally by 6.29 points, or 0.03%, to close at 21,616.88, while the S&P SL20 gained 18.82 points, or 0.31%, to finish at 6,084.54.\n\nThe ASPI traded firmly around the 21,670 level during early trading before selling pressure intensified towards midday, pulling the index close to 21,600. Buying interest emerged during the afternoon and supported a recovery towards 21,650, although renewed weakness towards the close pushed the index back into negative territory.\n\nTotal market turnover stood at LKR 1.89 billion, with approximately 45.51 million shares changing hands during the session.\n\nThe Food, Beverage and Tobacco sector dominated market activity, generating LKR 677.19 million in turnover from approximately 6.96 million shares. Melstacorp accounted for the majority of sector activity, recording LKR 599.10 million in turnover.\n\nCrossing transactions contributed approximately LKR 665.59 million, representing around 35% of total equity turnover. Melstacorp recorded the largest combined crossing activity, with four crossings valued at approximately LKR 565.84 million. This included a 2.00 million-share block executed at LKR 190.00, valued at LKR 380.00 million.\n\nForeign investors continued to remain net sellers, recording a net foreign outflow of LKR 473.53 million during the session.\n\nBroader market participation remained weak, with only 75 securities advancing against 132 decliners, resulting in an advance-to-decline ratio of 0.57. The negative breadth indicated relatively widespread selling pressure despite gains among selected heavyweight counters."
