    "chunk_text": "Surrogate advertising is a marketing method used to promote goods that may be prohibited. Companies often use the same brand name, logo or style on a legal proxy product \u2014 such as club soda, or cardamom in this case \u2014 to keep the main brand \u200bvisible to buyers.\n\nThe Maharashtra authorities asked the three actors to remove the advertisements from their social media accounts, not cooperate with \u200bany further broadcast and provide details of their endorsement agreements with the manufacturer.\n\nSurrogate advertisements have \u200bconcerned Prime Minister \u2060Narendra Modi's federal government in the past, with the current regulatory regime often being disputed in courts. The state in its notice to the actors said Indian law restricts advertisements that are misleading.\n\nIn one Vimal cardamom ad, the three actors appear in \u2060a festive \u200bcelebration throwing saffron on one another. It has 106 million views on \u200bYouTube.\n\nIn 2024, Reuters reported, India was drafting stricter rules to bar surrogate ads after complaints that liquor companies were promoting their brands using glasses with logos \u200bon or music CDs. The rules are yet to be finalised."
