    "md5": "152d44aa5d4867dc1e4ecb134ce1f9ff",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1983,
    "chunk_text": "Dry spell hits some parts of Sri Lanka, water level drops in major tanks\n\nColombo, Aug. 18 (Daily Mirror) - A dry spell in the Northern, Eastern and North Central provinces, as well as some areas of the Hambantota District, is reported, leading to water storage in more than 30 reservoirs and rivers falling to less than 50 per cent of their total capacity.\n\nFamilies in Mihintale, Polonnaruwa and the Hambantota District are seriously affected by the dry spell in the respective areas. Cattle in Hambantota are without water, reports said.\n\nWater storage in more than 30 minor reservoirs was less than 30 per cent of their capacity. Water storage in Egal Oya, Namal Oya, Pallan Oya, Pannalgama Oya and Rambakan Oya in the Ampara District was less than 50 per cent, according to data released by the Department of Irrigation.\n\nStorage in Hurulu Wewa, Mahakanadarawa and Nachchaduwa in the Anuradhapura District; Navakiri, Vakaneri and Unnichchai rivers in the Batticaloa District; Mau Ara, Muruthuhawela and Veheragala in the Hambantota District; Giritale, Kavudulla and Minneriya in Polonnaruwa; Tabbowa in the Puttalam District; Vendrasan in Trincomalee; and Paravakulam River, Giant Tank and Viyathukulam rivers in Mannar were also reported to be less than 50 per cent.\n\nDepartment of Irrigation Director H. M. P. S. D. Herath told the Daily Mirror that there is no issue with the major tanks, as water can be supplied for drinking purposes and cultivation until October this year. He therefore said there could be an uncertain period after October. \u201cThere are some drinking water shortages in rural villages which do not depend on major tanks and reservoirs,\u201d he said.\n\nNational Water Resources and Drainage Board Chairman Chandana Bandara told the media that the water situation is somewhat stable at the moment, but there could be a crisis by February next year. \u201cWe have begun planning to face the crisis, and a programme will be introduced to face a crisis,\u201d he said."
