    "md5": "2f6be0f9d392775c6d073fe4bd4e724d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1857,
    "chunk_text": "The campaign was bitterly contested. Following the October presidential election, Jayewardene had imposed a state of emergency, claiming that sections of the SLFP were plotting against him. The emergency remained in force during the referendum.\n\nThe referendum recorded a 71% turnout among 8,145,015 registered voters. More than 54% voted in favour, and 120 of the 168 electorates recorded majorities supporting the extension. Most voters in Tamil-majority areas, however, voted against it. But what happened at one polling booth in Attidiya tells a very different story from the official figures.\n\nA day or two before the referendum, I was travelling home from my office in Colpetty with a friend who was a close supporter of the late Lalith Athulathmudali, then Minister of Ports and Shipping. He often gave me a lift home. On this occasion, he suggested that we stop at an organisers\u2019 meeting Athulathmudali was holding in Ratmalana.\n\nMy friend introduced me to the minister as a staunch UNP supporter, a flattering but completely untrue description. Athulathmudali was giving his supporters last-minute instructions about manning polling booths. Someone complained that there were not enough people to cover several booths in the Attidiya area. The minister turned to my friend and asked whether he could help.\n\nAt around 8.00 a.m., the turnout was very low, and there was little for us to do. We were sitting around when a few men wearing green caps arrived and asked the presiding officer about the percentage of votes cast. They soon left. Shortly afterwards, a gang leader arrived with a group of men and ordered the opposition polling agents to leave the booth. They then demanded the remaining ballot papers from the presiding officer. The papers were distributed among the four of us, with instructions to mark them in favour of the \u201cLamp\u201d."
  },
