    "md5": "2e19dce1e8d99ea520ab2dcf293f0024",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1993,
    "chunk_text": "At around 8.00 a.m., the turnout was very low, and there was little for us to do. We were sitting around when a few men wearing green caps arrived and asked the presiding officer about the percentage of votes cast. They soon left. Shortly afterwards, a gang leader arrived with a group of men and ordered the opposition polling agents to leave the booth. They then demanded the remaining ballot papers from the presiding officer. The papers were distributed among the four of us, with instructions to mark them in favour of the \u201cLamp\u201d.\n\nThe election report published by the then relatively powerless Elections Commissioner Chandrananda de Silva, documented serious electoral irregularities. It recorded widespread voter intimidation, ballot-box stuffing, unauthorised replacement of ballot boxes, and threats against polling officials. My experience cannot, of course, represent every polling station in the country. But it gave me a personal understanding of why the conduct of the referendum became such a controversial issue.\n\nFor me, the enduring lesson is more personal. A referendum may be presented as the purest expression of the people\u2019s will, but that will can only be meaningful when voters are free to cast their ballots without fear, intimidation or manipulation. The events I witnessed in Attidiya 44 years ago remain a reminder that the integrity of an election is measured not only by the final result, but by what happens inside the polling booth.\n\nIn 1982, democracy was threatened by a referendum. Forty-four years later, the danger may lie at the opposite extreme. The Government is reportedly planning to extend the term of sitting judges by two years through its supermajority, thereby avoiding a referendum at a time when the people\u2019s direct mandate may be constitutionally required. Different circumstances, different methods, but the lesson remains the same. Democracy is weakened whenever the people\u2019s right to decide is set aside for political convenience."
  },
