    "date_str": "2026-08-18",
    "description": "\u201cCyclone Ditwah exposed critical structural gaps in  Sri Lanka\u2019s early warning  architecture\u201d - Prof. Amaratunga -",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/hard-talk/Cyclone-Ditwah-exposed-critical-structural-gaps-in-Sri-Lankas-early-warning-architecture-Prof-Amaratunga/334-348330",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787033553.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-18-dailymirrorlk-39bdcbe4-0009",
    "chunk_index": 9,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "767b5539a96d1e370d7d91bd88674b2d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1875,
    "chunk_text": "Near-site relocation within local administrative boundaries to preserve access to ancestral farming or trading grounds.\n\nDual-zone land access allowing farmers to cultivate original non-hazardous plots while living in safer cluster developments.\n\nLocal Empowerment & Capacity Building - Disaster management cannot remain top-heavy. Empowering Grama Niladhari-level Disaster Management Committees, local governments, and civil society ensures that community-level first responders have the training, localised risk maps, and resources to act immediately when central links break down.\n\nSri Lanka is moving in the right direction structurally and institutionally, but progress remains hampered by implementation bottlenecks and fiscal constraints:\n\nLegal & Policy Frameworks: Sri Lanka established early legislative anchors (Disaster Management Act No. 13 of 2005) and updated policies like the NDMP 2023\u20132030, aligning with the international Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction. Technical bodies like the National Building Research Organisation (NBRO) produce high-precision landslide hazard mapping.\n\nExecution & Enforcement (Lags Behind): Despite sound policies, enforceability remains a key hurdle. Unplanned urbanisation, informal hill-slope settlements, and economic pressures frequently lead local authorities to bypass hazard maps.\n\nSocio-Economic Integration: Relocation projects still frequently prioritise physical housing over socio-economic continuity, leading to community pushback and high vacancy rates in resettlement sites.\n\nSri Lanka has laid a solid policy and analytical foundation. However, transforming into a fully disaster-resilient nation requires closing the gap between national plans and local enforcement, securing sustainable climate financing, and treating socio-economic security as a mandatory component of disaster risk management."
  },
