    "chunk_text": "Bopage backs NPP but warns of gaps in governance and priorities\n\nFormer JVP General Secretary Dr. Lionel Bopage has backed the Government\u2019s anti-corruption drive but cautioned against turning arrests and investigations into political spectacles, while calling for stronger institutions, administrative reform, and better coordination within the Government.\n\n\u201cI don\u2019t agree that the Government is taking political revenge by arresting people,\u201d Bopage said, stressing that those responsible for corruption should be brought to justice through proper legal procedures.\n\nHowever, he warned that public ridicule of suspects and premature announcements about cases could undermine confidence in the process.\n\nIn an interview with the Daily FT, Dr. Bopage recalled the JVP\u2019s past with its founder Rohana Wijeweera, mistakes made by Wijeweera that contributed to the then President J.R. Jayewardene\u2019s decision to proscribe the party, the motives and limitations of the 2022 Aragalaya, the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) Government\u2019s shortcomings in bringing about change, and the importance of maintaining a balanced approach in relations with global powers.\n\nHe said the Government\u2019s anti-corruption drive would have limited impact without reforming a bureaucracy that, under successive administrations, had discouraged initiative and independent decision-making.\n\nQ: Have you received any invitations from the present Government to contribute your expertise, perhaps through an advisory board or discussions with the Government?\n\nA: No. There are former senior JVP members, such as Prof. Jayadeva Uyangoda and Prof. Athula Sumathipala, as well as many professional experts, particularly in the expatriate community, who are willing to contribute to building a better and more prosperous Sri Lanka.\n\nI met President Anura Kumara Dissanayake twice before the 2024 Presidential Election. We discussed general policy issues, but there was no invitation to contribute to the party or Government."
