    "chunk_text": "A: I am not aware of the internal politics of the NPP-JVP coalition, so I cannot comment. But I recall the negative experience of internal disputes within the JVP in 1971, which affected the insurrection. By 1983, factions within the JVP had again weakened the party and affected its activities.\n\nA: Yes. I supported it from Australia because Sri Lanka was in a disastrous crisis. The system was deeply corrupt and people wanted change.\n\nHowever, the Aragalaya was not a unified movement. It included people associated with the UNP, JVP, Frontline Socialist Party and many other groups, without a common agenda.\n\nNevertheless, it represented an important awakening among young people. We organised public meetings in Australia and supported the movement through advocacy and provided material and financial assistance when requested.\n\nQ: Was its main objective to change the Government or the system? Are you satisfied with the outcome?\n\nA: The objective was to change the system to some extent, but there was no clear common agenda for achieving that objective. Different groups tried to develop one but could not agree.\n\nThe movement developed largely through social media rather than through a conventional organisation. It could have gone further if the groups had united, reached compromises and developed a common agenda. The movement was eventually suppressed by President Ranil Wickremesinghe.\n\nQ: Do you mean former President Wickremesinghe was also part of the Aragalaya?\n\nA: One of the groups associated with the movement was the Black Hats. They were linked to a faction associated with President Wickremesinghe and played a prominent role in the Aragalaya.\n\nQ: You described the previous regime as autocratic and welcomed the change. Did you welcome the change that followed the Aragalaya?\n\nA: No. I did not support Wickremesinghe becoming President because it was not a decision made directly by the people who participated in the Aragalaya or by the general population."
