    "chunk_size_bytes": 1886,
    "chunk_text": "Q: You described the previous regime as autocratic and welcomed the change. Did you welcome the change that followed the Aragalaya?\n\nA: No. I did not support Wickremesinghe becoming President because it was not a decision made directly by the people who participated in the Aragalaya or by the general population.\n\nQ: But did President Wickremesinghe not help improve the economy and the situation at the time?\n\nA: That is debatable. Both the previous and present Governments adopted the IMF program, but their approaches have differed.\n\nUnder Wickremesinghe, the program involved significant restrictions on the public and relatively few concessions. The present Government also has to comply with IMF conditions because of legislation and agreements inherited from the previous Government, but it has provided greater concessions to the people.\n\nCurrent difficulties are also influenced by international developments, including geopolitical conflicts and tensions involving major powers. As a result, some election promises have become difficult to implement.\n\nAt the same time, I am critical of the Government because I do not think it has always got its priorities right.\n\nQ: Aren\u2019t you satisfied with what the NPP Government is doing to tackle corruption and improve the economy?\n\nA: I think it could have done better. Sri Lanka needs to reduce its excessive dependence on exports as the primary source of foreign exchange. Exports and services are necessary, but they should be built on a stronger domestic economic foundation.\n\nWe need to address basic requirements such as food, energy, water and telecommunications, and then build a stronger export economy.\n\nSince 1977, we have become heavily dependent on exports, tourism and migrant remittances. Tea, rubber, coconut, apparel and footwear are important, but these sectors are vulnerable when global conditions deteriorate."
