    "chunk_text": "A: In general, people are happy with his performance, although I cannot comment fully because I do not know what is happening inside the Government. From the outside, however, I think its communication and coordination could improve.\n\nAt times, important leaders make different statements on the same issue. There should be greater consensus before positions are announced publicly.\n\nQ: How do you assess the Government\u2019s international relations with the United States, China and India?\n\nA: Sri Lanka is in a very sensitive position. A significant amount of foreign exchange comes from the United States and Western markets, while China and India are also strategically and economically important.\n\nThe Government therefore has to maintain a delicate balance. We should protect Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereignty and economic interests without putting economic stability at risk.\n\nMaintaining good relations with the US, India and China is essential. If Sri Lanka antagonises any of these major powers, the consequences could be serious.\n\nQ: Do you see any lapses in the Government\u2019s relationship with the United States?\n\nA: No. I think the Government is trying to maintain good relations with the US because it understands the risks of antagonising a major economic power.\n\nThe same applies to India and China. Sri Lanka needs all three relationships while it rebuilds the economy.\n\nQ: Do you see a difference between the ideology of the original JVP and the present JVP?\n\nA: Obviously. The original JVP was based on socialism, whereas the present Government is not socialist. It is a coalition pursuing a social democratic agenda.\n\nThe Government is trying to recover the economy and improve living conditions within a capitalist framework. That is fundamentally different from running a socialist system.\n\nQ: The JVP originally promoted equality and non-discrimination. Do you think it has taken a different approach now and overlooked communal issues?"
