    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5ba1557f19c2d470a86f70ac5a0484e3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1142,
    "chunk_text": "The GSP+ provides preferential access to the EU market and is particularly important to Sri Lanka\u2019s apparel sector. Eligibility is conditional on the effective implementation of 27 international conventions covering human rights, labour rights, environmental protection and good governance.\n\nApparel industry stakeholders have warned that failure to replace the PTA could become a significant obstacle to retaining the concessions.\n\nSri Lanka lost GSP+ concessions in August 2010 amid concerns over its compliance with international human rights conventions. Following a renewed application in July 2016 and commitments to undertake reforms, the concessions were restored on 19 May 2017.\n\nThe most recent formal GSP+ monitoring cycle was conducted in 2025, following an EU delegation\u2019s visit to Sri Lanka in April that year. EU officials subsequently indicated that the review was progressing positively, while stressing that continued eligibility would depend on progress on outstanding human rights and governance commitments.\n\nThe proposal to this effect was submitted by Justice and National Integration Minister Harshana Nanayakkara."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
