    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6e30a2bc3dfb374255d8f29198a27290",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1980,
    "chunk_text": "GMOA calls for immediate revision of health sector cadre\n\nColombo, Aug. 19 (Daily Mirror) - The Government Medical Officers\u2019 Association (GMOA) has called for an immediate revision of the approved cadre of doctors and other health workers, warning that delays could threaten the future stability of Sri Lanka\u2019s free health service.\n\nThe GMOA said the health workforce is critical to ensuring continuous, equitable and internationally standardised patient care, and stressed the need to deploy adequate staff in all health institutions according to the services provided.\n\nAccording to the association, a scientific and analytical revision of the approved health workforce covering the entire country has not been carried out for a long period. The last revision of the approved medical cadre was conducted in 2015, while subsequent amendments have been limited to selected hospitals.\n\nThe GMOA said the health service has expanded significantly over the past decade due to population growth, the establishment of new hospitals and hospital units, the introduction of new specialist fields and advanced treatment services, changes to the health system, evolving disease patterns, epidemics and natural disasters.\n\nHowever, the approved cadre has not increased in line with these developments, resulting in health workers having to manage an unsustainable workload, it said.\n\nThe association also noted that Sri Lanka has lost more than 2,500 specialists and doctors since 2022 due to migration, while the number of approved doctors remains based on the 2015 cadre.\n\n\u201cHowever, that number is by no means sufficient to meet the current service needs of the Sri Lankan health system,\u201d the GMOA said.\n\nAlthough the GMOA has repeatedly highlighted the need to revise the approved medical cadre, the process has yet to be completed. The association said the failure to identify the actual workforce requirements has also hindered the recruitment of doctors needed for the future."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
