    "chunk_text": "Passenger drones could cost US$20\u201330 per trip from BIA to Colombo\n\nColombo, Aug. 19 (Daily Mirror) - Tourists arriving at Bandaranaike International Airport could potentially travel to hotels in Colombo by passenger drone for an estimated fare of US$20\u201330 per trip under a proposed service being explored by Sri Lanka.\n\nThe proposal was discussed at a special meeting held at the Ministry of Ports and Civil Aviation under the patronage of Minister Anura Karunathilaka, with the participation of Chinese company EHang, which has offered to provide passenger drone services.\n\nEHang Chief Financial Officer Conor Yang and General Manager of Operations Xiao Na Lee led the company\u2019s delegation. Deputy Minister Janitha Ruwan Kodithuwakku, Ministry Secretary W.W.S. Mangala, Civil Aviation Authority Director General Damith Rambukwella and senior officials also attended.\n\nThe proposed service is being considered primarily as a new mode of transport for tourists, while also exploring its potential use in emergency and disaster response and maritime navigation.\n\nDuring the meeting, the Minister highlighted that passenger safety would be a key priority and that the service could not commence commercial operations without the necessary approvals from Sri Lankan aviation authorities.\n\nThese approvals would have to cover aviation safety, the regulatory framework, airspace control, emergency operations, cybersecurity, data security and the establishment of appropriate take-off and landing sites.\n\nThe implementation of the project would also require coordination with several institutions, including the Ministry of Defence, Sri Lanka Air Force, Telecommunications Regulatory Commission, Board of Investment, Colombo Port City Economic Commission, Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority and airport and airline authorities.\n\nOfficials said that telecommunications approvals would be particularly important because the aircraft depend on remote-control and communication technologies."
