    "md5": "6e72439baf3f01d0ae407933cd6dbd4e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1823,
    "chunk_text": "JAAF and ILO lead apparel sector\u2019s push for disability inclusion with voluntary pledge\n\nThirteen apparel companies recently signed a voluntary pledge in Colombo committing to advance disability inclusion in the workplace, marking a milestone for Inclusive Threads of Better Work Sri Lanka, a joint initiative of the International Labour Organization (ILO), the International Finance Corporation (IFC), and, supported by the Australian Government\u2019s Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade.\n\nThrough the pledge, signatory companies commit to eight core principles, including promoting the rights of persons with disabilities, preventing discrimination, ensuring reasonable workplace accommodations, and collaborating with government institutions and industry partners to advance inclusive employment.\n\nThe ceremony brought together representatives from the Ministry of Rural Development, Social Security and Community Empowerment, the Employers\u2019 Federation of Ceylon (EFC), the Joint Apparel Association Forum (JAAF), and companies across the apparel sector, alongside government officials and development partners.\n\nSpeaking at the event, ILO Country Director for Sri Lanka and the Maldives, Joni Simpson, said the pledge reflects a shared commitment to progress rather than a claim that every challenge has already been solved.\n\n\u201cBy signing this pledge, employers are making a public commitment to strengthen disability inclusive employment within their companies. This is a commitment to keep moving forward, to review recruitment practices, remove barriers, engage with workers, and create more opportunities for persons with disabilities,\u201d Simpson said. \u201cReal and lasting progress is only possible through partnership between government, employers, workers, and organizations of persons with disabilities.\u201d"
  },
