    "md5": "7759a8b228f99f883c56c6dcec40d3d2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1997,
    "chunk_text": "She said the attorneys general offer \u201cno proof anyone in their states was misled\u201d and are attempting to \u201cpenalise Meta\u201d for industry-wide challenges like age verification. \u201cRather than sticking to the facts or the law, the states have instead decided to chase an outlandish payout,\u201d she added.\n\nMeta sought to block his testimony, allegedly for deleting Signal messages with former employees, but Rogers rejected what she considered a long-shot bid to eliminate a key witness.\n\nBejar told jurors that \u201cmove fast and break things\u201d was a mantra at Meta, which took a \u201cdon\u2019t ask, don\u2019t tell\u201d approach to monitoring whether children under 13 were online.\n\n\u201cMany products were shipped into the world\u201d, such as Reels short-form videos, \u201cand safety was not a consideration in how it was initially deployed\u201d, Bejar said.\n\nZuckerberg and Instagram chief Adam Mosseri are also expected \u2060to testify.\n\nThe case\u2019s impetus came from a US Senate committee hearing in 2021, when whistleblower Frances Haugen, a former data scientist at Facebook, claimed the company knowingly pushed products that could impact the health of young users as it pursued higher profits.\n\nMeta has repeatedly tried to end the coalition lawsuit, including in 2024, and as recently as June, when it sought summary judgement \u2013 a decision that a court might make without going to trial \u2013 which would have ended the lawsuit.\n\nAl Jazeera\u2019s Eva McKend, reporting from Washington, DC, said the states have a \u201csubstantial case\u201d, but that \u201cproving intent\u201d will be their biggest challenge.\n\nBut, she said, \u201cit speaks volumes that they were even able to get this far\u201d. \u201cNo doubt, Meta, with all the corporate might that they have, have tried for a long time to get this squashed and not let this reach before a judge,\u201d she added. \u201cSo [the company has] to be quite worried at this point.\u201d\n\nOn Wall Street, Meta shares traded lower most of Tuesday and closed down 4.4 percent at $543.67."
