    "chunk_text": "But, she said, \u201cit speaks volumes that they were even able to get this far\u201d. \u201cNo doubt, Meta, with all the corporate might that they have, have tried for a long time to get this squashed and not let this reach before a judge,\u201d she added. \u201cSo [the company has] to be quite worried at this point.\u201d\n\nOn Wall Street, Meta shares traded lower most of Tuesday and closed down 4.4 percent at $543.67.\n\nAs the trial began, child safety advocates and parents who trace their children\u2019s deaths to social media harms gathered outside of the court on Tuesday. Many parents held photos of their late children as they spoke to reporters outside, at times through tears.\n\nSeveral parents held a banner with the names and ages of children who died from social media-related harms. It was several feet long.\n\nAmong the people outside the court was Mary Rodee, who said her 15-year-old son Riley Basford died by suicide in 2021 after being victimised by a predator on Facebook.\n\n\u201cThey call it spontaneous suicide,\u201d she said. \u201cI call it what it was: the predictable outcome of a system that protects corporations instead of children.\u201d"
  },
