    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0d9f1a9ed0b975fc20f1c7b74cbfbeb0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1949,
    "chunk_text": "Services lead Sri Lanka\u2019s private sector expansion in July\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s services sector expanded at a faster pace in July, supported by growth in transportation, financial and professional services, while manufacturing activity also strengthened during the month, according to the Central Bank.\n\nThe Services Purchasing Managers\u2019 Index (PMI) rose to 61.4 in July from 56.5 in June, signalling a sharp acceleration in business activity.\n\nThe expansion was broad-based, led by transportation, financial and professional services, while the other personal services segment also recorded notable growth.\n\nNew business continued to increase, driven mainly by transportation and professional services, with financial and other personal services also contributing.\n\nEmployment rose during the month, supported by fresh recruitment and a slowdown in staff resignations. Backlogs of work increased, with the relevant sub-index rising to 51.2 from a neutral 50.0 in June.\n\nBusinesses became more upbeat about the near-term outlook, with the expectations for activity sub-index climbing to 75.0 from 72.7. The Central Bank attributed the stronger sentiment to anticipated economic improvements and expectations of higher tourist arrivals linked to the Kandy Esala Perahera.\n\nThe Manufacturing PMI, meanwhile, increased to 55.0 in July from 53.0 in June, indicating a continued and faster expansion in factory activity. A reading above 50 signals expansion, while one below 50 indicates contraction.\n\nGrowth in new orders and production was driven primarily by the food and beverage manufacturing sector. The new orders sub-index rose to 55.9 from 50.0, while production increased to 54.5 from 52.0.\n\nEmployment edged up to 51.0 from 50.5, reflecting manufacturers\u2019 positive expectations. However, the stock of purchases sub-index slowed to 51.5 from 56.6.\n\nSuppliers\u2019 delivery times remained lengthened, pointing to continuing supply-chain pressures."
  },
