    "chunk_size_bytes": 1712,
    "chunk_text": "Business leaders clash over role of foreign capital and democracy in Sri Lanka\u2019s economic recovery\n\nThe role of international capital and the efficiency of the democratic system in driving Sri Lanka\u2019s economic recovery became a point of intense debate among leading corporate figures and economists, highlighting the growing tension between the need for rapid policy execution and the preservation of institutional resilience.\n\nDuring a panel discussion hosted by the Sri Lanka-Korea Business Council last week in Colombo, titled \u201cSri Lanka\u2019s Future \u2013 Forecast, Scenarios and Challenges,\u201d a sharp contrast in economic philosophies emerged. Aroshi Nanayakkara, a global consulting company CEO and immediate past chairperson of the Sri Lanka Institute of Directors, challenged the conventional economic approach, questioning whether a democratic system with its frequent electoral cycles and shifting mandates is conducive to maintaining the long-term, single-minded vision necessary for economic transformation.\n\nDrawing on her extensive background across strategy, human resources, and risk management, alongside her experience serving on the boards of major financial institutions, Nanayakkara pointed to the prolonged policy consistency seen in other Asian economies with centralized planning.\n\n\u201cI\u2019m going to challenge saying is democracy the right way to go? Every five years when you have a new president coming in and a whole new parliament coming in, how are you going to maintain that vision for a country?\u201d Nanayakkara asked.\n\nShe further challenged the widely held belief that the nation is starved of capital, arguing that the domestic financial system already holds sufficient funds."
