    "chunk_size_bytes": 1891,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cI\u2019m going to challenge saying is democracy the right way to go? Every five years when you have a new president coming in and a whole new parliament coming in, how are you going to maintain that vision for a country?\u201d Nanayakkara asked.\n\nShe further challenged the widely held belief that the nation is starved of capital, arguing that the domestic financial system already holds sufficient funds.\n\nHowever, this perspective drew a strong and impassioned rebuttal from CAL Holdings Group CEO Kanishka Mannakkara, who vigorously defended the necessity of both foreign capital and the democratic process. Mannakkara warned that isolating the economy from global capital flows would be a severe miscalculation, particularly as the world undergoes a massive technological and industrial shift.\n\n\u201cFirst, a few people mentioned that we don\u2019t need foreign capital. I disagree with that for a couple of reasons,\u201d Mannakkara stated. \u201cWe are entering a new economy, it\u2019s really akin to the Industrial Revolution where the growth is very capital intensive, and we are seeing one of the largest phases of investment globally right now. To then imagine that Sri Lanka can somehow operate in an inter-economy ecosystem outside this global norm is crazy. So we do need that.\u201d\n\nHe further elaborated that the benefits of international capital extend far beyond mere financial liquidity, acting as a critical enforcement mechanism for institutional discipline.\n\n\u201cThat\u2019s only part of the story about international capital. You see, international capital also forces governance,\u201d he explained. \u201cWith that capital comes knowledge anyway, and also remember international capital will help reduce interest rates domestically because of the liquidity that it brings. So for all of these reasons, I think it is important for us to think about what makes international capital conducive.\u201d"
