    "description": "Why Sri Lanka should decriminalise homosexuality \u2014 irrespective of GSP+",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Why-Sri-Lanka-should-decriminalise-homosexuality-irrespective-of-GSP/172-348436",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787125433.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-19-dailymirrorlk-60ce1d6d-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "953e92e7cf85acbad0a9e4ad820292be",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1762,
    "chunk_text": "The European Union has outlined a set of recommendations for Sri Lanka to comply with in order to continue its preferential access to the EU market under the GSP+ trade programme post-2028. Among the main recommendations are repealing the Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA) in place of a law in line with international standards, amending or repealing the Online Safety Act, a formal moratorium on the death penalty, and enacting anti-discrimination laws and decriminalisation of consensual same sex relations.\n\nUnless the JVP/NPP government replicates the same misfortune, Sri Lanka would not be eligible for GSP+ after 2028. That would make Colombo\u2019s application for continued participation in the GSP a dead rubber.\u00a0 That, however, should not be a reason to sit on the human rights reforms.\n\nThe government itself has promised in its election manifesto to repeal the PTA in favour of an anti-terrorism law in line with international standards, and amendments to the Online Safety Act. However, its procrastination on these matters, in contrast to its sense of urgency towards the 22nd amendment to the Constitution, may cast doubt on its sincerity.\n\nThe same applies to the decriminalisation of consensual same sex relations.\n\nSections 365 and 365A of the colonial-era Penal Code still criminalises homosexuality. Penalties include up to 10 years in prison, though no one has been prosecuted yet under the said provisions. Yet, the discriminatory law fosters social stigma and creates a permissive context for abuse. Criminalisation also stands in the way of any meaningful measures for equal opportunities for LGBTQ communities in employment and social life, effectively entrenching discrimination against citizens based on sexual identity and orientation."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
