    "chunk_text": "Digital signature, paperless customs system to come into operation from October 1\n\nColombo, Aug. 19 (Daily Mirror) - The Head of the Revenue Administration Reform and Modernization Bureau and Senior Additional Secretary to the President, Seevali Arukgoda, commended the contribution of the Information Technology Division of Sri Lanka Customs towards the successful implementation of the Digital Signature and Paperless Customs Declaration System, which is scheduled to come into operation from October 1.\n\nHe made these remarks while delivering a special lecture on the Government\u2019s digitalisation policy and the role of the Revenue Administration Reform and Modernization Bureau at an awareness programme organised by the Ceylon Chamber of Commerce yesterday (18).\n\nThe programme, held at the Ceylon Chamber of Commerce auditorium, was aimed at raising awareness among importers and the business community on the new digital signature and paperless Customs declaration system.\n\nThe programme was organised in line with the Government\u2019s initiative to expedite paperless document processing at Customs, with the benefits of the system to the trade sector discussed extensively.\n\nOfficials from LankaPay and Sri Lanka Customs conducted a technical session, providing participants with comprehensive information on digital signatures, the procedure for obtaining a digital signature and its practical use when submitting Customs declarations.\n\nCustoms Directors Ruwan Thisera and Sisira Kumara, Directors of the Revenue Administration Reform and Modernization Bureau W. L. C. Thilakasiri and M. A. Premalal, Ceylon Chamber of Commerce Chief Executive Officer and Secretary-General Shiran Fernando, senior officials of Sri Lanka Customs and its Information Technology Division, representatives of LankaPay and a large number of representatives from the importing and business communities attended the programme."
