    "description": "NMSJ warns 22A could place SC in conflict of interest",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/NMSJ-warns-22A-could-place-SC-in-conflict-of-interest/108-348458",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787136466.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-19-dailymirrorlk-285b1669-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e0003819b7809d4373cefea25c52c7ae",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1754,
    "chunk_text": "NMSJ warns 22A could place SC in conflict of interest\n\nColombo, Aug. 19 (Daily Mirror) - The National Movement for Social Justice (NMSJ) has urged lawmakers to reconsider the proposed extension of the terms of Supreme Court and Court of Appeal judges, warning that the proposed 22nd Amendment to the Constitution could place the Supreme Court in a position where it may have to hear a case concerning the tenure of its own judges.\n\nIn a statement, NMSJ General Secretary Sunil Jayasekara said the proposed amendment should be considered through a careful, multi-party discussion involving all relevant stakeholders, with particular attention given to protecting the independence and integrity of the Judiciary.\n\nThe organisation said several parties have already indicated that they intend to seek judicial review of the constitutionality of the Bill, which was gazetted and presented to Parliament on August 18.\n\nAccording to the NMSJ, this could create a situation in which Supreme Court judges are required to determine the constitutionality of a constitutional amendment that directly affects the extension of their own terms of office.\n\nIt said this raises the fundamental legal principle that no person should be a judge in his or her own case, known as 'nemo judex in causa sua'.\n\nThe NMSJ noted that Article 105(7) of the Constitution contains the only provision referring to the retirement age of judges of the Supreme Court and Court of Appeal, describing it as an important safeguard for judicial independence.\n\nThe organisation stressed that the Judiciary plays a vital role in determining the legality and constitutionality of actions taken by the Executive and Legislature, making the protection of judicial tenure and privileges essential."
  },
