    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ed03acfa5a707a84878119bdb804d61e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1824,
    "chunk_text": "The organisation stressed that the Judiciary plays a vital role in determining the legality and constitutionality of actions taken by the Executive and Legislature, making the protection of judicial tenure and privileges essential.\n\nThe NMSJ also pointed to approaches adopted in other countries to deal with similar situations. It said Canada allows provincial judges to sit on the Supreme Court for special cases, while England has used recently retired judges as an additional panel in certain cases. It also noted that countries including Austria, Germany, South Korea, South Africa, Indonesia, Thailand and T\u00fcrkiye have separate constitutional courts to examine the constitutionality of legislation.\n\nSri Lanka, however, has neither a separate constitutional court nor a panel of retired judges that could be appointed to hear such cases, the organisation said.\n\nThe NMSJ said lawmakers should therefore find a solution within the existing constitutional framework to ensure that the Supreme Court is not placed in a position where it has to consider a matter directly affecting the tenure of its own judges.\n\nIt further said that if the Supreme Court determines, following a review of the proposed amendment, that a referendum is required, the final decision would rest with the people, thereby avoiding a conflict involving judges deciding on their own tenure.\n\nThe organisation stressed that justice must not only be fair but must also be seen to be fair, warning that extending the terms of sitting judges without a direct decision by the people through a referendum could lead to questions about the perceived impartiality of the Judiciary.\n\nIt said such circumstances could increase pressure on the Judiciary to demonstrate its independence and impartiality and potentially weaken public confidence in the Court."
  },
