    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0619b4b27164a543cef7a442f2a04a27",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1873,
    "chunk_text": "SL envoy seeks stronger India partnership while safeguarding sovereignty\n\nColombo, Aug. 19 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka\u2019s High Commissioner to India Mahishini Colonne has stressed the need to protect Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereignty, territorial integrity and strategic autonomy while further strengthening its strategic partnership with India.\n\nAddressing the 66th Course of India\u2019s National Defence College (NDC) in New Delhi on Tuesday (18), Colonne described the relationship as \u201ca partnership between unequal states, but not between unequal interests,\u201d while emphasising that both countries share fundamental interests in peace, stability, prosperity and security in the Indian Ocean.\n\nSpeaking on \u201cIndia\u2013Sri Lanka Bilateral Relations: Determinants, Status, Opportunities, Challenges and the Way Forward,\u201d she said the two countries were deeply interdependent and noted Sri Lanka\u2019s consistent assurance that its territory would not be used in a manner that threatens India\u2019s security interests.\n\nAt the same time, she underscored the importance of safeguarding Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereignty and strategic autonomy.\n\nColonne highlighted the recent momentum in bilateral relations, including President Anura Kumara Disanayaka\u2019s State Visit to India in December 2024, Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi\u2019s visit to Sri Lanka in April 2025 and the first-ever bilateral visit to Sri Lanka by an Indian Vice President in April 2026.\n\nShe also recalled India\u2019s swift humanitarian assistance to Sri Lanka following Cyclone Ditwah in November 2025 under Operation Sagar Bandhu.\n\nThe High Commissioner said India\u2019s support during Sri Lanka\u2019s 2022 economic crisis, including critical emergency assistance and its role as the first bilateral creditor to provide financing assurances for Sri Lanka\u2019s IMF programme, had also contributed to wider regional stability."
  },
