    "md5": "591690734acd5e3fe1428a7c1b5b82ca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1973,
    "chunk_text": "BASL president addresses high court of Bhutan during historic visit\n\nColombo, Aug. 19 (Daily Mirror) - Bar Association of Sri Lanka (BASL) President Rajeev Amarasuriya addressed the High Court of Bhutan during the first-ever visit by a BASL delegation to the Kingdom of Bhutan, as the two countries\u2019 legal professions sought to strengthen cooperation and exchange experiences in the administration of justice.\n\nAmarasuriya addressed the Superior Bench of the High Court, comprising eight Justices and presided over by Chief Justice Justice Duba Drukpa.\n\nWelcoming the BASL delegation, the Chief Justice said the Court was honoured by their presence and described the visit as an opportunity to strengthen ties between the legal communities of Bhutan and Sri Lanka.\n\nHe stressed that the Bench and the Bar are two branches of the same profession, united by the common pursuit of justice, and emphasised the importance of mutual respect, professional integrity and a shared commitment to the Rule of Law.\n\nAddressing the Court, Amarasuriya expressed appreciation for the warmth and hospitality extended to the BASL delegation by Bhutan\u2019s judiciary and legal profession.\n\nHe said the visit marked an important new chapter in relations between the two legal professions, noting that while Bhutan and Sri Lanka had followed different historical and constitutional paths, they remained connected as South Asian nations, members of the SAARC community and countries of the Global South with a shared spiritual and cultural heritage.\n\nReferring to Bhutan\u2019s constitutional development and its philosophy of Gross National Happiness, Amarasuriya said the country had demonstrated that development must ultimately focus on people.\n\nHe said the same principle should guide the administration of justice, noting that courts and lawyers have a broader responsibility to protect human dignity, resolve disputes peacefully, restrain arbitrary exercises of power and safeguard rights."
