    "md5": "7932140ba5f4268c1f9df922001c8a84",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1647,
    "chunk_text": "He said the same principle should guide the administration of justice, noting that courts and lawyers have a broader responsibility to protect human dignity, resolve disputes peacefully, restrain arbitrary exercises of power and safeguard rights.\n\nHe emphasised that judicial independence and the independence of the legal profession are not privileges belonging to judges or lawyers, but safeguards that ultimately belong to the people.\n\nAmarasuriya identified legal education, continuing professional development, opportunities for young lawyers, constitutional and public law, technology, court administration and institutional exchanges as areas where the two countries could cooperate and learn from each other.\n\nHe said that although the Himalayas and the Indian Ocean geographically separate Bhutan and Sri Lanka, the values that sustain justice recognise no such boundaries.\n\nThe engagement at the High Court followed the first bilateral meeting between the BASL and the Bar Council of Bhutan, where the two institutions affirmed their commitment to strengthening cooperation between their respective legal professions.\n\nThe commitment was reflected in a Joint Bar Statement focusing on justice, the Rule of Law, professional integrity, the administration of justice, and the independence of the judiciary and legal profession.\n\nDuring the visit, the BASL delegation also met Chief Justice of the Supreme Court of Bhutan Lyonpo Norbu Tshering, Attorney General Sonam Tashi and other dignitaries.\n\nThe visit marks a significant step in strengthening professional and institutional ties between the legal communities of Sri Lanka and Bhutan."
