    "chunk_size_bytes": 1126,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka once understood a version of this and never actually lost the legal authority to practise it again. Section 76 has sat largely unused through an IMF program rightly focused on stabilisation, exchange rate management, and fiscal consolidation. Those were urgent priorities, but they are not transformation. An economy that has stabilised its fiscal position while leaving its banking sector free to extract maximum interest from its most vulnerable borrowers\u00a0 elevated non-performing loans, the aftershocks of Parate execution, the compounding blow of Cyclone Ditwah on agriculture and SMEs\u00a0 has not recovered. It has merely stopped bleeding.\n\nA single policy rate cannot simultaneously nurse a battered SME sector back to health and cool an overheating property market and vehicle import market. It was never designed to do both at once. Spain, Greece, Brazil, Korea, India, Bangladesh, and Malaysia show, each in their own idiom, that a Central Bank\u2019s most powerful tool may not be the rate it sets, but the precision with which it decides who feels that rate first, and who is shielded from it a little longer."
