    "md5": "f8dbe2fefb39eb4f8b1a3d3ad6d25734",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1855,
    "chunk_text": "Hettigoda also called on the bureau to maintain momentum by hosting a follow-up press conference next month to formally disclose clear tender timelines and structural allocations for the upcoming Rs. 5 billion global campaign.\n\nJayasundera emphasized that destination marketing must actively obligate global travel distributors to promote Sri Lanka: \u201cThe tour operators, of course, promote many different destinations globally, so we need to get the support of the tour operators. We have to work together and carry out joint marketing with the tour operators. Because Sri Lanka is not the only country that is an option as a destination, if we want to get the support of the tour operators, we need to support the tour operator and make the tour operator obliged to support the destination.\u201d\n\nJayasundera also highlighted the expanding aviation landscape, pointing out that several international carriers are entering or resuming operations to Colombo.\n\n\u201cIn the next season, we will have about five new airlines coming into Sri Lanka. We have already, I think started yesterday, we have VietJet, Thai Airways, French Bee, Jetstar, and of course British Airways will start their operations to Sri Lanka,\u201d he noted, urging the state to implement joint promotional mechanisms that support seat capacity and encourage airlines to increase flight frequencies. Bilateral strategic marketing pacts have already been executed with Emirates, with discussions underway to finalize agreements with IndiGo, Turkish Airlines, and Qatar Airways. Simultaneously, Jayasundera emphasized that reaching 5 million arrivals and generating 8 billion to 10 billion US dollars in revenue by 2030 requires unlocking domestic air travel to seamlessly connect coastal resorts with cultural and eastern aerodromes, transforming Sri Lanka into a true year-round destination."
