    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b4e98bfaedf5bbb3dc8bbed6fbd64aab",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1753,
    "chunk_text": "Even though booksellers are \u2018happy\u2019 about the spike in book sales, they have expressed dissatisfaction regarding the idea of destroying books. However, documents from Anthropic revealed about a project of ingesting old books known as \u2018Project Panama\u2019. According to the contents, the company aims to \u2018destructively scan all the books in the world.\u2019\n\nAI systems have been developed and modelled similarly to how the human brain works. Some models are being trained on over a trillion words. Another development is the way in which users interact with AI by way of prompts. Prompts are either questions or instructions. Prompts keep most users engaged and they eventually become attached to an unknown robot.\n\nResearch studies indicate that chatbots can stimulate empathy, respond without judgment and offer a sense of privacy, making them not only sources of information but also potential providers of emotional support. As a result, users begin to develop affective bonds with these systems, raising important questions about the broader social and behavioural consequences of everyday human\u2013AI interaction.\n\nSystematic reviews of social chatbots highlight risks ranging from emotional over-reliance with negative impacts on real-world relationships to heightened loneliness. Multiple reports have underscored risks pertaining to the link between interactions with AI chatbots and subsequent teen suicides.\n\nThe dark side of using AI chatbots such as ChatGPT came to the limelight on August 15, when a\u00a0 Massachusetts teen was charged of the double murder of his mother and younger brother. Investigations revealed that the teen had used ChatGPT to search for \u2018theoretical ideas or fantasy stories\u2019 regarding the killing of his family."
  },
