    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f38ad25ac62b84ec2a4d468fe1f2f547",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1625,
    "chunk_text": "This was also true for the Yahapalana government and the Gotabaya Rajapaksa administration. The Maithri-Ranil government, which also promised a new Constitution, instituted a Constitutional Assembly for that end only in January 2016, after adopting the 19th Amendment to the Constitution in April 2015.\n\nTherefore, in a way, none of the current Opposition parties have the moral right to question the NPP government for bringing in piecemeal amendments in place of a new Constitution. However, people who voted the NPP has that right, not because the party did not foresee this necessity before the election, but there are no signs on the ground of a process to formulate a new Constitution, in spite of various claims by various government leaders.\n\nHealth and Media Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa told media in December, 2024 that a new Constitution would be presented in three years. In the same month Justice Minister Harshana Nanayakkara stated that initial steps of Constitution making would be taken in January, 2025. Prime Minister Harini Amarasuriya told Parliament in April, 2025 that the process of drafting a new Constitution would be initiated after the provincial council elections.\n\nThen again, she stated in Parliament on July 25 last year that initial steps for a new Constitution are being taken. Later in December, responding to the Opposition Leader, she said that at present reports of committees previously appointed to draft a new Constitution and other constitutional reform proposals are being considered. What is the real progress of the constitution making process? Or, is there really any process?"
  },
