    "time_ut": 1787207511.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-20-dailymirrorlk-6524264d-0006",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4ebfa09c5bec1012e6e3d84f9deba132",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1289,
    "chunk_text": "This is why our stand is stronger and it affects the government. Throughout the history we were in the JVP. While we were there we expected a socialist society, and now they have changed all their policies get into action in a pragmatic way.\n\nWe have learnt a lot from the Soviet Union and other socialist countries, especially in Eastern European countries, and China as well. They have changed this society a lot. But still, we have learned a lot and we need to implement a new socialist programme.\n\nQ\u00a0 You emphasised on the phrase \u2018being pragmatic\u2019. Do you mean AKD is being pragmatic?\n\nI said pragmatism. Being pragmatic is good. But pragmatism is not good because, then you fall into the circumstances. You don\u2019t have a strategy. You don\u2019t have a plan, long term plan. Whatever the situation arises, you fall into that. So that is called pragmatism. Then you don\u2019t have a clear vision. The word Pragmatic is good because according to the situation, even though you have a plan and a vision and a strategic programme, still, you have to be practical. So that is a different thing. Pragmatism is totally different. You don\u2019t have a vision.\n\nQ\u00a0Would you support an alliance formed by the opposition parties if the objective is to defeat the NPP led government? If not, why?"
  },
