    "md5": "6e5d13f1965d05a075e2d6efc8dedd65",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1873,
    "chunk_text": "El Ni\u00f1o : Heavier Maha rains, drier Yala raise food security concerns for Sri Lanka\n\nColombo, Aug. 20 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka is preparing for a potentially difficult agricultural cycle as changing weather patterns linked to El Ni\u00f1o raise concerns over food security, with heavier-than-usual rainfall expected during the coming Maha season followed by a significantly drier Yala season next year.\n\nThe Department of Agriculture is urging farmers to treat the increased rainfall expected during the Maha season as an opportunity to build water reserves for the next Yala, when drought conditions are expected to place greater pressure on agriculture.\n\n\u201cWe are expecting more rainfall during the Maha season, but the next Yala is expected to be drier. Therefore, the rainfall we receive during Maha has to be retained for use during the next Yala season,\u201d P. Sisira Kumara, Director of Extension and Training at the Department of Agriculture, said.\n\nThe Maha season is expected to receive increased rainfall from late September through October and November this year, and the Department is encouraging farmers to conserve both surface water and soil moisture by constructing small ponds in cultivation areas and adopting farming practices that reduce moisture loss such as burying coconut husks in cultivation lands to help retain moisture in the soil.\n\nThe Department is also considering bringing the Maha paddy cultivation forward by around two weeks this year, depending on when rainfall begins. The move is aimed at making maximum use of rainfall for cultivation, while reducing the amount of water drawn from reservoirs, which would enable more water to be carried over into the next Yala season. \u201cIf we can make greater use of the Maha rainfall for cultivation, we can conserve more water in the reservoirs and keep that water available for Yala,\u201d he said."
  },
