    "chunk_text": "The Department is also considering bringing the Maha paddy cultivation forward by around two weeks this year, depending on when rainfall begins. The move is aimed at making maximum use of rainfall for cultivation, while reducing the amount of water drawn from reservoirs, which would enable more water to be carried over into the next Yala season. \u201cIf we can make greater use of the Maha rainfall for cultivation, we can conserve more water in the reservoirs and keep that water available for Yala,\u201d he said.\n\nTo reduce the impact, the Department is also preparing to promote crop varieties that require less time and water to mature. Short-duration paddy varieties that can be harvested within around two and a half to three months, along with drought-resistant varieties, are being considered for cultivation. The Department is also intending to promote drought-tolerant vegetable varieties and other alternative crops for the next Yala season, in an attempt to reduce water-intensive cultivation while providing additional sources of income to farmers.\n\nMeanwhile, according to P. Sisira Kumara, the increase in heat towards the end of the recent Yala season did not have a major impact on paddy as temperatures began rising after the flowering stage. Vegetables and other crops, however, were affected by the higher temperatures, particularly with the increased heat contributing to a rise in pest problems, forcing farmers to use more pesticides, which inevitably resulted in increased production costs."
