    "chunk_text": "Russian missile barrage kills at least 13 people in Ukraine\u2019s Kyiv\n\nALJAZEERA - Russian forces have launched ballistic missile attacks on Ukraine\u2019s capital Kyiv, killing at least 13 people and wounding 33, officials said.\n\nExplosions rang out across several districts of Kyiv early on Thursday, shaking buildings and setting off car alarms, according to the Kyiv Independent, a Ukrainian news outlet.\n\nIn the Solomianskyi district, a nine-storey residential building collapsed and caught fire following the attack, Kyiv Mayor Vitali Klitschko said on Telegram. He said a children\u2019s hospital in the district was also damaged, with windows shattered and cars burning on the grounds.\n\n\u201cPeople are trapped\u201d at another residential building, he continued, with parts of Sviatoshynskyi and Solomianskyi districts also without electricity.\n\nUkraine\u2019s Emergency Services said at least 33 people were wounded in the \u201cmassive attack\u201d on the capital, with fires breaking out in residential buildings and at warehouses.\n\nIt said rescuers were at the scene of the attacks and had retrieved at least two people from the rubble of collapsed buildings.\n\nAn attack in the Brovary district of the Kyiv region killed one person and wounded another, Kyiv region\u2019s Governor Tymur Tkachenko said.\n\n\u201cOne industrial facility has been damaged. All relevant services are on site, working to extinguish the fire,\u201d Tkachenko added.\n\nReporting from Kyiv, Al Jazeera\u2019s Audrey MacAlpine said air alarms continued to sound in the city on Thursday morning.\n\n\u201cWe know that two fuel industrial facilities were hit,\u201d she said.\n\nMacAlpine said Russia\u2019s escalating missile attacks across Ukraine were causing growing concern as Ukraine faces a shortage of Patriot interceptor missiles. The lack of sufficient air defences will be \u201ca problem when Russia begins to target energy infrastructure, sites that will keep the heat and electricity on this winter\u201d, she said.\n\nThe attacks also prompted security responses in neighbouring NATO member states."
