    "chunk_text": "MacAlpine said Russia\u2019s escalating missile attacks across Ukraine were causing growing concern as Ukraine faces a shortage of Patriot interceptor missiles. The lack of sufficient air defences will be \u201ca problem when Russia begins to target energy infrastructure, sites that will keep the heat and electricity on this winter\u201d, she said.\n\nThe attacks also prompted security responses in neighbouring NATO member states.\n\nRomania\u2019s Ministry of National Defence said radar systems detected aerial targets near the Ukrainian border overnight, prompting the scrambling of two Spanish jets deployed on NATO missions and a Romanian air force helicopter.\n\nThe ministry said a drone entered Romanian airspace near Galati before crashing in an unpopulated area in Tulcea county.\n\nMeanwhile, Russia\u2019s Ministry of Defence said it had shot down 726 Ukrainian UAVs between Wednesday evening and Thursday morning over several regions, as well as over the Black Sea and Sea of Azov.\n\nThe attacks came a day after Ukraine\u2019s parliament confirmed Yevhenii Khmara as the country\u2019s new defence minister. Lawmakers approved his appointment 312-2, well above the 226 votes needed.\n\nKhmara is a career intelligence official who rose through the ranks of the Security Service of Ukraine (SBU). He previously led its Alpha special operations unit, whose personnel have reportedly been linked to Operation Spiderweb, last year\u2019s drone attack deep inside Russian territory.\n\nAddressing parliament before the vote, Khmara pledged to \u201cdo everything to defend Ukraine, to reduce Russia\u2019s war potential as much as possible, and to force the enemy into a just peace\u201d. Zelenskyy called the confirmation a sign that \u201cUkraine needs more strength. And it will have it.\u201d\n\nNot everyone was convinced, however. Opposition lawmaker Solomiia Bobrovska said pulling Khmara from intelligence work was \u201ca mistake\u201d, even while praising him personally."
