    "md5": "c64ea00cb2b825b77d374721116ca38e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1891,
    "chunk_text": "ASPI holds 21,400 despite third straight day of market weakness\n\nThe Colombo Stock Exchange extended its negative momentum for a third consecutive session on Thursday, 20 August 2026, with both benchmark indices closing marginally lower amid continued selling pressure across the broader market.\n\nThe All Share Price Index declined by 10.95 points, or 0.05%, to close at 21,405.62, while the S&P Sri Lanka 20 Index slipped by 1.14 points, or 0.02%, to end the session at 6,019.25.\n\nThe ASPI opened around the 21,440 level and initially moved higher, briefly crossing 21,460 during early trading. However, buying momentum faded as the session progressed. Despite several intraday recovery attempts, renewed selling pressure towards the close pushed the index back into negative territory.\n\nTotal market turnover stood at LKR 1.50 billion, with approximately 40.82 million shares changing hands during the session.\n\nThe Banks sector dominated market activity, generating LKR 490.64 million in turnover from approximately 3.61 million shares. SEYB.N recorded the highest turnover within the sector at approximately LKR 149.04 million.\n\nCrossing activity remained relatively limited compared with recent sessions. Crossings generated approximately LKR 109.90 million, accounting for around 7% of total equity turnover. JKH.N recorded the largest combined crossing activity, with two transactions totaling LKR 89.10 million involving 4.50 million shares at LKR 19.80.\n\nForeign investors returned to the selling side, although the magnitude remained relatively modest, recording a net foreign outflow of LKR 24.79 million.\n\nMarket breadth remained negative, with 93 securities advancing against 121 decliners, resulting in an advance-to-decline ratio of 0.77. The weaker breadth indicated that selling pressure remained broader than the relatively small declines in the headline indices suggested."
  },
