    "description": "Give war a chance:  Iran faces Trump\u2019s  economic  D\u2011Day",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Give-war-a-chance-Iran-faces-Trumps-economic-DDay/172-348538",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787269374.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-21-dailymirrorlk-7ba31ec8-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ccac9b0b66298d3483b8d800f23e9bb5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1745,
    "chunk_text": "Give war a chance:  Iran faces Trump\u2019s  economic  D\u2011Day\n\nIranian people endure more economic hardships as the war drags on\n\nTrade between the UAE and Iran is worth more than US$6.2 billion, with the surplus heavily in favour of Abu Dhabi, Iran\u2019s second\u2011most important economic partner after China\n\n\u201cGive War a Chance\u201d is the title of an article American political scientist Edward N. Luttwak wrote for the prestigious Foreign Affairs magazine in 1999. He argued that premature peacemaking does not lead to permanent peace.\n\n\u201cAn unpleasant truth often overlooked is that although war is a great evil, it does have a great virtue: it can resolve political conflicts and lead to peace. This can happen when all belligerents become exhausted or when one wins decisively. Either way the key is that the fighting must continue until a resolution is reached. War brings peace only after passing a culminating phase of violence. Hopes of military success must fade for accommodation to become more attractive than further combat,\u201d says Luttwark in the opening paragraph.\n\nHe argues that premature ceasefires tend to arrest war\u2011induced exhaustion and allow belligerents to reconstitute and rearm their forces. This intensifies and prolongs the struggle once the ceasefire ends \u2014 and it usually does end.\n\nLuttwak\u2019s argument, flawed or not, could not be more relevant to the ongoing United States war of aggression against Iran.\n\nAs the US\u2011Iran war continues and the 60\u2011day Memorandum of Understanding that saw more clashes than talks expired on Monday amid a widening trust deficit between the parties, the two countries seem more determined to exhaust each other in a war of attrition than to strike a deal to end the conflict."
  },
  {
