    "md5": "ddb7ef63ca18e058ebda8ecb02f9b593",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1899,
    "chunk_text": "It appears that the US acted in coordination with the UAE. The Gulf state did not resort to economic warfare even as more than 1,000 Iranian missile and drone attacks hit the country in the early stages of the war, though it could have.\n\nIran yesterday denied firing missiles at the UAE, claiming it was a \u2018false flag operation\u2019 by Israel and the US.\n\nBiting indeed, therefore, are these economic sanctions for Iran, which has seen its GDP per capita fall from US$8,000 in 2012 to less than US$5,000 last year, inflation soar to 90 per cent, and the US dollar sell at nearly 1.9 million riyals on the black market. Although Iran is not unfamiliar with dealing with sanctions and can endure the new sanctions, its resolve to fight the US\u2011led economic warfare has limits.\n\nSince a strong economy is vital not only to bolster the war effort but also to gain public support for it, Iran\u2019s strategy, aimed at exhausting US resolve to continue the fight at a huge economic cost, may backfire. High oil prices may disadvantage the Republican Party in the midterm elections. US military action may be constrained by dwindling weapon stockpiles. US morale may be low, as seen in reports from the USS Abraham Lincoln, deployed in the region for more than nine months. Yet none of these developments offer Iran permanent leverage. Trump will remain in power until the end of 2028. Together with Israel and America\u2019s Gulf allies, he will continue the war by military or other means.\n\nWhat options is Iran left with? To retaliate. This is the stance of hardliners, especially the Revolutionary Guard leadership, determined to punish the US for starting the war and killing the highly respected supreme leader. If Iran is not allowed to export oil, none in the region will. If Iran suffers economic harm, US allies in the region should also endure similar pain. Such a stance threatens to widen the war."
