    "chunk_size_bytes": 1888,
    "chunk_text": "Shanakiyan calls on Govt. to urgently ensure uninterrupted Filgrastim supply\n\nWarns cancer patients should not be forced to delay or discontinue treatment due to shortages\n\nSays patients reportedly paying Rs. 17,000-23,000 per Filgrastim injection privately\n\nIlankai Tamil Arasu Kachchi (ITAK) MP Shanakiyan Rasamanickam yesterday called on the Government to urgently ensure an uninterrupted supply of Filgrastim to cancer patients at State hospitals, warning that shortages were reportedly forcing patients to purchase the essential medicine privately at prices of up to Rs. 23,000 per injection.\n\nRaising the issue in Parliament, Rasamanickam said the shortage at the Maharagama Apeksha Hospital and other Government hospitals had been brought directly to his attention by cancer patients and their families.\n\nFilgrastim is used in chemotherapy, bone marrow transplantation and other cancer treatments.\n\nRasamanickam said patients were reportedly being forced to purchase Filgrastim from private pharmacies for between Rs. 17,000 and Rs.23,000 per injection, while Neupogen injections were reportedly being sold for between Rs .20,000 and Rs. 30,000 in some areas.\n\n\u201cFor families already facing the enormous financial and emotional burden of cancer treatment, being forced to purchase an essential medicine at such prices is unacceptable,\u201d he said.\n\nRasamanickam said he asked Health Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa to provide details on the extent of the shortage, hospitals affected, current stock levels, daily and monthly requirements, procurement arrangements and when adequate supplies would reach Government hospitals.\n\nAccording to Rasamanickam, Dr. Jayatissa acknowledged the shortage and assured Parliament that steps were being taken to resolve the immediate problem within one week. The Minister also said a special medical team had been established to address the issue."
  },
