    "md5": "7fba451d774665844eadabbd91f498b0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1897,
    "chunk_text": "Programme planned to certify chemical-free fruits\n\nColombo, August 21 (Daily Mirror) - The Government is planning to introduce a certification programme to ensure that fruits sold in the local market are free from harmful chemical residues, while measures are also being considered to promote safe fruit-ripening practices.\n\nTrade, Commerce, Food Security and Cooperative Development Minister Wasantha Samarasinghe instructed officials to identify fruit-ripening facilities operating under hygienic conditions and develop a mechanism to certify them at a special discussion held at the Ministry yesterday (20).\n\nThe programme is also expected to promote the establishment of technical facilities where fruits can be ripened safely without the use of toxic chemicals.\n\nThe Minister stressed that all stakeholders in the fruit supply chain, including farmers, middlemen, fruit traders and supermarket chains, should be made aware of the importance of producing, distributing and selling fruits free from harmful substances.\n\nHe instructed officials to launch awareness programmes on the benefits of certification and promote Good Agricultural Practices (GAP) among those involved in fruit production and distribution.\n\nAs an initial step, officials were also directed to identify several fruit producers and establish model technical stalls for the safe ripening of fruits. The Department of Agriculture, Sri Lanka Standards Institution (SLSI), Industrial Technology Institute (ITI) and National Post-Harvest Management Institute are expected to provide technical assistance and expertise for the initiative.\n\nAnother key measure discussed was the introduction of rapid test kits capable of detecting chemical residues in fruits within a short period. Officials were instructed to identify suitable testing kits and take steps to procure them to facilitate the testing of fruits entering the market."
  },
