    "md5": "f800ae8643c720c1511d3c3803b86c1c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1961,
    "chunk_text": "Hemas eyes Kenya as springboard for foreign expansion, pursues potential acquisitions\n\nTargets 15% int\u2019l revenue share by 2030, using Kenya as gateway to 330 million-strong East African market\n\nPlans phased rollout of high-margin stationery lines to scale Twiga\u2019s portfolio and profitability over the next two years\n\nReassures 80-85% of capital investment will stay in Sri Lanka despite dedicated US$ 100 mn M&A war chest\n\nHemas Holdings PLC is eyeing Kenya as an operational springboard for its expansion across the East African Community, alongside a pipeline of potential acquisitions following its recent buyout of Twiga Stationers and Printers Limited.\n\nBacked by a dedicated acquisition war chest, the conglomerate aims to lift its international revenue share to 15 percent by 2030 \u2014 though group leadership stressed that regional growth will not come at the expense of domestic investment.\n\nHemas Holdings Group CEO Ashish Chandra said the 15 percent target does not depend solely on acquisitions.\n\n\u201cOur focus is to reach about 15 percent of the total, which can actually happen from the current portfolios that we have,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cEarning from US$ does not only come from buying the company, it also comes from the exports.\u201d The group said at a media roundtable in Colombo this week that no further acquisitions are currently at an advanced stage, even as it continues to evaluate partnerships and joint ventures.\n\nThe Kenya move marks a milestone in the group\u2019s 75-year history, following three years of market study and relationship-building.\n\n\u201cHemas was started by my great-grandfather in 1948,\u201d said Hemas Consumer Brands Managing Director Sabrina Esufally. \u201cI don\u2019t think in his wildest dreams he would have imagined that what was conceived as a small local company would now become a regional player.\u201d\n\nKenya\u2019s appeal lies in its position as gateway to the East African Community\u2019s roughly 330 million-strong market."
  },
  {
