    "md5": "deffbd147ee1ef8f0b9f2f06997ed8f2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1721,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cHemas was started by my great-grandfather in 1948,\u201d said Hemas Consumer Brands Managing Director Sabrina Esufally. \u201cI don\u2019t think in his wildest dreams he would have imagined that what was conceived as a small local company would now become a regional player.\u201d\n\nKenya\u2019s appeal lies in its position as gateway to the East African Community\u2019s roughly 330 million-strong market.\n\nBasic exercise books currently account for about 85 percent of Twiga\u2019s revenue. Hemas plans to apply Atlas Axillia\u2019s design, R&D and LEAN manufacturing expertise to move Twiga up the value chain, adding higher-margin categories - ballpoint pens, pencils, colour products and art supplies - which the company estimates represent a US$ 300 million adjacent opportunity in Kenya. The first year will focus on stabilising operations and consolidating share in exercise books under Twiga\u2019s \u201cKasuku\u201d brand, which the company said commands 91 percent brand recall in Kenya; the higher-margin categories are due to roll out in years two and three. Cost synergies are also expected from joint raw material procurement, given Twiga\u2019s paper consumption is nearly three times that of Atlas.\n\nThe deal was structured as a US$ 22.0 million acquisition of a 75 percent stake, funded partly through a US$ 16.1 million foreign-currency borrowing facility over three and a half years. Twiga reported revenue of US$ 38 million \u2014 approximately Rs. 12.6 billion at current exchange rates \u2014 at an 8 percent normalised EBITDA margin for FY2025. The transaction lifts Hemas\u2019s international revenue contribution from 3 to 10 percent of group turnover; group executives said the deal is expected to become earnings-accretive from FY2029."
  },
