    "language": "en",
    "md5": "72b72bcfd90181a498e440d821e730ae",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1807,
    "chunk_text": "SLT-Mobitel leverages dollar reserves to self-fund network expansion, targets techco transition\n\nSLT chief says the telco is proactively managing its currency mismatch\n\n\u201cSLT Mobitel is not the struggling company it was two years ago. We are profitable. We are self-funding our growth\u201d -\u00a0 Mothilal\n\nSLT Mobitel has aggressively built up its dollar reserves and retired expensive foreign debts ahead of schedule to self-fund its network expansion while shielding the telecom operator from future currency fluctuations.\n\nSpeaking at a media briefing held in Colombo this week to announce the group\u2019s financial results for the first half of 2026, SLT Group Chief Executive Officer Dr. Mothilal De Silva outlined the company\u2019s strategic shift to clean up its balance sheet.\n\nHe noted that the telco is proactively managing its currency mismatch, as the company primarily earns in local currency but incurs heavy capital expenditure in foreign exchange for imported tech infrastructure.\n\n\u201cWe built up dollar reserves at the same time to protect ourselves against currency fluctuations. So we are earning rupees and we are spending dollars. So that\u2019s why these dollar reserves are important,\u201d De Silva explained.\n\nThe accumulated dollar reserves are being directly channeled into funding the operator\u2019s capital-intensive projects, including island-wide fiber rollouts, intelligent 5G deployments, and the construction of new data centers. De Silva emphasized that this strategy allows the company to aggressively advance its infrastructure without relying on external credit lines.\n\n\u201cWe are using our dollar reserves to fund network expansion without external borrowings. This dollar reserve strategy represents a proactive hedge, that is spending in dollars while earning in rupees,\u201d he said."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-21-dailymirrorlk-2c2f9d80",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-2c2f9d80",
    "date_str": "2026-08-21",
    "description": "SLT-Mobitel leverages dollar reserves to self-fund network expansion, targets techco transition",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/SLT-Mobitel-leverages-dollar-reserves-to-self-fund-network-expansion-targets-techco-transition/215-348570",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787284357.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-21-dailymirrorlk-2c2f9d80-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7c1d10acc1879bbd9da2c89a268ffbf1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1603,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cWe are using our dollar reserves to fund network expansion without external borrowings. This dollar reserve strategy represents a proactive hedge, that is spending in dollars while earning in rupees,\u201d he said.\n\nLooking ahead, SLT Mobitel is executing a major pivot from a traditional telecommunications provider to a comprehensive digital services technology company. De Silva noted that the operator is moving aggressively into adjacent sectors such as financial technology, medical technology, education technology, and agricultural technology. To support this transition, the company is preparing for its most significant capital expenditure in the coming years, termed a \u201ctransplanting\u201d project, which will involve comprehensive upgrades to its IP, optics, and core networks.\n\nArtificial intelligence and cloud services remain central to this future strategy. The company has already initiated nearly 15 AI applications internally and recently introduced AI tokenization for the first time in South Asia.\n\nFurthermore, the operator is establishing an AI hub to empower local developers and enterprises to deploy solutions cost-effectively, while aiming to train its entire workforce in AI competencies to build a workforce ready for 2030.\n\nTo further optimise capital allocation, the company is prioritizing infrastructure sharing with other operators. By engaging in active and passive network sharing for towers, fiber, and radio spectrum, the operator aims to reduce capital intensity and save valuable foreign exchange in collaboration with the Telecommunications Regulatory Commission."
  },
