    "chunk_size_bytes": 1894,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka becomes new transshipment hub for Lamborghinis\n\nColombo, August 21 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka\u2019s Hambantota International Port has become an important transit point for luxury vehicles including Lamborghinis as the Middle East conflict continues to disrupt shipping routes around the Strait of Hormuz.\n\nA senior official from the Sri Lanka Customs told the Daily Mirror that vehicles from countries such as Japan, South Korea and Italy are being routed through Sri Lanka before being sent to destinations in the Middle East, due to the ongoing global tensions and the closure of the Strait of Hormuz which was a vital shipping lane for luxury vehicles to be shipped to countries such as Dubai, Qatar, Saudi Arabia and other Middle Eastern ports.\n\nAccording to official figures, the Hambantota Port has already handled record vehicle volumes, with 90,219 vehicles recorded in June alone.\n\nConsidering the Port\u2019s handling volumes, 80% of the goods handled by the Port are for transshipment. Vehicle transshipments continued even during the period when vehicle imports into the country were suspended.\n\nIn the latest shipment, 14 Lamborghini vehicles arrived at the Hambantota Port yesterday. The luxury cars were transferred into seven 40-foot containers and transported to the Bandaranaike International Airport in Katunayake. They are scheduled to be flown to Dubai and Doha on August 21.\n\nAs Hambantota becomes an important Port for international shipping, more vessels are arriving in Sri Lanka for transshipment, a senior Port official said. However, a Customs official explained that the Customs Department does not earn import revenue from these vehicles, as they are only being transshipped. The Port only earns handling and related charges from these operations.\n\nThe Lamborghinis are reportedly worth around Rs. 100 million and Rs. 500 million each, depending on the model."
