    "md5": "79562d95296fc283cffcaf460ebde029",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1758,
    "chunk_text": "Cancer drug reportedly sold for over Rs. 25,000 amid shortage\n\nAn essential medicine used to protect cancer patients from infections is reportedly being sold for more than Rs. 25,000 on the black market amid a severe shortage in the country, the Doctors\u2019 Trade Union Alliance for Medical and Civil Rights said.\n\nTrade Union Alliance Chairman and Specialist Dr Chamal Sanjeewa alleged that the medicine, available under brand names including Neup, Neupogen, GCadF and Filgrastim, normally costs around Rs. 900 but has become extremely expensive and difficult to obtain due to the shortage.\n\nHe said the medicine plays an important role in the treatment of cancer patients, including children, as well as those undergoing chemotherapy.\n\nAccording to Dr Sanjeewa, the medicine is administered to stimulate the production of white blood cells and strengthen the body's immune system, thereby helping protect vulnerable patients from infections during cancer treatment.\n\nHe warned that patients who depend on the medicine are now facing serious difficulties in obtaining it, with some being forced to seek supplies through unofficial channels.\n\n\u201cEven at prices exceeding Rs. 25,000, the medicine is extremely difficult to find,\u201d Dr Sanjeewa alleged.\n\nAccording to him, the shortage was linked to a dispute over the acceptance of an imported consignment of the medicine. According to him, a large quantity of the drug has already been imported but remains in warehouses because the Medical Supplies Division has reportedly refused to accept the stock over an issue concerning the labelling of the medicine containers.\n\nDr Sanjeewa said nearly 100,000 units of the medicine were reportedly being held in warehouses without being released to the health system."
