    "chunk_size_bytes": 1749,
    "chunk_text": "According to him, the shortage was linked to a dispute over the acceptance of an imported consignment of the medicine. According to him, a large quantity of the drug has already been imported but remains in warehouses because the Medical Supplies Division has reportedly refused to accept the stock over an issue concerning the labelling of the medicine containers.\n\nDr Sanjeewa said nearly 100,000 units of the medicine were reportedly being held in warehouses without being released to the health system.\n\nHe argued that clearer instructions should have been provided to the importer at the initial stage and that the current situation could have been avoided.\n\nThe doctor warned that the issue was particularly serious because around 25,000 patients require the medicine every month. If the shortage continues, he said, more than 75,000 patients could face difficulties obtaining the medicine over the next three months.\n\nHe also alleged that several other categories of cancer medicines are currently in short supply in the country's health system, affecting both government hospitals and the private sector.\n\nDr Sanjeewa said the continued shortage of essential medicines was placing an additional financial burden on cancer patients and their families, particularly those who cannot afford to purchase medicines at highly inflated prices on the black market.\n\nHe urged the Ministry of Health, the National Cancer Institute, the National Institute of Blood Diseases and other relevant authorities to intervene immediately and resolve the dispute over the imported stock.\n\nHe stressed that administrative and regulatory issues should not be allowed to disrupt the supply of life-saving medicines to patients undergoing critical cancer treatment."
