    "description": "National Youth Innovation Challenge to mark World Food Day in Sri Lanka",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/National-Youth-Innovation-Challenge-to-mark-World-Food-Day-in-Sri-Lanka/56-796365",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1787339134.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-22-dailyftlk-a19d1cbe-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ba132bafb288e60750214dbd2107808d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1659,
    "chunk_text": "National Youth Innovation Challenge to mark World Food Day in Sri Lanka\n\nFrom left: Assistant FAO Representative \u2013 Administration Dihan Hettige, HNB Managing Director/CEO Damith Pallewatte, FAO Representative for Sri Lanka and Maldives Vimlendra Sharan, HNB Senior Vice President/Chief Sustainability Officer Priyanka Wijayaratne, Vice President \u2013 Agribusiness Deepal Udukumbura, and Assistant Manager \u2013 Sustainable Business Shanel Perera\n\nKey initiatives by FAO in partnership with Education Ministry and HNB\n\nThe Food and Agriculture Organisation of the United Nations (FAO), in partnership with the Education, Higher Education and Vocational Education Ministry and Hatton National Bank (HNB), has launched the World Food Day 2026 Youth Innovation Challenge, a nationwide competition that invites university students to develop innovative solutions to address some of Sri Lanka\u2019s most pressing agrifood systems challenges.\n\nWorld Food Day is observed annually on 16 October to commemorate the founding of FAO in 1945. Celebrated in over 150 countries, it raises awareness of the importance of food security, nutrition and sustainable agrifood systems while promoting collective action to ensure healthy diets for all.\n\nHeld under the World Food Day 2026 theme, \u201cInnovate Today \u2013 Nourish Tomorrow,\u201d the initiative seeks to harness the creativity, knowledge and entrepreneurial spirit of young people to generate practical, scalable solutions that contribute to more efficient, resilient and sustainable agrifood systems.\n\nThe challenge is being implemented with the support ofthe Agriculture, Livestock, Land and Irrigation Ministry; Fisheries,"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-22-dailyftlk-a19d1cbe",
    "num": "dailyftlk-a19d1cbe",
    "date_str": "2026-08-22",
    "description": "National Youth Innovation Challenge to mark World Food Day in Sri Lanka",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/National-Youth-Innovation-Challenge-to-mark-World-Food-Day-in-Sri-Lanka/56-796365",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1787339134.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-22-dailyftlk-a19d1cbe-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a8edc1e9534a5822b07e372988f319de",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1947,
    "chunk_text": "Held under the World Food Day 2026 theme, \u201cInnovate Today \u2013 Nourish Tomorrow,\u201d the initiative seeks to harness the creativity, knowledge and entrepreneurial spirit of young people to generate practical, scalable solutions that contribute to more efficient, resilient and sustainable agrifood systems.\n\nThe challenge is being implemented with the support ofthe Agriculture, Livestock, Land and Irrigation Ministry; Fisheries,\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s agrifood systems continue to face a range of challenges, including post-harvest losses, climate-related stresses, inefficient water use, pest and disease threats, weak market linkages, fisheries spoilage, gear loss, and constraints to affordable aquaculture management. At the same time, universities across the country possess considerable expertise in research, engineering, digital technology and entrepreneurship that can help address these challenges through innovation.\n\nPrime Minister and Education, Higher Education and Vocational Education Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya said: \u201cOur universities are home to talented young people with the creativity, knowledge and determination to solve some of our country\u2019s most pressing challenges. Through the World Food Day Youth Innovation Challenge, we are creating opportunities for students from diverse disciplines to apply their learning to real-world problems while strengthening innovation and entrepreneurship across higher education. We hope this initiative will inspire the next generation of innovators to contribute meaningfully to building a more resilient, sustainable and food-secure Sri Lanka.\u201d\n\nThe Youth Innovation Challenge will provide a national platform for multidisciplinary student teams from state and private universities and higher education institutes to develop solutions responding to real-world challenge statements in agriculture, fisheries, livestock, environment, climate resilience, food security and nutrition."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-22-dailyftlk-a19d1cbe",
    "num": "dailyftlk-a19d1cbe",
    "date_str": "2026-08-22",
    "description": "National Youth Innovation Challenge to mark World Food Day in Sri Lanka",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/National-Youth-Innovation-Challenge-to-mark-World-Food-Day-in-Sri-Lanka/56-796365",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1787339134.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-22-dailyftlk-a19d1cbe-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b9a090173113b0a0e9e8adea9a5466c3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1855,
    "chunk_text": "The Youth Innovation Challenge will provide a national platform for multidisciplinary student teams from state and private universities and higher education institutes to develop solutions responding to real-world challenge statements in agriculture, fisheries, livestock, environment, climate resilience, food security and nutrition.\n\nFAO Representative for Sri Lanka and the Maldives Vimlendra Sharan said: \u201cThe future of Sri Lanka\u2019s agrifood systems depends on innovation and on the energy and creativity of young people. Through this challenge, FAO and its partners are creating opportunities for students to transform ideas into practical solutions that can contribute to more resilient, sustainable and inclusive agrifood systems while supporting national development priorities.\u201d\n\nThe competition will be conducted through a multi-stage process beginning with a nationwide call for concept notes. Following an initial evaluation, approximately 25 teams will be invited to participate in an online pitching round, from which the top 10 teams will be selected to develop prototypes with mentorship from technical experts and industry partners.\n\nThe finalists will present their prototypes during the national World Food Day 2026 celebrations, where the top five teams will pitch their innovations before a panel of judges and potential investors. The top three teams will receive cash awards, while all finalists will gain national recognition and opportunities to connect with Government, academia, development partners and the private sector.\n\nIn addition to encouraging innovation, the challenge aims to strengthen collaboration between universities, government institutions, industry and development partners, helping bridge academic research with practical applications that can benefit farmers, fishers, livestock producers and consumers."
  },
