    "chunk_size_bytes": 1481,
    "chunk_text": "Dissanayake\u2019s critical contributions successfully challenge Eurocentric hegemony within film analysis. For decades, Western-trained scholars evaluated Asian visual culture through universalising lenses like Hollywood continuity editing or psychoanalysis. Dissanayake disrupts this by activating classical Eastern poetics, utilising the Sanskrit concepts of Rasa (aesthetic flavor) and Dhvani (suggestion) to decode the works of Satyajit Ray and Lester James Peries (Dissanayake 2003).\n\nDespite these insights, a rigorous anthropological critique highlights the core limitation of Dissanayake\u2019s approach: the trap of textual essentialism. Rooted primarily in literary and film studies, his methodology relies almost exclusively on high classical texts, elite canonical histories, and formal aesthetics. From an ethnographic standpoint, this high-literary bias risks flattening the internal fissures, power hierarchies, and actual lived experiences of South Asian communities.\n\nAs cultural anthropologists of the region have long observed, prioritising Brahmanical or highly Sanskritised textual models to explain modern creative practices inevitably replicates the ideological biases of elite, literate classes (Dirks 2001). By filtering visual culture exclusively through classical epics or Rasa theory, Dissanayake\u2019s framework inadvertently glosses over the complex socio-political fractures of caste and gender that dictate how communities actually experience social space."
