    "md5": "a394a87c7a8b3f89e2c00a2b576997b8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1944,
    "chunk_text": "Far from being only the story of this cunning leopard, Ondaatje uses the narrative of his return to Sri Lanka after thirty-eight years to pick up the pieces of his early family life. He uses the story of the man-eater as a metaphor for his relationship with his tyrannical but much-loved father. It is a classic parable and love story that has sold over a hundred thousand copies, describing a way of life that has now completely disappeared on the island he loved so much.\n\nAt that time, Quint Ondaatje was one of the six most powerful people in Holland and briefly ran the Dutch East India Company. Eventually, Bonaparte\u2014suspected of being jealous of his brother\u2019s popularity in Holland\u2014returned Louis to France and exiled Ondaatje to Java, where he died.\n\nSir Christopher Ondaatje was a direct descendant of the original Ondaatje (1659) and was immensely proud of his heritage. There have been many Ondaatjes in Ceylon and Sri Lanka\u2014lawyers, politicians, authors, and members of the Church, particularly associated with St. Thomas\u2019 in Gintupitiya. There is biographical information on many of these family members, the most recent of whom is Michael Ondaatje, Christopher\u2019s younger brother, who wrote Running in the Family (1982).\u00a0 The best of Christopher Ondaatje\u2019s books are The Man-Eater of Punanai and Woolf in Ceylon. Christopher switched between living in Canada, London, and The Bahamas with his wife, Valda. Their three children live in California and Greenwich, Connecticut, in the USA, and in England.\n\nSir Christopher Ondaatje\u2019s illustrious life was defined by extreme experiences, bold adventures, and a deep love for his island home. Yet, his final parting words to us revealed his true priorities: \u201cMy legacy is my family. If I am going to be remembered, I want to be remembered for my family. Nothing else matters. If I have failed with them - then I have failed in life - and that is a dangerous option.\u201d"
  },
