    "chunk_size_bytes": 1541,
    "chunk_text": "Sir Christopher Ondaatje\u2019s illustrious life was defined by extreme experiences, bold adventures, and a deep love for his island home. Yet, his final parting words to us revealed his true priorities: \u201cMy legacy is my family. If I am going to be remembered, I want to be remembered for my family. Nothing else matters. If I have failed with them - then I have failed in life - and that is a dangerous option.\u201d\n\nIt took fourteen years for me to organise this diagonal journey in 2018, binding together many of Christopher\u2019s famous haunts: Yala National Park, Gal Oya Valley, Sigiriya, Anuradhapura, Wilpattu National Park, and eventually Colombo. This journey ultimately proved to be his last visit to the land of his birth.\n\nWe had not seen Christopher and Valda for four years. Then 85 years old, we were amazed to see that osteoarthritis had affected his feet, requiring him to walk with a stick. However, his enthusiasm remained undiminished, and we piled into our van for the long three-and-a-half-hour journey to Tangalle. Ever the practical explorer, Christopher insisted on having a roadside cobbler repair his old safari boots\u2014which he hadn\u2019t used since 2004\u2014and visited a local ayurvedic dispensary for Mee tree seed oil, passionately believing in the healing powers of Sri Lankan ayurveda.\n\nThe journey took us to Yala National Park, where we met with wildlife personnel and visited the Ondaatje Bungalow, a property Christopher had gifted to the park in 1992. Returning to Hambantota Rest House was an emotional event."
  },
