    "md5": "5ee53ac527d0507527cdd56d74951c2f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1880,
    "chunk_text": "The journey took us to Yala National Park, where we met with wildlife personnel and visited the Ondaatje Bungalow, a property Christopher had gifted to the park in 1992. Returning to Hambantota Rest House was an emotional event.\n\nWe then moved to the rugged terrains of Kumana, a picturesque haven for avifauna and migratory birds, offering an authentic and arduous camping experience. Heavy October rains turned the tracks to mud and made the tents damp, but Christopher thrived. He delighted in the camp food, roaring campfires, and daily safaris. We witnessed a rare sighting of the Ceylon Sloth Bear and watched in awe as veteran wildlife expert Shirley Perera calmly reasoned with an aggressive bull elephant, successfully asking the giant to step back and let the jeep pass. We spotted leopards in the rain and in a glade, prompting Christopher to remark, \u201cThis is the best life in the world\u201d.\n\nFrom Kumana we ventured to the Gal Oya Valley, rich with medicinal forests and ancient Veddha history. We spent hours exploring the immense Senanayake Samudraya reservoir by boat, marvelling at elephants swimming between islands and birds roosting on submerged trees. We then rested and wrote at the magnificently restored Ulagalla Walawwa near Anuradhapura, reminiscing about old friends and Sri Lanka\u2019s past.\n\nThe final leg tested our endurance in Wilpattu National Park. Flooded causeways forced us to abandon our camping plans for a small, rented bungalow nearby. Despite heavy rains and rough game drives, spirits remained high as we tracked the Wilpattu leopard and shared tales of the mysterious \u2018Devil-bird\u2019. At Sigiriya, Christopher astutely observed that the oft-ignored north-eastern side of the rock clearly displays the full head and flowing mane of the lion for which it is named. He remained a visionary, endlessly fascinated by the island\u2019s mysteries."
  },
