    "md5": "626c1958954dce4221616a389ef1a263",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1841,
    "chunk_text": "The development poses significant challenges for Israel, pushes the prospect of normalisation with Saudi Arabia further out of reach and cuts short the vision of a \u201cnew Middle East\u201d Envisaged by the Abraham Accords. Israel\u2019s appropriate response should be to immediately tighten coordination with the United Arab Emirates and India, Birvadker says.\n\nTurkey is emerging as a major threat to Israel, no less significant than Iran, Birvadker says. Turkiye will seek to use the alliance to expand its influence in the Gulf and challenge Israel both diplomatically and militarily.\n\nPakistan, for its part, continues to consolidate its position as an influential player while demonstrating the value of its strategic assets to the US amid the continuing deadlock and lack of a solution to the confrontation with Iran. Islamabad has a long history of cooperation with Riyadh. Their security relationship has traditionally rested on a bilateral arrangement in which Saudi Arabia provided financial and energy support while Pakistan supplied military capabilities and personnel.\n\nThe Ankara-Islamabad axis is gaining momentum, with security procurement and industry at its core. Turkey is now Pakistan\u2019s second-largest arms supplier, and the two countries are moving beyond off-the-shelf procurement toward deeper cooperation involving the joint development and production of technology from the ground up.\n\nMore recently, at an industry exhibition held in Pakistan with representatives from 32 institutions in the two countries, Turkish Defence Minister Ya\u015far Guler said an official agreement for Pakistani participation in the development and production of the KAAN fighter jet was close to completion. The relationship became particularly significant during the India-Pakistan confrontation in May 2025, when Turkish drones were deployed."
  },
