    "time_ut": 1787381202.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-22-dailymirrorlk-283f3b6b-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "328cece21485863323959d9f5a87f756",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1892,
    "chunk_text": "Hayleys Advantis-National Council for Road Safety launch \u2018100 for 100\u2019 rider safety initiative\n\nFrom left: Hayleys Advantis Group Managing Director Ruwan Waidyaratne, Transport, Highways and Urban Development Minister Bimal Rathnayake and Citypak Chief Executive Officer Vaseeharan Thiagarajah\n\nHayleys Advantis in collaboration with the National Council for Road Safety (NCRS), recently launched \u2018100 for 100\u2019, a national rider safety movement, aimed at strengthening motorcycle safety across Sri Lanka through defensive riding training and practical road safety interventions.\n\nThe programme has a national ambition of mobilising 100 organisations, with each engaging 100 motorcycle riders, creating a potential reach of 10,000 riders across the country.\n\nThe launch comes at a time when motorcycle safety remains a critical national concern. In 2024, Sri Lanka recorded 2,403 fatal road accident cases, with motorcycles accounting for 34 percent of vehicles involved in road accidents. Motorcycle riders represented 31 percent of road user fatalities, making them the second-largest road user fatality group after pedestrians.\n\nAs a transportation and logistics company, Hayleys Advantis recognises its responsibility to contribute to safer roads for the people and communities connected to its operations. This led the company to initiate \u2018100 for 100\u2019 as a practical and scalable approach to reaching motorcycle riders with safety education and training.\n\nUnder the programme, each participating organisation will identify 100 riders from among its employees, operational riders, delivery partners or regular motorcycle commuters. These riders will receive defensive riding education and related safety guidance covering areas such as hazard anticipation, road discipline, responsible riding behaviour, helmet and personal protective equipment awareness and crash prevention."
  },
