    "chunk_size_bytes": 1944,
    "chunk_text": "Govt to strengthen specialist healthcare services: Health Minister\n\nColombo, August 22 (Daily Mirror) - The Health and Mass Media Ministry is committed to strengthening specialist healthcare services, improving postgraduate medical education, expanding hospital infrastructure and introducing modern medical technology to address future challenges in the health sector, Health Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa said.\n\nSpeaking at the fifth Annual Academic Session of the Neurosurgeons Association of Sri Lanka (NEUROSURG 2026), held at the Taj Samudra Hotel in Colombo on Thursday (20), the Minister said strengthening specialist healthcare services was among the government\u2019s key priorities.\n\nThe academic session, held under the theme \u201cA Collaborative Approach to Neurosurgical Excellence in Sri Lanka,\u201d also marked 70 years of neurosurgery in the country.\n\nMinister Jayatissa said the development of specialist healthcare services required continued investment in human resources, medical education, hospital infrastructure and modern technology.\n\nHe highlighted the importance of improving postgraduate medical education and providing specialist doctors with the necessary facilities and opportunities to further develop their knowledge and skills.\n\nThe Minister also highlighted the need to establish appropriate training programmes and create a suitable working environment to attract talented young doctors to neurosurgery and other specialised fields.\n\nHe said the government was committed to creating the necessary conditions for the health sector to meet emerging challenges while ensuring that patients have access to efficient and quality specialist care.\n\nThe Minister also said that advances in modern healthcare cannot be achieved by a single individual or professional group. Cooperation among specialist doctors, medical officers, nurses and other healthcare professionals is essential to achieve the best outcomes, he said."
