    "chunk_size_bytes": 1431,
    "chunk_text": "Jaffna traders fined over Rs. 650,000 for overpricing goods\n\nColombo, Aug. 23 (Daily Mirror) - Three businesses in Jaffna have been fined a total of Rs. 668,000 by the Jaffna Magistrate\u2019s Court for selling goods above regulated or printed prices.\n\nThe fines were imposed following raids conducted by district investigation officers of the Consumer Affairs Authority (CAA).\n\nA restaurant & liquor shop in Jaffna was fined Rs. 300,000 for selling a one-litre bottle of drinking water for Rs. 200, despite the maximum retail price being Rs. 120.\n\nMeanwhile, another caf\u00e9 was fined Rs. 100,000 for selling a bottle of soda above its printed retail price.\n\nAnother trader was fined Rs. 268,000 after being prosecuted for selling Ponni Samba rice above the maximum retail price of Rs. 255 per kilogram.\n\nDuring court proceedings, the owner claimed that the rice was Paal Ponni. However, following a court order, a sample submitted by the CAA was examined by the Rice Research Institute, which confirmed that the stock was imported Ponni Samba rice.\n\nThe CAA has urged consumers to check the printed price and maximum retail price before purchasing goods and to obtain and retain receipts as evidence when making complaints.\n\nConsumers can report suspected trade violations to the Consumer Affairs Authority through its hotline, 1977, during working hours.\n\n1,090 kg of illegal veterinary medicines worth nearly Rs. 3 million destroyed"
