    "md5": "57375c1b48aafd9e1a9a256751445bf6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1949,
    "chunk_text": "Over the past decade, NSBM Green University has evolved beyond its core role as a higher education provider to become an institution with a growing economic, social and regional footprint in Sri Lanka. To better understand and quantify this wider contribution, NSBM commissioned the Strategy, Risk and Transactions team of Deloitte FAS (Pvt.) Ltd along with subject matter expert, Prof. Priyanga Dunusinghe to undertake an economic impact assessment of the university, examining its contribution across areas including economic value creation, human capital development, employment, foreign exchange retention, regional development, sustainability and community impact.\n\nNSBM was incorporated in 2011 and commenced its early operations through the Nugegoda City Campus before opening the purpose-built NSBM Green University Town in Pitipana, Homagama in 2016. Since then, the university has expanded significantly, with its total student intake reaching more than 44,000 between 2012 and 2026 and its current student population exceeding 14,000.\n\nFrom 2012 to 2025, NSBM produced 22,855 graduates, contributing to Sri Lanka\u2019s skilled workforce across business, computing, engineering, design, science and postgraduate disciplines.\n\nNSBM\u2019s contribution is particularly relevant, given the continuing capacity constraints within Sri Lanka\u2019s higher education system. Each year, a significant number of students qualify for university entrance but are unable to secure a place within the state university system, increasing the importance of credible non-state higher education pathways.\n\nNSBM has sought not only to expand access to higher education but also to strengthen the transition from education to employment. The findings from the graduate survey undertaken as part of the assessment indicate that 86 percent of surveyed NSBM graduates are in permanent employment, while 74 percent are employed in a field related to their area of study."
  },
