    "chunk_text": "NSBM has sought not only to expand access to higher education but also to strengthen the transition from education to employment. The findings from the graduate survey undertaken as part of the assessment indicate that 86 percent of surveyed NSBM graduates are in permanent employment, while 74 percent are employed in a field related to their area of study.\n\nNSBM\u2019s connections with industry form an important part of this approach. The university has more than 132 documented organisational partnerships and connections with approximately 1,800 industry players, supporting internships, career guidance, knowledge sharing, industry exposure and graduate employment opportunities. The institution\u2019s contribution is also not limited to preparing graduates for employment.\n\nAround 11.5 percent of graduates surveyed established their own businesses, reflecting the role higher education can play in supporting entrepreneurship and developing future job creators.\n\nNSBM\u2019s contribution also extends to Sri Lanka\u2019s external economy. Overseas higher education can involve significant foreign exchange outflows through tuition fees, accommodation, travel and living expenses. The assessment found that 35 percent of NSBM undergraduates surveyed indicated that they would have pursued higher education overseas had NSBM not been available.\n\nApplying this finding to NSBM\u2019s current undergraduate population of 14,000, the study estimates that approximately 4,931 students are effectively retained in Sri Lanka, generating estimated foreign exchange savings of approximately US $ 337.8 million annually, based on the estimated first-year cost of overseas study.\n\nBeyond reducing outward foreign exchange expenditure, retaining students locally allows a greater share of household education expenditure to circulate within Sri Lanka through spending on education, accommodation, transport, food and other services."
