    "md5": "11c310f06790b3319b84fbc5d7fa4d65",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1865,
    "chunk_text": "However what comes across very loudly are voices from the parliamentary Opposition, saying that the bill is all about the extension of dates for the retirement age of judges in the country. The parliamentary Opposition tells all of us citizens\u2019 of Lanka, that the government simply wants to lengthen the tenure of the present Chief Justice (CJ) of the country.\n\nTo strengthen their call for rescinding\u00a0 the 22nd Amendment to the Constitution,\u00a0 the Association has called for the support of the Commonwealth Lawyers Association. This Association too,\u00a0 has rushed in to the support the local lawyers\u2019 association. In our country, we are unable to settle any of our internal problems within our borders, irrespective of whether we are Sinhalese, Tamil, Muslim or Burghers or of any other ethnicity.\n\nDuring the initial days,\u00a0 when members of the Tamil community felt they were discriminated against, they first attempted dialogue locally, But,\u00a0 when these efforts did not succeed both the Sinhalese and Tamils called for Indian help. The Indians were not able to solve our problems. They worsened it. It ultimately led to the Lankan President demanding that the Indian army leave our shores,\u00a0 and the\u00a0 Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam (LTTE) assassinating the Indian Prime Minister.\n\nAs we did not make a serious effort to settle problems within our shores, our country was burdened with a near three decade long civil insurrection. Over a hundred thousand of our people were killed and the scars of that battle still remain.\n\nRather than call in international organisations to help settle our problems,\u00a0 we need to look within ourselves and identify why we still cannot look on ourselves as Sri Lankans rather than viewing ourselves through the prism of race, profession etc. The minority communities within our nation state have very definite grievances."
  },
