    "md5": "6d2fb633a999778e1110e5081c0a7981",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1830,
    "chunk_text": "There were, however, voices of opposition. During the constitutional debate on August 3, 1978, an SLFP speaker warned that the establishment of what was described as the \u201cautocracy of JR Jayewardene\u201d would be met by a struggle outside Parliament. The warning anticipated State power, arrests and draconian laws, but insisted that the SLFP would not be intimidated.\n\nThe confrontation reached its climax in 1980. In May, Sri Lanka signed the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, undertaking not to create offences retrospectively or impose retrospective penalties. Yet Bandaranaike was summoned before the Special Presidential Commission to answer retrospective allegations carrying retrospective consequences.\n\nShe appeared but refused to submit to its jurisdiction, arguing that in a parliamentary democracy a Prime Minister and Cabinet were collectively responsible to Parliament,\u00a0 and ultimately to the people through a general election. The commission proceeded in her absence and, on September 25, 1980, found her guilty of \u201cabuse and/or misuse of power.\u201d\n\nThe Civil Rights Movement objected, arguing that a fact-finding commission should not impose disabilities where ordinary rules of evidence and procedure did not apply, the findings were final and no appeal was available.\n\nOn October 16, Parliament passed two resolutions. One deprived Bandaranaike of her civic rights for seven years; the other expelled her from Parliament. Election laws were then amended to prevent a person under such disability from speaking, canvassing or acting as an agent at parliamentary elections. Violations could attract imprisonment or a fine, while candidates at whose instance the disqualified person participated could themselves be disqualified. Similar amendments were made to the Presidential Election Act."
  },
