    "md5": "861a89b77e528873e4092d024978ad9d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1926,
    "chunk_text": "Prof. Weddikkara: I would add sound technical knowledge, practical experience, adaptability and professional integrity. Sri Lankan professionals have been exposed to a wide range of projects and challenging commercial environments. Many have progressed into senior management and leadership positions internationally, reflecting the confidence placed in their professional judgement and capabilities.\n\nSeneviratne: This is a national responsibility of the profession. When government undertakes a development project, Quantity Surveyors are involved in managing costs, which ultimately means managing public money. Every taxpayer's rupee spent should have accountability and deliver value for money.\n\nQ: What should students, professionals and industry stakeholders take from PAQS 2026?\n\nSeneviratne: Quantity Surveying was perhaps not a very well-known profession in the past, but it has now become a prominent profession in the global arena. For clients and developers, if you want to get value from your project and from every rupee you spend, you should be hiring a qualified Quantity Surveyor to manage your costs.\n\nProf. Weddikkara: My message to the next generation is simple: never stop learning, never stop questioning and never stop innovating. Embrace technology and new ideas, but always preserve the professional judgement, integrity and responsibility that define the profession. For industry stakeholders, good governance, transparent procurement, responsible cost management and long-term value are essential. Every rupee invested deserves professional care, accountability and the best possible value.\n\nProf. Weddikkara: It is an opportunity to share our journey, learn from the region and demonstrate the strength and professionalism of Sri Lankan Quantity Surveyors. We should welcome the PAQS community not only as international colleagues, but as friends and partners in the development of our profession."
