    "chunk_size_bytes": 1543,
    "chunk_text": "Through a single Government-owned platform, decision-makers can monitor the performance of tea, rubber, coconut and cashew, assess district-level trends, review exports, track investments and subsidy programs, understand climate and weather-related impacts and identify areas requiring timely intervention. The platform is intended to help Government institutions better target programs and investments while monitoring progress against national development priorities.\n\nThe IDAT Program reflects the Government\u2019s wider commitment to developing trusted digital public infrastructure and strengthening evidence-based governance across agriculture and fisheries. Both platforms have been formally handed over as Government-owned digital public assets, with institutional ownership, capacity building and continuous data use incorporated into their operating models.\n\nCommenting on the initiatives, Digital Economy Ministry Secretary Waruna Sri Dhanapala said, \u201cDigital technologies, interoperable data systems and advanced analytics are becoming fundamental to modern governance. The Fisheries and Plantation Sector DSSs demonstrate how Digital Public Infrastructure can strengthen evidence-based decision-making, improve institutional coordination and enhance service delivery across strategically important sectors. Through IDAT, we are supporting a Government-led approach that uses trusted data and digital systems to advance economic resilience, sustainability, inclusive development and Sri Lanka\u2019s broader digital transformation.\u201d"
