    "md5": "55adf8b7bfadf42506488962dfbad917",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1970,
    "chunk_text": "President Anura Kumara Dissanayake recently highlighted capital punishment during a high-level anti-narcotics meeting, where officials discussed the possibility of imposing the death penalty for major drug offences. However, the government later clarified that no final policy decision or official agreement had been reached to end the country\u2019s long-standing moratorium on executions.\n\nThe mechanism is aimed at improving coordination among government agencies and ensuring that necessary assistance can be obtained promptly during emergencies within prison premises.\n\nDuring the inspection of the prisons, the Justice Minister also met male and female remand prisoners affected by delays in Government Analyst reports and assured them that measures had been taken to expedite the release of those reports.\n\nSenior officials from the Department of Prisons, Sri Lanka Police, armed forces, Colombo District Secretariat, health authorities, fire services, the National Water Supply and Drainage Board and other relevant institutions participated in the discussions and inspections.\n\nMeanwhile, Sri Lanka\u2019s prison population has declined gradually this month, falling to 38,464 inmates as of August 23, compared with 39,516 recorded on August 1.\n\nDespite the reduction of more than 1,000 inmates, official figures continue to show a significant disparity between convicted prisoners and those awaiting trial or sentencing. Unconvicted inmates account for more than 72 per cent of the total prison population.\n\nAccording to the latest figures, 36,449 inmates are male and 2,015 are female.\n\nOf the total prison population, 10,362 are convicted prisoners, comprising 10,101 males and 261 females. Meanwhile, 28,102 inmates remain unconvicted, including 26,348 males and 1,754 females.\n\nThe figures also show that 103 convicted prisoners (102 males and one female) were released, while 16 unconvicted prisoners (including 12 males and four females) were released on bail."
