    "md5": "ca394f9d58d0f526e06daf4ff7984fa1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1914,
    "chunk_text": "Audit exposes massive Parliament fuel irregularities\n\nColombo, Aug. 24 (Daily Mirror) - A special audit covering the 8th and 9th Parliaments has uncovered a series of irregularities involving fuel allocations, vehicle privileges and expenditure related to several senior parliamentary positions, including the Speaker, Deputy Speaker, Chairman of Committees and Secretary-General of Parliament.\n\nAccording to the audit findings, there were no effective restrictions on the Speaker\u2019s fuel consumption, allowing fuel to be obtained without a specified limit. During the final two years of the 9th Parliament, the Speaker\u2019s office recorded an average monthly fuel consumption of 3,994 litres in 2023 and 6,122 litres in 2024.\n\nThe audit noted that fuel consumption in 2023 increased by 66% compared to 2022, while consumption in 2024 increased by 155%. As a result, annual fuel expenditure for the Speaker\u2019s office rose to Rs. 19.37 million in 2023 and Rs. 26.05 million in 2024.\n\nThe report also identified irregularities in the fuel and vehicle privileges granted to the Deputy Speaker. Although Cabinet approval had been granted on November 5 to provide the Deputy Speaker with privileges equivalent to those of a Non-Cabinet Minister, the audit found that fuel had been issued without clearly defined limits.\n\nAccording to existing Presidential Secretariat circulars, a Deputy Minister is entitled to a maximum of three official vehicles, with the same provision applying to the Deputy Speaker. However, during 2023 and 2024, an additional private vehicle was provided, with 21,299 litres of fuel valued at approximately Rs. 8.55 million issued for it.\n\nThe audit further found that fuel consumption for the three official vehicles assigned to the Deputy Speaker exceeded the applicable limits by 6,980 litres in 2023. In 2024, two official vehicles exceeded the prescribed limits by a further 1,515 litres."
  },
