    "chunk_text": "PUCSL sets higher solar-storage payments\n\nColombo, Aug. 24 (Daily Mirror) - The Public Utilities Commission of Sri Lanka (PUCSL) has introduced higher feed-in tariffs for renewable energy projects equipped with battery storage, as the authorities seek to add at least 450MW of solar PV and battery energy storage capacity to the national electricity system by March next year.\n\nThe revised tariffs, which come into effect tomorrow (25), will remain valid until February 24, 2027, covering renewable energy plants, rooftop solar prosumers and projects incorporating Battery Energy Storage Systems (BESS).\n\nThe move comes as the National System Operator (NSO) has been directed to secure sufficient additional capacity to address a projected electricity capacity shortfall during the first quarter of 2027 and avoid power cuts caused by inadequate generation capacity.\n\nUnder the new tariff framework, rooftop solar systems combined with battery storage will receive significantly higher payments during the prioritised feed-in period.\n\nFor systems above 250kW and up to 1,000kW, the tariff has been set at Rs. 45.53 per unit for the first 15 years, while systems exceeding 1,000kW will receive Rs. 42.49 per unit during the prioritised period.\n\nSeparate tariffs have also been introduced for ground-mounted and floating solar projects equipped with BESS, creating an additional incentive for developers to combine renewable generation with energy storage.\n\nThe PUCSL has also imposed a cost ceiling on BESS capacity already offered to the system. The NSO has been instructed to ensure that the average monthly cost of the 160MW of BESS already offered does not exceed Rs. 20 per kWh.\n\nAny cost exceeding the Rs. 20 per kWh threshold will not be recognised, recovered or permitted to be passed on to electricity consumers through the end-user tariff."
