    "language": "en",
    "md5": "047d43e401920e253f8b84b5fce7b4f9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1735,
    "chunk_text": "There are also reports of accounts being closed at one branch and subsequently reopened at another, in circumstances where officers moved between branches over the years. How could a customer\u2019s banking history and risk profile be effectively monitored if accounts could effectively move from one branch environment to another without adequate institutional scrutiny?\n\nThe main issue is not that banks can never be victims of fraud. They can. The issue is whether banking officers, compliance systems and supervisory mechanisms performed the checks that they were supposed to perform.\n\nIf customers colluded with bank employees to circumvent controls, the banking sector must confront an uncomfortable reality. the weakest link was not necessarily the technology\u2014it may have been human integrity and institutional accountability.\n\nA commercial bank is not merely a payment machine. It is a critical gatekeeper of the national financial system. When a bank approves large international payments, particularly payments supposedly connected to imports, it carries a responsibility to establish that the transaction is commercially credible, properly documented and consistent with the customer\u2019s profile and regulatory requirements.\n\nThe existence of thousands of suspicious transactions involving hundreds of accounts and more than 100 companies should raise an obvious question:\n\nOne of the clearest weaknesses exposed by the investigations is the absence, or previous inadequacy, of real-time connectivity between banking systems and the Customs/import-monitoring infrastructure.\n\nThat gap effectively created an opportunity for money to leave the country before the system could establish whether the promised goods ever arrived."
  },
