    "chunk_size_bytes": 1874,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka needs a zero-tolerance banking integrity framework built on real-time bank\u2013Customs data integration, automated monitoring of suspicious transactions, enhanced due diligence, independent approval of high-value remittances, regular rotation of high-risk banking staff, protected whistle-blower channels, forensic audits, strong board-level oversight, personal accountability for collusion or bribery, and intelligence-sharing across banks. The objective must be simple. Prevent fraud before money leaves the country and ensure that no customer or bank employee can exploit regulatory gaps with impunity. Most importantly, the banking industry must stop treating compliance as a paperwork exercise. Compliance must become an intelligence function.\n\nThey should become the beginning. The deeper question is whether the country\u2019s banking architecture allowed a sophisticated network to operate for an extended period without sufficient detection. If the answer is yes, institutional responsibility must also be examined.\n\nBank\u2019s senior management, compliance units and regulators must be held accountable. How many alerts were raised, investigated and reported? Were suspicious patterns identified, employees warned, accounts restricted and authorities notified? If red flags existed, why was decisive action not taken earlier?\n\nAnother dimension that cannot be ignored is the growing culture among some young officials of seeking instant wealth and overnight success at any cost. When legitimate achievement is replaced by the pursuit of quick money, fraud, bribery, money laundering and financial manipulation can become dangerously normalized. Sri Lanka must confront this mindset by strengthening financial ethics, accountability and awareness\u2014because a society that glorifies unexplained wealth risks creating a generation that values money over integrity."
  },
