    "chunk_size_bytes": 1404,
    "chunk_text": "Cut thermal power to stabilise electricity tariffs: PUCSL\n\nColombo, Aug. 24 (Daily Mirror) - Electricity tariff fluctuations could be minimised if Sri Lanka reduces its reliance on thermal power generation and increases the use of renewable energy sources, Public Utilities Commission of Sri Lanka (PUCSL) Chairman K. P. L. Chandralal said.\n\nSpeaking at a media briefing held today (24) on the purchase of electricity from power plants using different energy sources, Chandralal said the high cost of thermal power generation had been a key factor behind electricity tariff fluctuations in recent years.\n\nHe said Sri Lanka should make greater use of available solar and other renewable energy sources instead of continuing to rely heavily on thermal power.\n\nChandralal also said that thermal power generation places pressure on the country\u2019s foreign exchange reserves, as the fuel required for power generation has to be imported.\n\nHe said fluctuations in global oil prices could affect the cost of imported fuel and, consequently, the cost of electricity generation.\n\nSuch changes in generation costs could eventually impact electricity tariffs, he added.\n\nChandralal said that reducing dependence on thermal power and increasing the contribution of renewable energy to the country\u2019s electricity generation mix would help reduce cost fluctuations and ensure greater stability in electricity tariffs."
