    "chunk_id": "2026-08-25-dailyftlk-7239eb3e-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "afc98cf51cbd8a26fff65b67927166ca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1574,
    "chunk_text": "Second THRIVE PSC meets in Kilinochchi, ensuring gender equality central to district development\n\nSenior Government representatives, development partners and implementing agencies gathered in Kilinochchi on 19 August for the second Project Steering Committee (PSC) meeting of the Together for Her: Resilience-building, Inclusivity and Voices for Equality (THRIVE) project.\n\nFunded by the Australian Government and jointly implemented by UN Women, Chrysalis and the Foundation for Innovative Social Development (FISD), THRIVE works across six districts to help women build sustainable livelihoods, strengthen resilience to climate and economic shocks, and take on greater leadership roles within their communities.\n\nIn its second year of implementation, the project\u2019s highest governance forum was hosted in a project district, Kilinochchi, reflecting local leadership and a commitment to ensuring that decision-making is informed by the realities, priorities, and aspirations of women on the ground.\n\nCo-chaired by the Women and Child Affairs Ministry, the Australian High Commission and UN Women Sri Lanka, the PSC convened national ministries, district administrations and implementing partners to review progress, identify priorities for the year ahead, and strengthen coordination.\n\n\u201cTHRIVE demonstrates the impact that can be achieved when government, development partners and communities work together to expand opportunities for women and strengthen resilient livelihoods,\u201d said Women and Child Affairs Ministry Additional Secretary (Development) Nilmini Herath."
  },
