    "md5": "0b2e09aa519f4700466ef389bb00f9d6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1967,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka to launch oil, gas licensing round today\n\nColombo, Aug. 25 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka will launch today a new licensing round for offshore oil and gas exploration this year, with the Petroleum Development Authority of Sri Lanka (PDASL) preparing to invite international energy companies to explore and develop the country\u2019s petroleum resources.\n\nThe authority said the licensing round is aimed at attracting global energy companies to explore and produce oil and natural gas offshore Sri Lanka. It said exploration carried out to date has confirmed viable natural gas accumulations off the western coast, with the development of these resources expected to strengthen energy security and support industrial growth.\n\nThe PDASL is expected to announce further details of the licensing process on Tuesday at a press conference.\n\nThe latest move follows a long-running effort to develop Sri Lanka\u2019s offshore petroleum resources. Exploration in the country dates back to the late 1960s, while significant progress was made in the Mannar Basin in 2011 when Cairn Lanka drilled two wells and discovered natural gas in the Barracuda and Dorado fields.\n\nThe government plans to seek investment proposals for exploration and production in four blocks of the Mannar Basin. Cabinet approval for the licensing round was granted in May 2025, while the PDASL subsequently began work to market the blocks to potential international investors.\n\nThe process had previously been delayed by legal proceedings. However, recent reports indicate that the legal obstacles have been cleared, allowing authorities to move ahead with the international bidding process.\n\nSri Lanka has also established a legal framework for petroleum exploration and production through the Petroleum Resources Act No. 21 of 2021. A national policy on natural gas was gazetted in 2020, setting out measures to develop domestic demand and provide operators with options to commercialise offshore gas."
