    "chunk_size_bytes": 1934,
    "chunk_text": "Tug-of-war delays cancer treatment equipment for five years\n\nColombo, Aug. 25 (Daily Mirror) - The procurement of advanced cancer radiation treatment machines for Sri Lanka\u2019s public health system has been delayed for nearly five years, putting the lives of thousands of cancer patients at risk, Trade Union Alliance for Medical and Civil Rights Chairman Specialist Dr Chamal Sanjeewa said.\n\nHe alleged that a prolonged \u201ctug-of-war\u201d between health officials and certain specialists had stalled the procurement of linear accelerators, which are to be purchased through funding involving the Asian Development Bank and the General Treasury.\n\nHe said the procurement process, which was expected to be completed during the tenure of former Health Minister Keheliya Rambukwella, was halted following revelations before the Parliamentary Committee on Public Enterprises (COPE) over the proposed purchase of used and inferior equipment instead of the latest cancer treatment technology.\n\nAlthough successive Health Ministers had subsequently taken steps to revive and expedite the project, the procurement process had again stalled, he alleged.\n\nDr Sanjeewa said Sri Lanka continues to rely heavily on cobalt-based radiation treatment, a technology that has largely been phased out in many countries in favour of more advanced systems.\n\nHe claimed that cobalt treatment could cause significant side effects and that some specialists were increasingly reluctant to use the method where more advanced alternatives were available.\n\nAccording to him, linear accelerator technology can provide more precise radiation treatment, reduce damage to surrounding healthy tissue and improve the quality of life of cancer patients.\n\nHe said the Government had already approved the procurement of new equipment for several hospitals providing cancer treatment, but the process remained incomplete due to disagreements among officials and specialists."
