    "md5": "0dcf768c74dc9bedf87f8feb95b72fd3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1957,
    "chunk_text": "BASL raises gratification concern over judges\u2019 tenure extension\n\nColombo, Aug. 25 (Daily Mirror) - The Bar Association of Sri Lanka (BASL), in a Special Determination petition filed before the Supreme Court challenging the constitutionality of the proposed Twenty-Second Amendment to the Constitution, has raised concerns over whether the extension of the tenure and related benefits proposed for sitting judges could amount to a \u201cgratification\u201d under the Anti-Corruption Act.\n\nThe petitioners, BASL President Rajeev Amarasuriya and its Secretary Nalin De Silva stated that a serious question arises as to whether the proposed extension of the tenure and attendant benefits granted to sitting judges could constitute a \u201cgratification\u201d within the meaning of the Anti-Corruption Act, No. 9 of 2023, as amended.\n\nThe BASL said this issue assumes particular significance because the same judges may be required, in their judicial capacity, to determine the constitutionality of the Bill seeking to grant such an extension.\n\nHowever, the petitioners stressed that they were not alleging any wrongdoing or casting aspersions on the judges. They said the relevant provisions of the Anti-Corruption Act were cited only to draw the Supreme Court\u2019s attention to the serious risk of public perception arising in the circumstances.\n\nThe petitioners also informed that it may be argued that legislative and judicial acts could be immune from the application of the relevant provisions of law.\n\nThe BASL specifically referred to Section 93(b) of the Anti-Corruption Act, which applies to Supreme Court and Court of Appeal judges, among others.\n\nThe provision states that a Supreme Court or Court of Appeal judge, judicial officer or other person exercising judicial or quasi-judicial power who solicits or accepts any gratification as an inducement or reward for doing or refraining from doing any act in their judicial capacity commits an offence of bribery."
  },
