    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5c21ef4e2088983eb6865feffa4ba24e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1817,
    "chunk_text": "The provision states that a Supreme Court or Court of Appeal judge, judicial officer or other person exercising judicial or quasi-judicial power who solicits or accepts any gratification as an inducement or reward for doing or refraining from doing any act in their judicial capacity commits an offence of bribery.\n\nThe BASL also raises a potential conflict-of-interest issue, noting that the sitting Supreme Court judges who would determine the constitutionality of the Bill could themselves benefit from the proposed extension of their tenure.\n\nThe petitioners are seeking a declaration that the proposed \u201cTwenty Second Amendment to the Constitution\u201d requires approval by the People at a referendum, arguing that the Bill threatens the independence of the Judiciary and the sovereignty of the People.\n\nThe BASL has also requested the Chief Justice to constitute a full bench comprising all sitting Supreme Court judges to hear the matter, citing the grave, exceptional and urgent constitutional issues arising from the Bill and an alleged conflict of interest.\n\nThe Bill, published in the Gazette on August 7 and placed on Parliament\u2019s Order Paper on August 18, seeks to amend the constitutional provisions governing the retirement age of superior court judges.\n\nUnder the proposed amendment, the retirement age of Supreme Court judges would be increased from 65 to 67, while that of Court of Appeal judges would be increased from 63 to 65. The Bill also provides a special rule for the Chief Justice, whose retirement would occur either upon reaching 67 or completing six years from appointment as Chief Justice, whichever comes first.\n\nThe Bill further proposes to increase the maximum number of other judges of the Court of Appeal from 19 to 24, effectively allowing five additional judges to be appointed."
