    "md5": "d69313574d4ab5468bdbaee075f6ff56",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1897,
    "chunk_text": "Tax hikes risk pushing consumers to illicit liquor: DCSL\n\nDistilleries Company of Sri Lanka PLC (DCSL) has warned that further increases in alcohol taxes could drive more consumers towards illicit products, undermining the legitimate manufacturers and depriving the government of revenue.\n\nThe country\u2019s largest spirits producer said successive adjustments to the excise duties, combined with a higher corporate income tax rate for the alcoholic beverage industry, had increased pressure on profitability and consumer affordability.\n\n\u201cThe unregulated segment of the market remains a structural drag on the legitimate industry,\u201d DCSL said in its annual report for 2025/26.\n\nThe company said every litre sold outside the excise system generated no revenue for the state while allowing the illicit producers to undercut the tax-compliant manufacturers on the price. Unregulated alcohol also posed public health risks, it added.\n\nDCSL identified further changes to the corporate tax or excise regime as the largest external variable facing the group in the 2026/27 financial year. The pricing and portfolio decisions would be calibrated once any such changes were confirmed, it said.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s alcohol industry has long argued that the steep tax-led price increases can reduce the legal sales without delivering a corresponding rise in government revenue, as the price-sensitive consumers shift towards the cheaper unregulated alternatives. DCSL did not provide the market-wide data quantifying the scale of the illicit alcohol consumption.\n\nThe company\u2019s payments to the state climbed 20.4 percent to Rs.116.57 billion during the year ended March 31, 2026, from Rs.96.86 billion a year earlier.\n\nThe total was equivalent to about 71 percent of DCSL\u2019s standalone gross turnover of Rs.164.38 billion. The excise duties alone rose 20.7 percent to Rs.102.90 billion, from Rs.85.26 billion."
  },
