    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6d018be075dc703d926c4ad4c5d327eb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1747,
    "chunk_text": "WindForce eyes South Asia engineering services expansion as generation curtailments squeeze profit\n\nWindForce PLC is moving to commercialise its in-house engineering and turbine repair capabilities into a standalone business line, with plans to deploy specialised technical services across South Asia, as Sri Lanka\u2019s largest renewable independent power producer (IPP) looks to build revenue streams beyond generation.\n\nThe push comes as the group revenue rose 16 percent year-on-year to Rs.8.04 billion in the financial year 2025/26, driven by the newly commissioned capacity and full-year contributions from the recent assets.\n\nProfit after tax, however, moderated by 4 percent to Rs.2.15 billion, weighed down by the industry-wide generation curtailments imposed by the National System Operator (NSO), formerly the Ceylon Electricity Board, during the low-demand weekend periods \u2014 the restrictions that cost the company an estimated 6.75 GWh in wind power generation alone. That squeeze is sharpening the case for diversifying into higher-margin technical services.\n\nAccording to the group\u2019s latest annual report disclosures, WindForce operates a dedicated engineering workshop backed by an in-house technical team of over 100 professionals, handling complex component overhauls, including wind turbine generators and gearboxes.\n\nHaving built end-to-end operational capabilities across its domestic fleet, the company is evaluating plans to offer these repair and maintenance services to the third-party operators locally, before expanding into the wider South Asian corridor \u2014 alongside its civil engineering and plant lifecycle management expertise, leveraging its existing overseas footprint to capture the cross-border opportunities."
