    "description": "Sri Lanka\u2019s data appetite surges 29% in 2Q as satellite broadband gains momentum",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Sri-Lankas-data-appetite-surges-29-in-2Q-as-satellite-broadband-gains-momentum/273-348820",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787636756.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-25-dailymirrorlk-37572817-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2e24a68297aa44ddd57a623a343a1a47",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2007,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s data appetite surges 29% in 2Q as satellite broadband gains momentum\n\nTotal data consumption reaches 1.48mn TB, led by mobile streaming and digital platforms\n\nSatellite broadband usage climbs to 13,118 TB as Starlink subscriptions near double to 8,455\n\nSmartphones dominate terminal landscape, with 17.59mn active units, while legacy voice minutes contract\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s national data consumption expanded at a rapid pace in the second quarter of 2026, driven by an insatiable appetite for mobile streaming and social media, alongside a notable surge in satellite broadband adoption, according to the latest statistics from the Telecommunications Regulatory Commission of Sri Lanka (TRCSL).\n\nTotal data usage across the fixed, mobile and satellite networks reached an all-time high of 1.48 million terabytes (TB) during the three months ending June 30, 2026, a 29 percent year-on-year (YoY) increase from 1.14 million TB in the corresponding quarter of 2025.\n\nThe most striking driver of that growth was satellite broadband; the consumption on the Starlink Lanka\u2019s network, the country\u2019s sole licensed satellite broadband operator, climbed to 13,118 TB in the quarter, up from 10,585 TB in the first quarter of 2026 and just 1,254 TB when the TRCSL first began tracking the segment in the third quarter of 2025.\n\nThe satellite subscriptions nearly doubled over the same period, rising from 4,471 at end-2025 to 8,455 by June 2026, reflecting a swift uptake among the users seeking resilient connectivity beyond the terrestrial network boundaries.\n\nMobile broadband remained the bedrock of national digital consumption, accounting for close to 70 percent of overall traffic. The mobile data usage crossed the one-million-TB threshold for the first time, reaching 1.03 million TB in the quarter, up from 786,636 TB a year earlier. The fixed broadband networks handled 432,137 TB, an increase of nearly 21 percent YoY, supported by the ongoing migration toward the fibre and LTE connections."
  },
  {
