    "md5": "25b988aeadb4a14d7a43b12517ec6b7c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1850,
    "chunk_text": "Mobile broadband remained the bedrock of national digital consumption, accounting for close to 70 percent of overall traffic. The mobile data usage crossed the one-million-TB threshold for the first time, reaching 1.03 million TB in the quarter, up from 786,636 TB a year earlier. The fixed broadband networks handled 432,137 TB, an increase of nearly 21 percent YoY, supported by the ongoing migration toward the fibre and LTE connections.\n\nThe hardware ecosystem reflects the same shift toward high-capacity connectivity. Of the 24.82 million mobile end-user devices active on the local networks as of June 2026, smartphones and tablets accounted for 17.59 million units or roughly 71 percent. Basic feature phones continued their decline, falling to 5.61 million devices or about 23 percent of the total, down from 6.39 million devices a year earlier.\u00a0.\n\nThe dedicated data devices such as dongles and routers numbered 475,619, while the internet of things (IoT) segment appeared as a newly tracked category for the first time, with 267,458 connected devices \u2014 highlighting how enterprise and consumer hardware is increasingly aligning with the heavy bandwidth requirements. The remaining 877,469 devices or 4 percent, fell into the TRCSL\u2019s \u201cother\u201d equipment category.\n\nThe subscriber metrics displayed a more mixed picture. Total cellular mobile subscriptions stood at 29.42 million as of June 2026, while the mobile broadband connections reached 21.86 million, a penetration rate of just over 100 per 100 inhabitants.\n\nThe fixed broadband subscriptions stood at 2.02 million. Across all 2.71 million fixed-line connections \u2014 spanning both voice and data services \u2014 LTE technology dominated, with a 60 percent share, followed by fibre at 19 percent, while the legacy copper and ADSL lines held 14 percent and 6 percent, respectively."
  },
