    "chunk_id": "2026-08-25-dailymirrorlk-a177e3e5-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6932fcc1d4daeb3ec4149101f44a6b36",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1873,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s apparel sector flags double risk from EU-India trade deal\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s apparel industry has warned that the recently concluded European Union (EU)-India Free Trade Agreement (FTA) could weaken its competitiveness in the European market by giving the Indian garments better tariff access while potentially complicating the use of the Indian-origin fabric by the local manufacturers.\n\nThe industry representatives raised the concerns during the discussions between the Export Development Board (EDB) and consultants appointed by the European Commission to assess the agreement\u2019s implications for Sri Lanka.\n\nThe EU-India FTA, the negotiations for which concluded on January 27, is expected to grant duty-free access to several labour-intensive Indian exports, once it completes the necessary legal review, signature and ratification procedures. The Indian apparel would consequently become more price-competitive in the EU, one of Sri Lanka\u2019s most important export markets.\n\nThe Sri Lankan manufacturers face a second potential disadvantage through the rules governing the origin of fabrics and other inputs sourced from India and used locally to produce the garments for export to the EU, under the GSP+ concession.\n\nThe industry representatives cautioned that any restriction on Sri Lanka\u2019s ability to benefit from the cumulation arrangements involving the Indian inputs could further erode the sector\u2019s competitiveness.\n\nCumulation allows the materials sourced from the designated partner countries to be treated as originating locally when determining whether a finished product qualifies for preferential tariff treatment.\n\nThe EDB said the government would engage with the EU to seek appropriate cumulation arrangements with India, aiming to protect Sri Lanka\u2019s export competitiveness and support greater integration of regional supply chains."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-25-dailymirrorlk-a177e3e5",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-a177e3e5",
    "date_str": "2026-08-25",
    "description": "Sri Lanka\u2019s apparel sector flags double risk from EU-India trade deal",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Sri-Lankas-apparel-sector-flags-double-risk-from-EU-India-trade-deal/273-348821",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787636802.0,
