    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "de9defd7309c7e9a08152357a343a1ef",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1714,
    "chunk_text": "The EDB said the government would engage with the EU to seek appropriate cumulation arrangements with India, aiming to protect Sri Lanka\u2019s export competitiveness and support greater integration of regional supply chains.\n\nJoint Apparel Association Forum Secretary General Yohan Lawrence participated in the discussion and highlighted the challenges facing the garment industry once the agreement becomes operational.\n\nThe EU is Sri Lanka\u2019s second-largest merchandise export destination after the United States and accounts for about 24 percent of the country\u2019s goods exports, according to the EDB.\n\nEDB Chairman Mangala Wijesinghe said that maintaining the preferential access through the EU\u2019s GSP+ scheme would become increasingly important as the Indian products gain greater price competitiveness.\n\n\u201cWe intend to position Sri Lanka as a reliable, sustainable and high-quality supplier to the global market, while encouraging the local enterprises to move towards higher-value, differentiated products and strengthen compliance with the EU standards,\u201d Wijesinghe said.\n\nEuropean Commission consultant and international trade economist Paul Baker said that the economic modelling indicated that the agreement could substantially increase the Indian exports to the EU and strengthen the foreign direct investment flows into India.\n\nHe said the EU\u2019s growing focus on sustainability, environmental performance, traceability and regulatory compliance could still provide opportunities for the Sri Lankan exporters capable of meeting those requirements.\n\nThe consultants said the concerns raised by the EDB and apparel industry would be considered in the final study submitted to the European Commission."
  },
