    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d3cf65083249ad8b582f44c55ea23d28",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1932,
    "chunk_text": "Govt.\u2019s Rs.95bn MSME,  agriculture credit programme nears exhaustion\n\nData from Finance Ministry shows Rs.75.6bn released under programme by August, equivalent to about 79% of combined allocation for MSMEs and agriculture\n\nUptake led by agriculture-related facilities, which disbursed Rs.38.02bn or 93% of Rs.40.8bn allocated\n\nTwo MSME facilities carrying a combined allocation of Rs.13.95bn are scheduled to be launched only in 4th quarter\n\nFinMin says demand has already pushed 2 major schemes beyond their original allocations\n\nThe government has disbursed nearly Rs.76 billion, under a Rs.95 billion concessionary financing programme for businesses and agriculture, bringing the package close to exhaustion months before the end of 2026 and prompting the government to consider expanding similar credit schemes next year.\n\nThe data from the Finance Ministry showed that Rs.75.6 billion had been released under the programme by August, equivalent to about 79 percent of the combined allocation for the micro, small and medium enterprises (MSMEs) and agriculture.\n\nThe uptake was led by the agriculture-related facilities, which disbursed Rs.38.02 billion or 93 percent of Rs.40.8 billion allocated. The MSME programmes released Rs.37.58 billion, representing 68.5 percent of their Rs.54.89 billion allocation.\n\nHowever, two MSME facilities, carrying a combined allocation of Rs.13.95 billion, are scheduled to be launched only in the fourth quarter, weighing on the overall utilisation rate.\n\nThe demand has already pushed two major schemes beyond their original allocations, according to the Finance Ministry, as the Re-MSME Plus programme disbursed Rs.25.99 billion against an allocation of Rs.25.7 billion, reaching 2,645 businesses.\n\nThe New Sapiri Rural Loan Scheme, which provides financing across 34 crop varieties, released Rs.25.94 billion to more than 50,000 beneficiaries, surpassing its Rs.25 billion annual target."
  },
