    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0dde3146a7801119152ad4d45eb6b2d3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1869,
    "chunk_text": "The demand has already pushed two major schemes beyond their original allocations, according to the Finance Ministry, as the Re-MSME Plus programme disbursed Rs.25.99 billion against an allocation of Rs.25.7 billion, reaching 2,645 businesses.\n\nThe New Sapiri Rural Loan Scheme, which provides financing across 34 crop varieties, released Rs.25.94 billion to more than 50,000 beneficiaries, surpassing its Rs.25 billion annual target.\n\nThe financing package combines the government funds, with concessional funding from the Asian Development Bank (ADB). The government-funded loans carry an annual interest rate of 5 percent and are capped at Rs.25 million, while the ADB-backed facility offers loans of up to Rs.50 million at 6 percent.\n\nThe programme also includes a disaster relief facility for the businesses affected by Cyclone Ditwah. About Rs.8.06 billion of its Rs.9.3 billion allocation had been disbursed to 3,966 borrowers, more than 90 percent of whom were micro and small enterprises.\n\nSeparately, 70 entrepreneurs, whose borrowings had fallen into the non-performing category during the economic crisis, received Rs.235 million in working capital financing. Another 342 MSMEs have signed revised agreements with the banks, under the government\u2019s loan restructuring programme and resumed operations, according to the ministry.\n\nAgricultural lending included Rs.11.56 billion, provided to 451 small and medium-scale paddy millers and cooperative societies, under the Odapana scheme. The funding supported the purchase of about 70,000 metric tonnes of paddy.\n\nThe scheme allows the eligible borrowers to obtain up to Rs.75 million at concessionary rates for paddy purchases. A further Rs.7.5 billion was allocated for the 2026 Yala season to support the purchases at the government-guaranteed price, with loans of up to Rs.25 million offered interest-free."
  },
