    "chunk_text": "US$36bn export dream needs labour reform, not a 1940s engine: TPA\u2019s Barath Arullsamy\n\nColombo, Aug. 25 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka\u2019s ambition to earn US$36 billion from exports by 2030 risks becoming \u201ca glossy brochure resting on a 1940s labour engine\u201d unless the Government urgently introduces a modern Employment Act, Vice President (International Affairs) Democratic Peoples Front - Tamil Progressive Alliance\u00a0 Barath Arullsamy said.\n\n\u201cThe Government wants factories to compete in the economy of 2030 while employers and workers remain governed by laws written in 1941, 1942, 1950 and 1954. A national export strategy without a national employment strategy is merely a press release,\u201d he said.\n\nNearly two years after assuming office, the Government has still not published a consolidated labour reform draft or fixed legislative timetable. Although Labour Minister Anil Jayantha Fernando has announced a unified labour code before the end of 2026, Arullsamy said \u201csoon\u201d could not substitute for a transparent deadline.\n\nHe noted that the previous administration had already presented a proposed New Employment Act to the NLAC and referred the reform process towards legal drafting. While unions legitimately criticised its content and consultation process, the present Government had inherited substantial institutional work and ILO-related expertise.\n\n\u201cIt was entitled to correct the previous process, but not to bury the reform. A change of government cannot become a two-year pause button,\u201d he said.\n\nArullsamy\u2019s policy study mapped approximately 44 labour-related enactments, with around 17 forming the principal operative framework. These include the Wages Boards Ordinance of 1941, Factories Ordinance of 1942, Industrial Disputes Act of 1950, Shop and Office Employees Act of 1954, EPF Act of 1958, Termination of Employment Act of 1971 and ETF Act of 1980."
  },
