    "md5": "049c9dc910a5bd7df003d93c64105899",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1730,
    "chunk_text": "Arullsamy\u2019s policy study mapped approximately 44 labour-related enactments, with around 17 forming the principal operative framework. These include the Wages Boards Ordinance of 1941, Factories Ordinance of 1942, Industrial Disputes Act of 1950, Shop and Office Employees Act of 1954, EPF Act of 1958, Termination of Employment Act of 1971 and ETF Act of 1980.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s National Export Development Plan seeks to increase export earnings from US$16.1 billion in 2024 to US$36 billion by 2030. Arullsamy said this could not be achieved by concentrating almost exclusively on apparel.\n\nRubber products, processed food, fisheries, spices, electronics, boatbuilding, pharmaceuticals, gems, logistics and knowledge services also required manufacturing support, skilled workers and stable employment relations.\n\nHe also warned against treating RCEP membership as an automatic economic solution.\n\n\u201cRCEP is a doorway, not a factory. If Sri Lanka has no competitive products to carry through it, imports will enter while our manufacturers remain outside. Labour reform is not an RCEP condition, but it is essential for surviving the competition that RCEP will bring,\u201d he said.\n\nLatest official figures place labour-force participation at only 49.2% in the first quarter of 2026. Arullsamy said low unemployment could not be celebrated when more than half the working-age population remained outside the active labour force.\n\nHe acknowledged the increase of the national minimum wage to Rs.30,000 per month and Sri Lanka\u2019s ratification of ILO Convention No. 190 on workplace violence and harassment. However, he said wage amendments and international ratifications were not substitutes for a comprehensive employment framework."
