    "md5": "7fc6a6ba48e9ca95320d66c74d243072",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1839,
    "chunk_text": "He acknowledged the increase of the national minimum wage to Rs.30,000 per month and Sri Lanka\u2019s ratification of ILO Convention No. 190 on workplace violence and harassment. However, he said wage amendments and international ratifications were not substitutes for a comprehensive employment framework.\n\n\u201cGSP+ is not a charity. It is a compliance dividend. Ratification certificates will not protect market access if enforcement remains weak,\u201d he said.\n\nArullsamy said conditions in private estates were among the concerns discussed during a recent meeting between a TPA delegation led by Mano Ganesan MP and UN Resident Coordinator Marc-Andr\u00e9 Franche.\n\nAn Amnesty International investigation covering 45 private estates and smallholdings in Galle and Matara documented allegations of intimidation, violence, withheld wages, debt bondage and movement restrictions, with several practices reportedly meeting ILO indicators of forced labour.\n\n\u201cThese allegations require independent investigations. Sri Lanka cannot strengthen Customs screening for foreign goods allegedly produced through forced labour while looking away from credible allegations on its own estates,\u201d he said.\n\nPublished estimates place estate employment at around 100,000, down from approximately 300,000 in the 1990s. Arullsamy estimated that the core active registered field workforce could now be closer to 72,000, depending on whether casual and off-payroll workers were included.\n\nHe warned that arbitrary outgrower and revenue-sharing models could become \u201cdisguised informalisation\u201d if workers lost guaranteed earnings, EPF, ETF, leave, maternity protection, collective bargaining and termination safeguards.\n\n\u201cProductivity reform is necessary, but efficiency cannot mean transferring every commercial risk from the company to the worker,\u201d he said."
