    "language": "en",
    "md5": "36455af0fbd56698d7c37c45f6f53a7d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1220,
    "chunk_text": "El Ni\u00f1o may bring above-average rainfall, flood risk in October-November\n\nColombo, Aug. 25 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka could experience above-average rainfall during October and November due to prevailing El Ni\u00f1o conditions, raising the possibility of flooding in some areas, the Department of Meteorology has warned.\n\nDirector General of the Department of Meteorology Ajith Wijemanna said heavy rainfall exceeding seasonal averages could be expected during the period.\n\nHe said the forecast was based on an analysis of weather data covering the next six months.\n\nSpeaking to the media on the prevailing weather conditions, Wijemanna said around 30% of Sri Lanka\u2019s annual rainfall is normally received during October and November.\n\nHe warned that if rainfall exceeds normal levels, reservoirs could fill rapidly, increasing the risk of flooding in certain areas.\n\n\u201cRainfall spreads across the entire country during October and November, and heavy rainfall is possible. This does not mean that flooding will definitely occur, but we need to be prepared for the possibility,\u201d he said.\n\nMeanwhile, Wijemanna said the effects of the El Ni\u00f1o phenomenon are expected to intensify during December, January and February."
  },
