    "chunk_text": "Man held over illegal road construction\n\nColombo, Aug. 25 (Daily Mirror) - A man has been arrested for allegedly entering the Panilkanda Reserve in the Deniyaya area of Matara District without authorization and constructing a road through the forest to gain access to private land.\n\nOfficers of the Deniyaya Kekundeniya Forest Field Assistant Division inspected the area on August 17 following a tip-off and subsequently arrested the suspect.\n\nMatara District Forest Officer Chandana Dayaratne said another person from the same area is also due to be arrested for allegedly aiding and abetting the forest crime.\n\nHe said the suspects will be charged under the Forest Conservation Ordinance and produced before the Magistrate\u2019s Court.\n\nAccording to the Forest Conservation Ordinance, forests are declared under several categories, including reserved forests and village forests, while the Forest Conservation Department has the authority to intervene in activities carried out within such areas.\n\nThe Department also has powers to investigate forest crimes committed in government-owned protected areas that have not been formally gazetted, and to arrest suspects and produce them before court.\n\nUnauthorized entry into a gazetted forest area is an offence under the law. Other activities considered forest crimes include setting fires, encroachment, hunting wildlife, obstructing waterways, setting traps, poisoning water sources, transporting timber without a licence and engaging in unauthorized timber-related businesses.\n\nMeanwhile, Conservator of Forests (Protection and Law Enforcement) Wasantha Gajanayake said the number of forest crimes reported during the first eight months of this year has decreased compared to the corresponding period last year.\n\nHe attributed the decline mainly to the Forest Protection Unit established in 1995 and said members of the public have continued to provide information about forest crimes."
