    "md5": "f8e201ccad26334c5dae9c37a6f2fbd2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1999,
    "chunk_text": "Billions in Mannar basin oil and gas put up for international exploration\n\nColombo, Aug. 25 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka has opened nearly 34,000 square kilometres of the Mannar Basin to international energy companies for petroleum exploration, raising fresh questions over the country\u2019s potentially vast but largely untapped oil and gas resources.\n\nThe 2026 Sri Lanka Petroleum Exploration Licence Awarding Round, officially launched today, will offer four exploration blocks in the Mannar Basin to international companies.\n\nThe move comes as authorities estimate that the basin could hold around two billion barrels of petroleum and nine trillion cubic feet of natural gas, based on existing geological data.\n\nThe official launch ceremony was held at the Sri Lanka Petroleum Corporation Head Office Auditorium in Dematagoda under the patronage of Energy, Ports and Civil Aviation Minister Anura Karunathilake, Labour Minister Dr. Anil Jayantha and Deputy Minister of Finance and Planning.\n\nPetroleum Development Authority of Sri Lanka Chairman Prof. Dhammika Thanthrigoda, Director General Dr. Neil de Silva, Ceylon Petroleum Corporation Chairman D.J.A.S. de Rajakaruna and senior Ministry officials were also present.\n\nDr. de Silva said previous exploration had already confirmed the presence of natural gas and liquid hydrocarbons in the Mannar Basin, indicating significant petroleum potential.\n\nHowever, he said that only a relatively limited area of the basin had been explored so far.\n\nThis leaves a vast area yet to be assessed using modern geological exploration techniques and advanced technologies, according to the Authority.\n\nInternational companies participating in the latest exploration round will also be given access to geological data on the Mannar Basin currently held by the Petroleum Development Authority.\n\nThe scale of the estimated resources could prove significant for Sri Lanka, which has historically relied heavily on imported petroleum to meet its energy requirements."
