    "chunk_text": "PERSONA Beyond the family name: Tilak de Zoysa\u2019s journey in business\n\nSuccess in business is rarely defined by a single qualification or an unbroken record of achievement. For corporate leader Tilak de Zoysa, a career spanning industry, corporate leadership, diplomacy and humanitarian service began with an unlikely chapter: failing his O/L examination twice.\n\nA member of the prominent de Soysa family and nephew of businessman and philanthropist Sir Cyril de Zoysa, de Zoysa would eventually rise to become one of Sri Lanka\u2019s longstanding corporate figures, leading some of the country\u2019s best-known companies and business chambers while building an international profile through humanitarian and diplomatic work.\n\nYet, his early years offered few indications of the conventional academic path to corporate leadership.\n\nAt Royal College, Colombo, de Zoysa\u2019s interests were largely outside the classroom. He excelled in sport, becoming Royal Primary Wrestling Champion and later playing cricket and rugby. Academically, however, he struggled. After failing his O/L examination twice, he passed on his third attempt with strong results.\n\nRather than pursuing university education, he chose a different route, one that would eventually shape his approach to management and leadership.\n\nFor seven and a half years, de Zoysa worked in the tea plantations of Maskeliya, gaining first-hand exposure to almost every aspect of the industry, from planting and maintaining tea bushes to plucking, weighing, processing and preparing tea for auction.\n\nMore significantly, plantation life exposed him to managing people and resolving difficult labour issues. Living in relative isolation and working closely with plantation employees taught him lessons in discipline, humility and the importance of respecting workers, principles that would later underpin his management philosophy."
