    "md5": "780bcf150f896cb9c9e8f9eabad02653",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1941,
    "chunk_text": "More significantly, plantation life exposed him to managing people and resolving difficult labour issues. Living in relative isolation and working closely with plantation employees taught him lessons in discipline, humility and the importance of respecting workers, principles that would later underpin his management philosophy.\n\nHe later joined his brother\u2019s refrigerator business, working in sales and after-sales service, and by 1971 had risen to become Sales and Service Manager.\n\nA defining turn came in 1978, when his father asked him to return to the family-controlled Associated Motorways (AMW), which was then navigating a difficult period with around 1,400 employees and weak business conditions.\n\nIt was at AMW that de Zoysa would make his mark on Sri Lanka\u2019s automobile industry.\n\nTravelling to Japan with his father, he worked on developing relationships with Nissan and strengthening AMW\u2019s international partnerships. He subsequently rose to become Deputy Chairman and Managing Director of the company.\n\nAmong the significant deals associated with his tenure was the introduction of CEAT tyres to Sri Lanka.\n\nDe Zoysa took CEAT\u2019s Managing Director, during a visit to Sri Lanka, to AMW\u2019s tyre rebuilding facility in Kalutara and later to the Kalutara Bodhiya, an institution closely associated with his family\u2019s philanthropic legacy. The interaction helped establish confidence between the two sides and was followed within weeks by a visit to India to formalise the partnership. He was also involved in securing and developing relationships with international automotive brands including Maruti, Suzuki, Yamaha and Renault.\n\nHis corporate career subsequently expanded well beyond the automobile sector.\n\nOver the years, de Zoysa chaired 11 companies, with leadership roles spanning businesses including Carson Cumberbatch, Associated CEAT, Dutch Lanka Trailers, Trinity Steel and Wipro International, among others."
