    "md5": "4dfa988bd0ef14bb842a387b4d31d942",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1874,
    "chunk_text": "Over the years, de Zoysa chaired 11 companies, with leadership roles spanning businesses including Carson Cumberbatch, Associated CEAT, Dutch Lanka Trailers, Trinity Steel and Wipro International, among others.\n\nHe served on numerous corporate boards and was appointed to the Monetary Board of the Central Bank of Sri Lanka, while his diplomatic engagements included serving as Consul of Croatia.\n\nAlongside his corporate career, humanitarian work became an important part of de Zoysa\u2019s\u00a0public life.\n\nHe served as Chairman of HelpAge Sri Lanka for 13 years before becoming the first Asian Chairman of HelpAge International in the United Kingdom and subsequently serving as its Global Ambassador.\n\nFollowing the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami, he was involved in overseeing the distribution of substantial international assistance, an experience that placed considerable emphasis on transparency and accountability in handling donor funds.\n\nHis international contributions were recognised by Japan in 2004 when he received the Order of the Rising Sun, Gold Rays with Neck Ribbon, an honour conferred on foreign nationals for distinguished contributions.\n\nDespite a career spanning boardrooms, international organisations and diplomacy, de Zoysa\u2019s approach to management remains grounded in lessons learnt much earlier in his working life.\n\nHe advocates listening to competing viewpoints before making decisions, remaining impartial, accepting responsibility and giving executives sufficient freedom to make decisions of their own.\n\nFor de Zoysa, leadership is not about creating employees who remain dependent on the person at the top. It is about developing people capable of thinking and acting independently, while ensuring that credit goes to those responsible for an achievement.\n\nThat principle perhaps best captures a career that did not follow the conventional route."
