    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6c75b800800cea9fcf8d472a27e3d06b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1858,
    "chunk_text": "Can ASUS crack the c-suite and state tenders amid influencer push?\n\n\u200bTaiwanese hardware major ASUS is seeking to convert its rapid retail momentum into lucrative enterprise and public sector accounts in Sri Lanka, though industry observers caution that breaking the institutional stranglehold of legacy commercial PC vendors will require far more than lifestyle marketing and consumer hype.\n\n\u200bWhile the brand has more than doubled its domestic PC market share from roughly 18 percent in the second quarter of last year to approximately 40 percent, its presence has largely been built on consumer retail channels, prosumers, and student buyers. Making meaningful inroads into corporate boardrooms, institutional fleets, and high-value government procurement cycles presents a fundamentally different test of institutional credibility.\n\n\u200bGlobally, the commercial and C-suite fleet landscape remains dominated by the \u201cBig Three\u201d\u2014Lenovo, HP, and Dell\u2014which collectively command over 60 percent of global personal computer shipments and an even larger share of enterprise deployments. While ASUS accounts for roughly 7 percent of global PC shipments, its enterprise footprint remains modest compared to its consumer presence. Corporate Chief Information Officers (CIOs) and government tender boards routinely default to established enterprise ecosystems due to long-term lifecycle management, centralized IT fleet deployment infrastructure, and dedicated multi-year service-level agreements.\n\n\u200bTo challenge these entrenched players, ASUS is deploying its dedicated commercial portfolio, spearheaded by the new ExpertBook Ultra alongside the expanded ExpertBook B and P series. The company is positioning these lightweight, neural-processing-equipped machines as modern alternatives for C-suite executives, MSME leaders, and mobile decision-makers."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
