    "md5": "94566b668d0ce85b37d7eecf41f48994",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1754,
    "chunk_text": "\u200bTo challenge these entrenched players, ASUS is deploying its dedicated commercial portfolio, spearheaded by the new ExpertBook Ultra alongside the expanded ExpertBook B and P series. The company is positioning these lightweight, neural-processing-equipped machines as modern alternatives for C-suite executives, MSME leaders, and mobile decision-makers.\n\n\u200b\u201cOf course, the overall market capacity isn\u2019t as large as that of our bigger neighbors. However, Sri Lanka has top-end users and technology enthusiasts who want the latest innovations. While we may not bring in massive quantities, we must make these products available to meet that demand. The needs and demands of Sri Lanka should not be neglected; every market is valid.\u201d\n\n\u200bHowever, unit economics remain a steep barrier for broad corporate rollouts. Mainstream AI-enabled commercial models begin at Rs. 254,490, while top-tier executive ultrabooks and specialized high-performance workstations climb past Rs. 1,112,990.\n\n\u200b\u201cOur entry-level pricing starts around Rs. 250,000, though pricing varies across different channels. Last year, costs were higher. This year, we\u2019re using the same chips in both top-tier and entry-level devices. This strategy aims to expand our reach from the top of the market to the broader consumer base,\u201d Sharma explained. He added that the company is actively investing in enterprise and user education: \u201cWhile AI is still relatively new and can be somewhat expensive, we\u2019re working to make it more affordable. With support from Qualcomm and our regional headquarters, we\u2019re also providing training and educational resources to help users understand how to effectively use AI on their devices, beyond just using it for things like ChatGPT.\u201d"
  },
