    "chunk_size_bytes": 1808,
    "chunk_text": "\u200b\u201cOur entry-level pricing starts around Rs. 250,000, though pricing varies across different channels. Last year, costs were higher. This year, we\u2019re using the same chips in both top-tier and entry-level devices. This strategy aims to expand our reach from the top of the market to the broader consumer base,\u201d Sharma explained. He added that the company is actively investing in enterprise and user education: \u201cWhile AI is still relatively new and can be somewhat expensive, we\u2019re working to make it more affordable. With support from Qualcomm and our regional headquarters, we\u2019re also providing training and educational resources to help users understand how to effectively use AI on their devices, beyond just using it for things like ChatGPT.\u201d\n\n\u200bSharma maintained that the creator and digital economy represents a legitimate and rapidly expanding segment of professional computing demand:\n\n\u200b\u201cBeyond gamers, there\u2019s also demand from creators and those who need powerful graphics for video editing and high-quality viewing experiences. The demand is increasing, driven by the growth of social media creators and influencers on platforms like TikTok and Instagram. We are paying attention to that demand.\u201d\n\n\u200bThe vendor is also setting its sights on larger institutional demand, including potential digital infrastructure requirements linked to major development hubs such as the Colombo Port City. But as public sector bodies and large corporations seek to balance digital transformation with stringent fiscal discipline, ASUS will need to prove that its commercial support infrastructure can deliver the round-the-clock enterprise reliability demanded by corporate fleets. Until then, winning over Sri Lanka\u2019s C-suite may prove far more complex than dominating the retail shelf."
  },
