    "chunk_text": "Millions bleed into dormant state enterprises as liquidation delays persist\n\nSri Lanka continues to bleed millions in public funds to sustain defunct state enterprises, driven by severe bureaucratic inertia that has stalled formal winding-up procedures, the Auditor General has revealed.\n\nDespite a binding Cabinet directive issued on September 3, 2025, explicitly instructing the Ministry of Industry and Entrepreneurship Development to appoint liquidators for inactive state-owned institutions, no substantive action had been taken as of May 31, 2026. According to the National Audit Office, eight state enterprises and statutory bodies remain in institutional limbo. Stripped of commercial production or service delivery, these entities continue to drain public resources while locking up valuable state assets. The entities marked for delayed liquidation include Sri Lanka Cement Corporation, Lanka Cement PLC, Hingurana Sugar Industries Company Limited, Kantale Sugar Industries Limited, the Small and Medium Enterprises Authority, the Wood-based Design Centre, Kahatagaha Engineering Services Limited, and Lanka Textile Mills Embilipitiya Limited.\n\nThe most glaring fiscal hemorrhage highlighted in the audit is Kantale Sugar Industries Limited, where manufacturing operations ceased in 1994. The state audit uncovered that 32 workers\u2014all of whom have exceeded the mandatory retirement age of 60\u2014remain firmly on the payroll of the shuttered facility. The Auditor General observed that while operational activities at the Kantale Sugar institution were brought to an end over three decades ago, the entity is maintained at a fruitless expenditure of approximately Rs. 1.7 million per month. This sum covers salaries, allowances, administration, and overheads for its aging workforce, with no concrete liquidation steps taken by the end of May 2026."
  },
