    "chunk_size_bytes": 1926,
    "chunk_text": "The most glaring fiscal hemorrhage highlighted in the audit is Kantale Sugar Industries Limited, where manufacturing operations ceased in 1994. The state audit uncovered that 32 workers\u2014all of whom have exceeded the mandatory retirement age of 60\u2014remain firmly on the payroll of the shuttered facility. The Auditor General observed that while operational activities at the Kantale Sugar institution were brought to an end over three decades ago, the entity is maintained at a fruitless expenditure of approximately Rs. 1.7 million per month. This sum covers salaries, allowances, administration, and overheads for its aging workforce, with no concrete liquidation steps taken by the end of May 2026.\n\nThe persistence of defunct commercial arms such as Kahatagaha Engineering Services Limited and Lanka Textile Mills Embilipitiya Limited reflects the state\u2019s prolonged inability to amputate non-viable corporate appendages from operating entities. While legacy institutions like the Wood-based Design Centre and the Small and Medium Enterprises Authority remain dormant corporate shells without an operational mandate, the Ministry is simultaneously struggling to deal with shuttered production assets within the Ceylon Ceramics Corporation. Although the Cabinet granted policy clearance in May 2025 to hand over four defunct ceramic factories in Eragama, Mahiyanganaya, Uswewa, and Oddusudan to private investors under public-private partnerships, progress remains gridlocked.\n\nMeanwhile, another four inactive ceramic factory sites\u2014located in Bangadeniya, Yatiyana, Weuda, and Elayapattu\u2014are being excised from the corporation\u2019s books to be converted into industrial estates.\n\nThe Auditor General warned that these prolonged liquidation delays trigger severe downstream economic costs, locking up hundreds of acres of prime land and decaying factory infrastructure while unresolved liabilities drain the Treasury."
  },
