    "chunk_text": "July tea exports slide 15% as key categories weaken\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s tea export volumes fell sharply in July 2026, extending the decline seen so far this year, even as rupee-denominated export prices improved from a year earlier.\n\nTea exports declined 15.1 percent year-on-year to 20.41 million kilograms in July, from 24.04 million kilograms a year earlier, a reduction of 3.63 million kilograms, according to data compiled by Forbes & Walker Research based on Sri Lanka Customs figures.\n\nThe weakness was broad-based, with all major export categories other than instant tea recording lower volumes during the month.\n\nPacketed tea, the largest export category in July, fell to 8.92 million kilograms from 10.77 million kilograms a year earlier, while bulk tea shipments declined to 8.59 million kilograms from 10.16 million kilograms.\n\nTea bag exports eased to 2.16 million kilograms from 2.24 million kilograms, while green tea exports dropped to 0.39 million kilograms from 0.56 million kilograms. Instant tea was the only category to buck the trend, rising to 0.35 million kilograms from 0.30 million kilograms.\n\nDespite the volume contraction, the average FOB value increased to Rs. 1,921.95 per kilogram in July from Rs. 1,766.51 a year earlier, an increase of Rs. 155.44.\n\nIn US dollar terms, however, the average FOB value slipped to US$5.72 per kilogram from US$5.87, highlighting the impact of currency movements on the rupee-denominated improvement. Green tea was the only category to record an increase in its dollar FOB value during the month.\n\nThe July decline also pulled down the year-to-date performance, with tea exports during the first seven months of 2026 falling 4.9 percent to 143.51 million kilograms from 150.85 million kilograms in the corresponding period of 2025.\n\nPacketed tea accounted for much of the decline, with shipments falling by 4.69 million kilograms to 63.89 million kilograms. Bulk tea exports dropped by 2.73 million kilograms to 59.92 million kilograms."
