    "md5": "36c8e80a7db28c04b684239011ebb1ab",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1582,
    "chunk_text": "WTTC projections put Sri Lanka\u2019s US$ 10bn tourism target a decade behind schedule\n\nLong-term forecast points to a US$ 3.54bn gap between organic revenue growth and govt.\u2019s 2030 target\n\nWTTC projects Sri Lanka\u2019s tourism employment will grow from 991,000 jobs in 2025 to 1.52 mn by 2036\n\nS L\u2019s total tourism GDP outpaces the Maldives and Cambodia despite trailing both on pure tourism earnings\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s travel and tourism sector will generate US$ 6.46 billion in international visitor spending by 2036, according to the World Travel & Tourism Council\u2019s (WTTC) latest long-term forecast which is a US$ 3.54 billion shortfall from government\u2019s target of US$ 10 billion in tourism revenue from five million arrivals by 2030.\n\nThe WTTC\u2019s independent projections show steady but moderate organic growth: visitor exports are estimated to rise from US$ 3.73 billion in 2025 to US$ 4.49 billion in 2026, before reaching US$ 6.46 billion in 2036. Extrapolating the WTTC\u2019s implied 2025\u20132036 growth rate forward, Sri Lanka would not reach the US$ 10 billion mark until around 2045 \u2014 some 15 years behind the government\u2019s original 2030 target. The widening gap between these baseline forecasts and the state\u2019s targets points to a structural challenge for tourism industry.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s tourism earnings fell 11.5 percent year-on-year to US$ 1.79 billion in the first seven months of 2026, per Central Bank of Sri Lanka data, leaving the industry needing to average roughly US$ 480 million a month for the rest of the year to hit the revised US$ 4.2 billion target."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-26-dailymirrorlk-807a82d4",
