    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5140a66ab02376c40e3fc5c5530ab2ac",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1636,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s tourism earnings fell 11.5 percent year-on-year to US$ 1.79 billion in the first seven months of 2026, per Central Bank of Sri Lanka data, leaving the industry needing to average roughly US$ 480 million a month for the rest of the year to hit the revised US$ 4.2 billion target.\n\nDespite the shortfall against state targets, tourism\u2019s broader economic footprint remains significant. WTTC forecasts the sector\u2019s total contribution to GDP will rise from US$ 9.92 billion in 2025 to US$ 11.12 billion in 2026, and to US$ 16.62 billion by 2036 \u2014 lifting tourism\u2019s share of whole-economy GDP from 9.3 percent to 10.3 percent over the same period.\n\nThat total GDP figure tells a different story from visitor exports alone. Even though Sri Lanka trails both the Maldives and Cambodia in pure international visitor spending by 2036, its total tourism GDP contribution \u2014 which captures indirect effects such as investment and domestic supply chains, and induced effects such as spending by tourism-sector workers, not just foreign exchange earnings \u2014 is considerably larger than either: nearly double the Maldives\u2019 US$ 5.22 billion in 2025 and about 39 percent bigger than Cambodia\u2019s US$ 7.15 billion, a gap that widens by 2036 to more than double the Maldives\u2019 US$ 7.07 billion and roughly 20 percent ahead of Cambodia\u2019s US$ 13.9 billion. The gap suggests Sri Lanka\u2019s tourism sector, while smaller in pure export terms, is better anchored in the domestic economy \u2014 plausibly because resort-heavy, import-reliant markets like the Maldives generate less downstream domestic activity per tourist dollar spent."
  },
