    "md5": "7c05b22ad5c15d30009b3e0f896a63f6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1917,
    "chunk_text": "That total GDP figure tells a different story from visitor exports alone. Even though Sri Lanka trails both the Maldives and Cambodia in pure international visitor spending by 2036, its total tourism GDP contribution \u2014 which captures indirect effects such as investment and domestic supply chains, and induced effects such as spending by tourism-sector workers, not just foreign exchange earnings \u2014 is considerably larger than either: nearly double the Maldives\u2019 US$ 5.22 billion in 2025 and about 39 percent bigger than Cambodia\u2019s US$ 7.15 billion, a gap that widens by 2036 to more than double the Maldives\u2019 US$ 7.07 billion and roughly 20 percent ahead of Cambodia\u2019s US$ 13.9 billion. The gap suggests Sri Lanka\u2019s tourism sector, while smaller in pure export terms, is better anchored in the domestic economy \u2014 plausibly because resort-heavy, import-reliant markets like the Maldives generate less downstream domestic activity per tourist dollar spent.\n\nMeeting Sri Lanka\u2019s targets and keeping pace with that momentum, however, will hinge on clearing operational bottlenecks. Guevara said potential cannot be realised without close public-private collaboration, warning that labour shortages, skills gaps and limited visa access for workforce mobility are already constraining the sector in some countries. In Sri Lanka, total travel and tourism employment is projected to grow from 991,000 jobs in 2025 to 1.52 million by 2036.\n\nSupporting that expansion \u2014 and justifying premium pricing \u2014 will require sustained investment in hospitality training, Guevara said, adding that countries treating tourism as a national strategic priority, with investment-friendly policies, stronger connectivity and reduced friction across the traveller journey, consistently gain a competitive edge. The public-private partnerships built today, she said, will define the next era of global travel and tourism."
