    "md5": "69f42060fc7db28ade508cfd76163679",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1761,
    "chunk_text": "When economic warnings become political weapons\n\nSri Lanka has been through an economic crisis that brought the country to its knees. People stood in queues for fuel, medicines became difficult to find, businesses struggled to survive and families were forced to cut back on even their most basic needs. The wounds of that crisis are still fresh.\n\nThat is precisely why politicians must understand the enormous responsibility that comes with speaking about the country\u2019s economy.\n\nFormer President Ranil Wickremesinghe last week raised concerns about Sri Lanka\u2019s ability to meet its debt obligations from 2027 and 2028 onwards. Speaking at a book launch, he warned that the country would face a major challenge in finding the necessary funds and said Sri Lanka would need to build its reserves significantly and raise billions of dollars through international bonds.\n\nThe Government was quick to reject the warning. Minister Anil Jayantha argued that foreign reserves should be viewed as an external buffer rather than the sole measure of economic progress. Minister Vijitha Herath, meanwhile, assured the diplomatic community in Colombo that the Government was confident Sri Lanka would be able to meet its debt obligations from 2028 onwards.\n\nBoth sides are entitled to their views. But the public deserves something more than competing political statements. It deserves facts.\n\nSri Lanka cannot afford to turn the question of debt repayment into another political battlefield. Even rumours and exaggerated statements can affect investor sentiment, business decisions and public confidence. When politicians speak about another possible economic crisis, they must therefore understand that their words can have consequences far beyond the political stage."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-26-dailymirrorlk-b32f9c4b",
