    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8d4a9c692e92e061564663a6bcc91caf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1903,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka cannot afford to turn the question of debt repayment into another political battlefield. Even rumours and exaggerated statements can affect investor sentiment, business decisions and public confidence. When politicians speak about another possible economic crisis, they must therefore understand that their words can have consequences far beyond the political stage.\n\nBut criticism must come with evidence. If there is a genuine risk that Sri Lanka could face a financing gap in 2027 or 2028, the public has a right to know exactly what that risk is. At the same time, if the Government has a credible debt repayment plan extending to 2030, it must publish enough information for independent economists, businesses and ordinary citizens to understand how that plan will work.\n\nSaying everything is under control is not enough. Neither is saying another crisis is around the corner.\n\nThis is where political maturity becomes important. A former President who played a central role in managing the economic crisis has every right to raise concerns about the future. But such warnings should be accompanied by clear evidence and solutions rather than leaving the public with the impression that another economic catastrophe is inevitable.\n\nLikewise, a Government that says the country is capable of meeting its obligations must be prepared to demonstrate how.\n\nThe Government has spoken about poverty reduction, agriculture, science and technology, digitalisation and sustainable development. These are all important ambitions. But Sri Lanka cannot borrow its way out of every problem. Nor can it depend indefinitely on foreign assistance.\n\nPeople want stability. They want to know that their savings are safe, that their jobs will remain secure, that prices will not suddenly explode and that the country will not once again return to the desperate conditions experienced during the last crisis."
  },
