    "md5": "31406a2400c0fed2c5ce5a3c8abde2b3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1915,
    "chunk_text": "Let\u2019s Not Bring Back The Death Penalty By  Any Means\n\nThe prison cells shown here house prisoners who are on death row at Welikada Prison which also contains the gallows used when death sentences were carried out\n\nBut, seen in the modern context, hanging (or the firing squad, lethal injection or the electric chair) can be just as barbaric\n\nTalk of reviving the death penalty is back again, and this concerns not just the 800 plus people on \u2018death row,\u2019 with sentences commuted to life imprisonment. It concerns all of us as citizens of a civilised nation.\n\nEight hundred is a high figure if we compare with the United States. The US with its population of 343 million has close to 2000 people under death sentence, only two and half times as much,\u00a0 though its population is nearly fifteen times bigger than ours. Worldwide, the figure tops 25,000.\n\nBut most countries have now abolished the death penalty. The majority of the 193 UN member states have either fully abolished capital punishment or maintain a legal moratorium against executions, while around 55 UN member countries still practise legal executions.\n\nNo country can be called uncivilised for carrying out the death penalty. But there has to be a soul-searching debate regarding this much abhorred practise in any country which considers itself civilised.\n\nExecuting people for various crimes was standard practise all over the world until a few centuries ago, and people were killed in horrible ways. Venezuela is recognised as the first country to abolish the death penalty by presidential decree in 1863, the first country to do so. In 1786, Grand Duke Peter Leopold of Tuscany issued a penal reform ending the death penalty locally, but Tuscany is not an independent country. In 1849,\u00a0 San Marino abolished the death penalty for common crimes, (fully in 1865), though it was actually\u00a0 suspended there\u00a0 since 1468, hence not carried out."
  },
