    "chunk_size_bytes": 1508,
    "chunk_text": "Executing people for various crimes was standard practise all over the world until a few centuries ago, and people were killed in horrible ways. Venezuela is recognised as the first country to abolish the death penalty by presidential decree in 1863, the first country to do so. In 1786, Grand Duke Peter Leopold of Tuscany issued a penal reform ending the death penalty locally, but Tuscany is not an independent country. In 1849,\u00a0 San Marino abolished the death penalty for common crimes, (fully in 1865), though it was actually\u00a0 suspended there\u00a0 since 1468, hence not carried out.\n\nInfluenced by Buddhist teachings and imperial policy, ancient Japan officially suspended capital punishment in 810 AD,\u00a0 and this lasted nearly 350 years. Unfortunately, modern Japan continues to exercise the death penalty, resuming executions last year after nearly three years. On June 27, 2025,\u00a0 Takahiro Shiraishi, widely known as the \u201cTwitter Killer,\u201d was hanged for the 2017 murder, dismemberment, and sexual abuse of nine people he met online.\n\nThis marked the end of a 2-year, 11-month hiatus \u2013 the longest pause since Japan\u2019s Ministry of Justice began publicly disclosing the names and crimes of death row inmates in 2007. This resumption is unfortunate,\u00a0 since\u00a0 89-year-old Hakamada Iwao \u2013 once the world\u2019s longest-serving death row inmate who spent\u00a0 nearly 50 years in prison --\u00a0 was finally aquitted last year. After spending years in solitary confinement, Iawo had lost the power of speech."
  },
