    "md5": "2bd676b3c74b7ff6542189504efba227",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1785,
    "chunk_text": "This marked the end of a 2-year, 11-month hiatus \u2013 the longest pause since Japan\u2019s Ministry of Justice began publicly disclosing the names and crimes of death row inmates in 2007. This resumption is unfortunate,\u00a0 since\u00a0 89-year-old Hakamada Iwao \u2013 once the world\u2019s longest-serving death row inmate who spent\u00a0 nearly 50 years in prison --\u00a0 was finally aquitted last year. After spending years in solitary confinement, Iawo had lost the power of speech.\n\nIn China, convicts are executed by firing squad or lethal injection. In Iran, Iraq and Somalia, it is by hanging or firing squad. In Saudi Arabia, the legal method of execution is hanging, though beheading is often carried out.\u00a0 In Afghanistan under the Taleban, execution is by firing squad and is carried out publicly.\n\nIn the US. 23 states have abolished the death penalty, while 27 states have capital punishment laws. But four of these \u2013 California, Ohio, Oregon, and Pennsylvania \u2013 do not actively carry out death warrants. The common method of execution is lethal injection, using a traditional multi-drug cocktail or a single massive dose of a powerful sedative. But there is also Nitrogen Hypoxia, forcing the victim to breathe pure nitrogen through a mask, causing suffocation. Electrocution is the third method used.\n\nUntil a few centuries ago, barbaric methods of torture were used to execute people all over the world. It is hard to say just when death by hanging became widely accepted almost everywhere as a more \u2018civilised\u2019 method of execution. But we inherited this practise from the British (though the Dutch used this method before them). Ancient Sri Lanka used beheading and often barbaric methods of execution. As far as we know, death by hanging wasn\u2019t standard practise under the kings."
  },
