    "md5": "a67da4518d5795b51020e7232e59fd1e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1915,
    "chunk_text": "Until a few centuries ago, barbaric methods of torture were used to execute people all over the world. It is hard to say just when death by hanging became widely accepted almost everywhere as a more \u2018civilised\u2019 method of execution. But we inherited this practise from the British (though the Dutch used this method before them). Ancient Sri Lanka used beheading and often barbaric methods of execution. As far as we know, death by hanging wasn\u2019t standard practise under the kings.\n\nNo one knows exactly. The victim is not there to recount his or her ordeal. Executions are justified given the heinous nature of crimes committed. Some are serial killers. But definitions of crime can be stretched in infinite ways, especially by authoritarian governments. Hence, millions\u00a0 of political activists, opponents, prisoners of war, minority group members and those deemed enemies of the state for very biased reasons have been executed in the 20th century alone, and this has continued unabated to this day (by the Islamic State, and in some of the non-democratic countries listed above).\n\nIt is a very humane gesture on the part of governments\u00a0 not to take away the life of even the most disgusting criminals. For such people, life imprisonment in less than ideal conditions would be a worse punishment than death. Mexico, for example, prefers to imprison drug traffickers instead of executing them. Mexico abolished the death penalty for civilian crime in 1935, and imposed a total ban in Article 22 of the Mexican constitution.\n\nThen there are the miscarriages of justice. A statistical study published in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences (PNAS) determined that at least 4.1% of all defendants sentenced to death in the United States are actually innocent. This happens all over the world. What are the final thoughts of any person facing execution for a crime he or she didn\u2019t commit like?"
