    "md5": "d770f021430ab6f3da9b25488b1b0d22",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1592,
    "chunk_text": "Drought preparedness must begin before crisis, Sri Lanka tells UNCCD COP17\n\nSri Lanka has highlighted the need to strengthen drought preparedness and resilience before drought conditions develop into a crisis, calling for increased international support for developing countries.\n\nEnvironment Minister Dr. Dammika Patabendi made the call while participating in a High-Level Ministerial Dialogue on \u201cWater and Drought Resilience\u201d at the UNCCD COP17 in Ulaanbaatar, Mongolia, yesterday (25).\n\nHe said developing countries require greater access to climate information, early-warning systems, technology, capacity development and adequate and predictable financial resources to prepare for increasing drought risks.\n\nThe Minister emphasized that climate change is increasing the need for countries to strengthen long-term preparedness and resilience rather than responding only after droughts have become severe.\n\nHe reiterated Sri Lanka\u2019s commitment to strengthening land degradation neutrality, sustainable land management and drought resilience through a people-centred and collaborative approach.\n\nMinister Patabendi also highlighted Sri Lanka\u2019s integrated efforts to address land, water, climate and biodiversity challenges, including the restoration of traditional tank villages, the \u201cVanaspathi\u201d national forest conservation programme and the protection of wetlands.\n\nHe reaffirmed Sri Lanka\u2019s willingness to work with the UNCCD, regional partners and the wider international community to strengthen global efforts on land restoration, water security and drought resilience."
