    "md5": "cf00cc0598b2f70b6b2ddc871dd5df77",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1801,
    "chunk_text": "Until recently - and on the basis of the MoU - Iran had been involved in negotiations with Oman, a US ally, to agree a mechanism for administering the strait. However, those talks have so far not produced an agreement and, last week, Trump even threatened to bomb its ally \"if it gets in the way\" of his administration's own talks with Iran over the issue.\n\nHe said in a statement he also hoped for \"practical arrangements\" to restore safe navigation, adding: \"Future management of the strait and a permanent solution will follow in due course.\"\n\nAraghchi confirmed the meeting in a statement, saying \"regional solutions\" were discussed in talks with representatives from Pakistan and Oman.\n\nHe added that discussions covered the future management of the strait, as well as the framework for a new corridor and a joint mine-clearing operation.\n\nSpeaking to state television news after the meeting Iran's Deputy Foreign Minister Kazem Gharibabad said the entry route to the Gulf would be \"through Iranian waters and the exit route through Omani territorial waters, and ships will pass through Iranian waters on both routes\".\n\nThe minister added that the south corridor of the waterway, which has been recommended to shipping firms by the US and is situated close to the coast of Oman, would be \"closed\" under the framework, and the meeting \"does not mean that the Strait will be opened from tomorrow\".\n\nHe said \"no military vessels will be allowed to pass through the Strait of Hormuz, and only commercial vessels will be allowed to pass through it\".\n\nGharibabad called the route \"temporary\", adding Iran and Oman will negotiate a new permanent route within a timeframe of 60 days to replace the previous maritime traffic plan.\n\n\"If Iran's requests are not granted, the Strait will be closed,\" he said."
