    "md5": "5d9a3cdef5deb9e7348991c8eed418e1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1331,
    "chunk_text": "Nuwara Eliya records lowest minimum temperature at 14.6\u00b0C\n\nColombo, Aug. 26 (Daily Mirror) - Nuwara Eliya recorded Sri Lanka\u2019s lowest minimum temperature at 14.6\u00b0C on today (26), the Department of Meteorology\u2019s daily temperature report said.\n\nThe central highlands experienced significantly cooler conditions compared to other parts of the country, with Bandarawela recording 19.4\u00b0C and Badulla 21.7\u00b0C. Katugastota recorded a minimum temperature of 22.8\u00b0C.\n\nIn contrast, warmer nighttime temperatures were recorded across several northern, eastern and coastal areas.\n\nPolonnaruwa recorded the island\u2019s highest minimum temperature at 27.5\u00b0C. Jaffna, Mullaitivu and Puttalam each recorded 27.2\u00b0C, while Trincomalee and Rathmalana recorded 27.1\u00b0C. Mannar recorded 27.0\u00b0C.\n\nColombo recorded a minimum temperature of 26.8\u00b0C, while Anuradhapura recorded 26.7\u00b0C and Batticaloa 26.5\u00b0C. Vavuniya and Katunayake recorded 26.4\u00b0C and 26.2\u00b0C respectively.\n\nOther recorded minimum temperatures included 25.6\u00b0C in Pothuvil, 25.4\u00b0C in Mattala, 25.2\u00b0C in Maha Illuppallama, 25.1\u00b0C in Monaragala, 24.9\u00b0C in Galle, 24.8\u00b0C in Hambantota, 24.5\u00b0C in Kurunegala and 24.4\u00b0C in Ratnapura.\n\nThe figures highlight the marked temperature difference between Sri Lanka\u2019s cool central highlands and warmer lowland and coastal areas."
  },
