    "chunk_text": "\u201cSri Lanka can be recognised as one of the countries that experienced a severe economic and balance-of-payments crisis and subsequently achieved one of the fastest recoveries,\u201d he said.\n\nA sharp improvement in government finances has been central to the turnaround.\n\nDescribing the increase as a \u201cremarkable achievement,\u201d he said the Government had been able to maintain fiscal discipline through a combination of increased revenue mobilisation, expenditure control and improvements in fiscal balances, alongside measures aimed at ensuring debt sustainability.\n\nThe fiscal adjustment has been a critical part of Sri Lanka\u2019s economic reform programme following the 2022 crisis, when weak government revenue, large fiscal deficits, depleted foreign reserves and an unsustainable debt burden converged to leave the country unable to meet its external debt obligations.\n\nAccordingly, the authorities now see the economy moving beyond the immediate stabilisation phase, although maintaining the gains will depend on continued fiscal discipline and the durability of reforms.\n\nWeerasinghe also said Sri Lanka could be identified as one of the countries that experienced a severe economic and balance-of-payments crisis.\n\nHe said the Government\u2019s revenue-to-GDP ratio had increased by almost 100 percent within three years, reversing a period in which State revenue had fallen to exceptionally low levels and contributed to widening fiscal imbalances.\n\nSri Lanka is also seeking to translate the improvement in its macroeconomic position into stronger sovereign creditworthiness.\n\nWeerasinghe said the authorities were currently holding discussions with all three major international credit-rating agencies over Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereign rating.\n\nThe country had previously been placed in the restricted-default category following the suspension of external debt repayments during the crisis. It has since moved out of that category and into speculative-grade territory following progress in restructuring its debt."
