    "md5": "c18704f986bcbf3949a31d8c0d2f3e9b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1483,
    "chunk_text": "Prophet Muhammad\u2019s teachings offer strength to fight drugs and build a just society: President\n\nColombo, Aug. 26 (Daily Mirror) - Claiming that Islam rejects all forms of vice that corrupt both the inner purity of the individual and the moral fabric of society, President Anura Kumara Dissanayake said the teachings and example of the Holy Prophet Muhammad provide immense strength to programmes aimed at uplifting the moral fabric of society, such as the national campaign launched by the Government to combat the scourge of illicit drugs, which is spreading like a blight across the country.\n\nThe President made these remarks in a message issued to mark Milad-un-Nabi.\n\n\"As the month of Rabi' al-Awwal dawns in the Islamic lunar calendar, Muslims across the world today (26) commemorate Milad-un-Nabi, the birth of the Holy Prophet Muhammad, with deep devotion and reverence, placing sacrifice at the forefront. With the aim of creating a society founded on equality, this is a commemoration that seeks a profound spiritual transformation that goes beyond a mere celebration.\n\nThe Holy Prophet Muhammad was one of the greatest teachers ever sent to humanity, illuminating the path of boundless patience and compassion to lead people from darkness to light. The purpose of the many hardships he endured and the unparalleled sacrifices he made over a period of 23 years was to build a virtuous society in which peace, brotherhood, purity, trustworthiness and justice would prevail."
  },
