    "md5": "d1a4bb4787e5151c1567b92cc99c58f7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1860,
    "chunk_text": "El Nino: Health Sector bracing for weather related diseases\n\nColombo, Aug. 26 (Daily Mirror) - With the El Ni\u00f1o weather phenomena set to trigger rainfall higher than normal, to be followed by a dry spell, the Health Ministry is gearing up to deal with a possible rise in climate-related diseases, an official said yesterday.\n\nThe Environment and Occupational Health Unit of the Health Ministry is working in this regard at the moment.\n\nAccording to Consultant Community Physician of the division, Dr. Inoka Suraweera, the health authorities are liaising with the Meteorology Department and the Disaster Management Centre on the developments. She said regular public awareness regarding heat-related diseases was being carried out in engagement with the media as an initial step.\n\n\u201cOn top of that, we are carrying out capacity-building programmes for health workers, particularly clinicians at the divisional level, regarding heat-related diseases. Actually, we are on board,\u201d she told the Daily Mirror.\n\nReferring to the expected high rainfall next, she said that the health authorities were bracing for possible weather events such as floods that could lead to diseases.\n\nBesides, she said that the health authorities were in discussions with the Asian Development Bank (ADB) to implement a National Heat Health Action Plan through grant assistance.\n\n\u201cWe began this discussion even before the El Ni\u00f1o warning,\u201d she said.\n\nShe admitted that attention was being paid in this regard with the rise in temperature as a result of global warming.\n\nIn addition, she said there was another programme called \u2018Heat and Workers\u2019 Health\u2019 to be implemented with the assistance of the International Labour Organization (ILO).\n\nThe programme is for the health benefits of people such as agricultural and construction workers who are exposed to excessive heat."
  },
