    "chunk_text": "Bus driver jailed for 13 years for high-speed crash that killed seven\n\nColombo, Aug. 26 (Daily Mirror) - The Court of Appeal has dismissed an appeal filed by a private bus driver and affirmed his conviction and 13-year-and-three-month rigorous imprisonment sentence for causing the deaths of seven people and injuries to dozens of others in a fatal road accident near Chilaw in 2017.\n\nThe Court observed that the bus was travelling at an excessive speed estimated at between 80 km/h and 120 km/h, swaying from side to side, overtaking dangerously and racing another bus. It subsequently collided with the rear of a three-wheeler travelling on the left side of the road, swerved across the road, struck a bridge or culvert and overturned.\n\nA two-judge bench comprising Justices Amal Ranaraja and Dr. Sumudu Premachandra delivered the judgment yesterday, concluding that the sentence imposed by the High Court of Puttalam was lawful and neither excessive nor disproportionate to the offences committed.\n\nThis appeal relates to a motor traffic accident on November 6, 2017, near the Mundalama Bridge on the A-3 road. According to the prosecution evidence, the accused, Mohamed Jifry Mohamed Wahil, was driving a crowded passenger bus travelling from Colombo towards Jaffna when he engaged in a series of highly reckless manoeuvres.\n\nSeven people died in the accident, while 21 suffered grievous injuries and 24 others sustained minor injuries.\n\nJustice Dr. Premachandra observed that reckless and negligent driving by public transport operators was a common occurrence, particularly on inter-provincial routes, where speeding, dangerous overtaking and racing between private buses and Sri Lanka Transport Board buses could endanger passengers and pedestrians.\n\nThe prosecution case was supported by several eyewitnesses, including passengers and other road users. The Court observed that witnesses testified to the bus travelling at a terrifying speed and swaying dangerously before the collision."
