    "chunk_size_bytes": 1465,
    "chunk_text": "ANPD highlighted concerns about TikTok's \"logged-out feed,\" which allows users to browse content without creating an account, enabling widespread use \u200cof the \u2060platform by children and teenagers in Brazil without effective age-verification checks.\n\nThe feature, the regulator said, is available in Brazil but not in the U.S. or Europe, where access is restricted to registered users.\n\nTikTok said in a statement the fine relates to conduct dating back to 2021 \u200band does not take into \u200baccount a compliance plan approved \u2060by ANPD in 2025, as well as additional safeguards introduced since then.\n\nIt added the measures comply with Brazil's data protection law for both logged-in and logged-out users and will continue to engage with \u200bthe regulator as it evaluates possible legal steps.\n\nANPD Director Coutinho told reporters that ByteDance had committed \u200bto implementing a plan \u2060aimed at bringing TikTok into compliance with Brazilian law.\n\nAccording to Tuesday's decision, ByteDance must delete the personal data of teenagers registered on the platform if the necessary parent, guardian, or other legal support arrangements are not provided and documented within 60 days.\n\nThe company faces fines of 137,081.49 \u2060reais \u200bper day if it fails to comply with the data-deletion, reporting, or notification requirements. \u200bThe total fine, the regulator added, could be reduced by 25% if ByteDance waives its right to appeal and swiftly pays the penalty."
  },
