    "chunk_text": "The Nepal government initiated rescue operations in flood-hit areas, but helicopters were unable to land in places where the floodwater levels are high. Chinese President Xi Jinping called for an all-out search for those missing in Tibet and stronger monitoring and early-warning systems to prevent secondary disasters.\n\nSecurity footage from the Chinese side of the Nepal-Tibet border captured people fleeing moments before a massive torrent of rocks, mud and water tore through the area, destroying buildings and vehicles and claiming several lives.\n\nThe exact cause of the mudslide remained uncertain. However, the German Research Centre for Geosciences (GFZ) reported that a 4.4-magnitude earthquake struck around seven minutes before the timestamp visible on the security footage.\n\nThe avalanche is believed to have unleashed a massive surge of water and debris downstream, devastating villages, roads, bridges and hydropower infrastructure.\n\nRasuwa Chief District Officer Narendra Pariyar said authorities had received reports of villages and infrastructure project sites being washed away, although the scale of the damage was not immediately clear, Reuters reported.\n\n\u201cThere could be heavy casualties or loss of property,\u201d Pariyar said.\n\nTimure Bazaar, a market near the Tibet border, and the nearby customs area were extensively damaged, with vehicles and goods swept away, according to local officials. A newly constructed Friendship Bridge at Rasuwagadhi, which had been rebuilt after last year's deadly flood, was also washed away.\n\nThree Nepal Police facilities were damaged \u2013 the Area Police Office in Timure, the Police Post in Syaphrubesi and the Police Post at Rasuwagadhi \u2013 according to Nepal Police. Nine Armed Police Force personnel deployed in Timure near the Tibet border were reported missing, The Kathmandu Post reported."
