    "md5": "1d4d5ce02f0c343a567698cbbe27cafb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1683,
    "chunk_text": "Three Nepal Police facilities were damaged \u2013 the Area Police Office in Timure, the Police Post in Syaphrubesi and the Police Post at Rasuwagadhi \u2013 according to Nepal Police. Nine Armed Police Force personnel deployed in Timure near the Tibet border were reported missing, The Kathmandu Post reported.\n\nThe flood from Rasuwa Bhote Koshi has reached Narayani Devghat and water levels are continuing to rise. Authorities have urged residents in settlements and market areas along the river to remain on high alert and move to safer locations.\n\nNepal Prime Minister Balendra Shah chaired an emergency meeting of the Council of Ministers to review the flooding, damage and rescue efforts. Police, the Nepal Army and the Armed Police Force have been deployed for search and rescue operations, while two Army helicopters were sent towards the affected areas, according to The Kathmandu Post.\n\nHowever, rescue operations have been hampered by the conditions on the ground. Water levels were too high for helicopters to land in Syapru Besi and Timure, Army spokesperson Raja Ram Basnet said.\n\nShah said authorities had instructed officials to move people living in lower-lying areas to safer locations. \u201cPeople along the river are advised to exercise extra caution and remain alert,\u201d he said.\n\nThe flooding moved downstream into the Trishuli river basin, prompting warnings for Nuwakot, Dhading, Gorkha and Chitwan. Traffic from Kathmandu towards Rasuwa, Nuwakot, Chitwan and Dhading was suspended amid the flood threat, The Kathmandu Post reported.\n\nNepal Home Minister Sudan Gurung also appealed to residents along the Bhote Koshi and Trishuli riverbanks to evacuate to safer areas."
