    "md5": "f183485f1d8c7229f0a8d96a8f7fb3ec",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1850,
    "chunk_text": "The flooding moved downstream into the Trishuli river basin, prompting warnings for Nuwakot, Dhading, Gorkha and Chitwan. Traffic from Kathmandu towards Rasuwa, Nuwakot, Chitwan and Dhading was suspended amid the flood threat, The Kathmandu Post reported.\n\nNepal Home Minister Sudan Gurung also appealed to residents along the Bhote Koshi and Trishuli riverbanks to evacuate to safer areas.\n\nThat disaster was later linked to the draining of a supraglacial lake in Tibet, according to a regional climate monitor.\n\nAcross the Nepal border, a mudslide struck China's Gyirong county and the Gyirong port, cutting off roads, communications and power in parts of the Shigatse region, Xinhua reported. The agency said there were major casualties and missing people in the area.\n\nThe Chinese Embassy in Nepal also activated its emergency response mechanism, saying it was assessing the situation of Chinese citizens and enterprises in the affected areas and urging Nepal's authorities to intensify rescue efforts.\n\nFormer Nepal Prime Minister Sher Bahadur Deuba urged residents living around the river Trishuli to remain vigilant and called on security agencies, political parties and civil society groups to assist in rescue operations.\n\nBihar has been put on alert after heavy rain and devastating floods in Nepal raised concerns over rising water levels in rivers flowing into the state. Six districts along the Kosi, Gandak and Ganga have been placed under special vigilance.\n\nHome Minister Amit Shah spoke to Bihar Chief Minister Samrat Choudhary to take stock of the situation. The Centre is closely monitoring developments, while NDRF teams have been sent to affected areas.\n\nRelief and rescue agencies have been asked to remain prepared. The Centre and Bihar government are maintaining close coordination to respond to any worsening flood situation."
