    "md5": "778bcb824f0519f9bbaf51edc60cb7b5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1672,
    "chunk_text": "Home Minister Amit Shah spoke to Bihar Chief Minister Samrat Choudhary to take stock of the situation. The Centre is closely monitoring developments, while NDRF teams have been sent to affected areas.\n\nRelief and rescue agencies have been asked to remain prepared. The Centre and Bihar government are maintaining close coordination to respond to any worsening flood situation.\n\nQianggong Zhang, head of climate and environmental risks at the Kathmandu-based International Centre for Integrated Mountain Development, said an ice-rock avalanche in the Lhende Khola river on the Nepali side may have triggered the disaster.\n\nHowever, the cause of the avalanche remains unconfirmed. Zhang cautioned that the threat could persist, with a blockage still lodged upstream along the Nepal-China border.\n\nAuthorities have warned that another major flood could occur if the blockage suddenly collapses or is swept away.\n\nMeanwhile, Prime Minister Narendra Modi spoke with Nepal PM Balendra Shah, expressing condolences over the deaths caused by devastating floods.\n\nPM Modi assured Shah of India\u2019s full support and offered all possible humanitarian and relief assistance. India is closely monitoring the situation and remains in touch with Nepal as rescue operations continue, he said.\n\nWith floodwaters continuing downstream and rescue teams struggling to reach some of the worst-hit areas, authorities across Nepal, Tibet, China and Bihar remained on high alert.\n\nExtending a helping hand to its neighbour, India is going to dispatch relief material to Nepal with two flights, one scheduled to leave later tonight and another one early tomorrow morning, sources told India Today."
