    "md5": "06bbc642eddaee401bdd5cd7e13c2a2c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1937,
    "chunk_text": "Are Sri Lanka\u2019s wildlife laws enough to stop the destruction of medicinal plants?\n\nSri Lanka is preparing to mark World Wildlife Day 2026 amid growing concerns over the exploitation of medicinal and aromatic plants, raising questions over whether existing laws and enforcement are strong enough to prevent the country\u2019s valuable biodiversity from being depleted.\n\nThis year\u2019s World Wildlife Day theme, \u201cMedicinal and Aromatic Plants: Conserving Health, Heritage and Livelihoods,\u201d highlights the increasing threat faced by species that are commercially exploited for medicinal and cosmetic products.\n\nThe Department of Wildlife Conservation says millions of organisms are used to meet human demand, while several medicinal and aromatic species in Sri Lanka are increasingly threatened because of their high commercial value and excessive use.\n\nThe issue raises a difficult question: If Sri Lanka already has a strong legal framework for protecting wildlife, why are threatened species continuing to face pressure from commercial exploitation and illegal international trade?\n\nSri Lanka became a party to the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) in 1978, with the Department of Wildlife Conservation responsible for implementing the convention.\n\nWildlife officers conduct raids and enforce the Fauna and Flora Protection Ordinance to prevent the illegal exploitation of protected species. However, the continued international demand for valuable wildlife resources makes enforcement a constant challenge.\n\nThe concerns extend beyond animals. Rare and threatened plants are also vulnerable to exploitation, particularly when their commercial value increases.\n\nConservationists and authorities therefore face the challenge of balancing human demand for traditional medicines and other products with the need to ensure that valuable plant species are not driven towards extinction."
