    "md5": "eea2f1c20cbd815866266a5b0d9e0f28",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1073,
    "chunk_text": "Conservationists and authorities therefore face the challenge of balancing human demand for traditional medicines and other products with the need to ensure that valuable plant species are not driven towards extinction.\n\nSri Lanka will mark World Wildlife Day on March 3 at the Lunugamvehera area of Yala National Park. The event will include awards for winners of wildlife-related school and open competitions, recognition of the best tourist bungalow in a national park and the declaration of the Thalawila coral reef area as the \u201cThalawila\u2013Kalpitiya Sanctuary.\u201d\n\nThe national celebration will be held under the patronage of Environment Minister Dr. Dammika Patabendi, with Professor Sagarika Kannangara of the University of Kelaniya delivering the keynote address on \u201cWildlife Conservation Ensures Human Survival.\u201d\n\nBut as Sri Lanka celebrates its rich biodiversity, the central question remains: Can the country protect its wildlife and medicinal plant heritage from growing commercial pressure before some species become too valuable\u2014or too rare\u2014to save?"
