    "md5": "ccd9cb8cb172b90f33628a15d24fbe74",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1883,
    "chunk_text": "Squabbling over judges amid starvation and indebtedness\n\nAccording to Numbeo, a user-generated cost-of-living statistics website, Sri Lanka is the second most expensive country in South Asia to live in\n\nWith problems faced by the people increasing, the government\u2019s popularity today is at a new low. The ruling party too,\u00a0 has seen \u2018the writing on the wall.\u2019\n\nHowever, let\u2019s get back to our tale of woes. As of\u00a0 January 1, 2026,\u00a0 \u2018the national minimum wage in Sri Lanka has been LKR 30,000/- per month and LKR 1,200/- per day.\n\nOur people are hungry. Our people are angry. Our people have too little cash to clothe themselves with, and our wages are inadequate to provide a family of four (father, mother and two children) with two square meals a day. Worse. the price of fuel is on the rise, and there is little likelihood of wages rising to meet the cost of living.\n\nOn the plus side,\u00a0 the government has been largely successful in bringing to book politicians and bureaucrats involved in corruption. Former Ministers, deputy ministers and a variety of local government politicians have been charged with fraud and corruption. They are in the process of being brought to justice. Among them were former President Mahinda Rajapaksa\u2019s younger son, a former Minister of Health, Deputy Ministers and a variety of local and provincial council officials.\n\nThe Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption (CIABOC) has not released a single cumulative census explicitly combining all past and present figures of former ministers, local politicians, and bureaucrats under one total count. However, enforcement data shows that 67 public officials have been arrested on bribery and corruption charges in 2026 alone, alongside active grand-scale probes into approx. 300 major complaints targeting former high-ranking officials and their political affiliates."
  },
