    "md5": "c18d2d497125a147e4c0618f4df3672a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1957,
    "chunk_text": "The Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption (CIABOC) has not released a single cumulative census explicitly combining all past and present figures of former ministers, local politicians, and bureaucrats under one total count. However, enforcement data shows that 67 public officials have been arrested on bribery and corruption charges in 2026 alone, alongside active grand-scale probes into approx. 300 major complaints targeting former high-ranking officials and their political affiliates.\n\nWith a basic family comprising around four persons (father, mother and 2 children) an average family needs around Rs. 60,000/- per month, to merely provide two nutritious meals per day, per family alone! Even if both father and mother work, their total monthly income averages Rs. 60,000/-, a sum just sufficient for two daily meals.\n\nIn other words, parents are often short of sufficient funds for children\u2019s education, health, clothes, medical or travel needs. This has led to malnutrition among children and anemia among women. UNICEF reports 17.3% of children in our country under five suffer from stunting, with rates reaching up to 32% in tea estate areas. While anemia affects approximately 8.5% to 18.5% of women of reproductive age in this land.\n\nAdditionally we have a massive external debt hanging over our heads. Former President Wickremesinghe, warns Lanka could fall into a situation of bankruptcy once again if we are unable to repay foreign creditors in a timely fashion.\n\nOur main source of foreign exchange earnings is tied to the countries of West Asia which bring in our highest share of foreign exchange earnings via expatriate workers. Expatriate workers in West Asia provide roughly 50% of Sri Lanka\u2019s total worker remittances. Total annual remittances hit a record US$ 8.076 billion in 2025, the combined estimated earnings sent back from the Middle East amounted to approximately US$ 4.04 billion for that year."
  },
