    "md5": "e655a8d00182002a5a47aa774d4eedb7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1654,
    "chunk_text": "Our main source of foreign exchange earnings is tied to the countries of West Asia which bring in our highest share of foreign exchange earnings via expatriate workers. Expatriate workers in West Asia provide roughly 50% of Sri Lanka\u2019s total worker remittances. Total annual remittances hit a record US$ 8.076 billion in 2025, the combined estimated earnings sent back from the Middle East amounted to approximately US$ 4.04 billion for that year.\n\nIf the war in Iran expands to other countries in West Asia,\u00a0 it could easily create a situation similar to that which we faced during the COVID epidemic. In turn it could lead to the West Asian job market closing down. In the event of the West Asian does market close down, it will lead to an exodus of expatriate workers from the region and we could once again face a financial crisis and leave the country facing bankruptcy.\n\nWe have still not learnt lessons from the time the COVID epidemic struck. The outbreak of the epidemic led to the repatriation of over a million of our expatriate workers and a loss of a major source of foreign earnings. Neither the government nor the political Opposition have made any move to seek new sources of income.\n\nWe still remain dependent on remittances of expatriate workers to meet costs of imports as well as to repay our external debt by 2028. Government\u2019s failure to fulfill many of its post-election promises has taken toll of its popularity among the masses. Today we are beginning to hear even drivers of three-wheeled vehicles (among the government\u2019s main propagandists earlier) now berating the President and his men in the language of Billingsgate."
  },
