    "chunk_id": "2026-08-27-dailymirrorlk-67e92294-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "df397a425fc7ec3772572ae809029a18",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1645,
    "chunk_text": "Returning refugees and challenges ahead for the govt.\n\nThe letter noted that around 89,000 Sri Lankan Tamils continue to reside in Tamil Nadu, both inside refugee camps and outside.\n\nSri Lankan Tamils who fled to Tamil Nadu during the height of the armed conflict have been living in isolated conditions for many years. In May 2025, a 75-year-old Tamil refugee was arrested as he returned to the island at Palaly airport, despite being recognised by the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR) and obtaining all necessary clearance. This incident raised concerns regarding Sri Lanka\u2019s sensitivity towards the refugee repatriation process.\n\nThe issue of repatriating Tamil refugees has been brought to the attention of successive governments despite having no response.\n\nFollowing a cabinet meeting held earlier this month, it was decided that the government would facilitate clearance of immigration during the voluntary repatriation of individuals who had left the country without using an approved port and a formal passport during the period of the war, sought refuge in India, but proved the nationality as Sri Lankan.\n\nThe United Nations and many international organisations welcomed this decision, recognising it as a \u2018key step towards a safe and dignified return after years of displacement.\u2019\n\nIn February this year, just two months prior to the Tamil Nadu Assembly elections, Chief Minister M. K. Stalin wrote a letter to Prime Minister Narendra Modi seeking a \u201chumane and pragmatic\u201d resolution to the long-pending citizenship issue and legal status of Sri Lankan Tamils living in the state for over four decades."
  },
