    "md5": "0c230afa7b894e322f3128a4b46b49dd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1754,
    "chunk_text": "In February this year, just two months prior to the Tamil Nadu Assembly elections, Chief Minister M. K. Stalin wrote a letter to Prime Minister Narendra Modi seeking a \u201chumane and pragmatic\u201d resolution to the long-pending citizenship issue and legal status of Sri Lankan Tamils living in the state for over four decades.\n\nA survey conducted by the State Commissionerate of Rehabilitation and Welfare of Non-Resident Tamils revealed that 130 individuals born before July 1, 1987 were eligible to apply for Indian passports.\n\nIn May 2025, the Supreme Court of India dismissed a petition from a Tamil refugee and former LTTE member who sought permission to remain in the country with his family, in a ruling that underscores the precarious legal status of Tamil refugees in the country. A bench comprising Justice Dipankar Dutta and K. Vinod Chandran ruled that \u201cIndia is not a dharamshala to host refugees from all over the world and that the country is struggling with a 140 crore population.\u201d\n\nReturning to their motherland is not an easy process. Recent policy changes require refugees to undergo mandatory screening by the State Intelligence Service rather than regular police checks. Returnees accused of treason, murder or other wrongdoings would be detained upon their arrival. Children born in Indian refugee camps face complex citizenship processing hurdles.\n\nRepatriating Tamil refugees is indeed a welcome move. They have lived in exile their entire life and they have a right to return to their motherland. But it has to be done with a strategy. They shouldn\u2019t face a plight similar to that of Malaiyaha Tamils. They were brought down as indentured labour,\u00a0 but it took them decades to get an identity of their own and voting rights."
  },
