    "date_str": "2026-08-27",
    "description": "El Ni\u00f1o preparedness: Don\u2019t leave out  the poor",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/El-Ni\u00f1o-preparedness-Dont-leave-out-the-poor/172-348954",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787813325.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-27-dailymirrorlk-12aac74d-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5a0eb6758f0e4d6b451144ee8744cf47",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1101,
    "chunk_text": "El Ni\u00f1o preparedness: Don\u2019t leave out  the poor\n\nPeople in low income settlements who are at the receiving end of many hardships need to be looked after during a crisis such as El Ni\u00f1o. Even though the El Ni\u00f1o has not yet been established,\u00a0 it is important to ensure that these people have continued access to fresh drinking water for instance. With Sri Lanka\u2019s reservoirs becoming vulnerable to dry spells it is important to consider the plight of urban populations as well. Many low income settlements in Colombo for instance depend on pipe-borne water. But if the water board decides to limit the distribution of water during a prolonged dry spell, these people will also be affected. On the other hand,\u00a0 they live in dire conditions with no water storage facilities. Regular water distribution is required to ensure that these people are safe. It is not only for drinking purposes that people use water. They need water to do cooking, cleaning, washing and to use the toilet. Therefore the government needs to consider these people when drawing plans to prepare for an impending El Ni\u00f1o."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-27-dailymirrorlk-3c26cc20",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-3c26cc20",
    "date_str": "2026-08-27",
    "description": "Is Nanda Malini to blame for the 1988-89 bloodbath?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Is-Nanda-Malini-to-blame-for-the-1988-89-bloodbath/172-348955",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787813434.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-27-dailymirrorlk-3c26cc20-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f9087ecaa73a01ccb6009fe1e4360fbc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1493,
    "chunk_text": "Is Nanda Malini to blame for the 1988-89 bloodbath?\n\nThey called her the unmatched Sri Lankan nightingale. Some affectionately called her the \u201cQueen of Song\u201d or the \u201cVoice of the Nation.\u201d Her voice was hailed as\u00a0 supremely melodious. She was praised as \u201ca national treasure and a gift to this land.\u201d\n\nNational newspapers printed many full-page articles about her when she passed away on August 20. Electronic media aired numerous programmes highlighting her voice, talent, and artistic excellence. They also focused on the courage she showed by using her music to fight social injustice. Together, these stories are a clear testimony to the love and respect people had for legendary Sri Lankan singer Nanda Malini.\n\nNanda Malini started to address poverty, systemic failures and social injustice through her powerful lyrics\n\nHowever, not all those who listened to her songs celebrated her artistry or beauty of her songs. There was a very small group of people who were not prepared to value her contribution to Sri Lankan music, appreciate her courage to stand against injustice, or even respect her as an artiste.\n\nThe rationale behind their aversion towards Nanda Malini is purely political, born out of her protest songs against injustice that led critics to launch a scathing attack on her. Correspondingly, they want such arguments to justify especially the macabre bloodbath orchestrated by the state that claimed over 60,000 lives towards the end of the nineteen eighties."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-27-dailymirrorlk-3c26cc20",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-3c26cc20",
    "date_str": "2026-08-27",
    "description": "Is Nanda Malini to blame for the 1988-89 bloodbath?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Is-Nanda-Malini-to-blame-for-the-1988-89-bloodbath/172-348955",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787813434.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-27-dailymirrorlk-3c26cc20-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9275abc95488535fffd39b8f4df3a6f0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1915,
    "chunk_text": "The rationale behind their aversion towards Nanda Malini is purely political, born out of her protest songs against injustice that led critics to launch a scathing attack on her. Correspondingly, they want such arguments to justify especially the macabre bloodbath orchestrated by the state that claimed over 60,000 lives towards the end of the nineteen eighties.\n\nA female social media activist launched a harsh attack against Nanda Malini days before she breathed her last. The attacker\u2019s social media profile picture indicated her affiliation to the United National Party (UNP). She bluntly blamed the singer for the death of over 60,000 youth during the crackdown on the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP) by the UNP government, first led by President J.R. Jayewardene and later by President Ranasinghe Premadasa. This has now triggered a social media debate and a bitter exchange of insults between pro- and anti-JVP groups.\n\nThe pro-JVP groups questioned this female critic as to why\u00a0 regular preaching by various religious leaders has not had a major impact on the youth if Nanda\u2019s songs provoked them to rebel against the government. This is, in a way, a total denial of the impact Nanda had on the youth. No, her songs had an impact on the people who were frustrated by poverty and oppression by\u00a0 successive governments.\n\nHowever, the allegation that Nanda provoked the youth to take up arms is factually incorrect, despite her ideological closeness to the JVP since the early eighties. She started singing protest songs challenging poverty, state oppression and the negative impact of the open economy only in eighties, over a decade after the first insurrection of the JVP in 1971.\n\nUntil that time, she mostly sang patriotic songs, devotional Buddhist songs, songs for children, romantic love songs and songs praising mothers.\n\nIn that case,\u00a0 which singer provoked the youth to take up arms in 1971?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-27-dailymirrorlk-3c26cc20",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-3c26cc20",
    "date_str": "2026-08-27",
    "description": "Is Nanda Malini to blame for the 1988-89 bloodbath?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Is-Nanda-Malini-to-blame-for-the-1988-89-bloodbath/172-348955",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
