    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a7f3b6c65a25add47dd92b576394fae3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1971,
    "chunk_text": "Until that time, she mostly sang patriotic songs, devotional Buddhist songs, songs for children, romantic love songs and songs praising mothers.\n\nIn that case,\u00a0 which singer provoked the youth to take up arms in 1971?\n\nUnlike the case with the first JVP insurrection, it was the UNP government of the eighties that was totally responsible for the 1988-89 bloodbath, despite it\u00a0 being commonly known as the second insurrection of the JVP.\u00a0 The top JVP leaders\u00a0 who had been incarcerated in connection with the first insurrection were released in 1977. Then they conducted their political activities democratically and peacefully, contesting by-elections, District Development Council elections and finally the 1982 Presidential election.\n\nThe rampant corruption, lawlessness, and highhandedness of the ruling party became the order of the day during that period. The situation was so appalling that polling agents of opposition parties in many polling booths were chased away and the ballot boxes were stuffed by UNP sponsored thugs during the 1982 Presidential election.\n\nNanda Malini, at this stage, started to address poverty, systemic failures, and social injustice through her powerful lyrics. Her songs written by intellectual lyricists such as Professor Sunil Ariyaratne became a rallying point for social justice, youth aspirations, and democratic ideals. Her humble origin would have laid the groundwork for her concerns over the pathetic situation the common people were undergoing.\n\nMeanwhile, the UNP government conducted a referendum to extend the term of\u00a0 Parliament by six more years in December, 1982. It was described by the Opposition parties and independent poll observers as the most rigged poll in\u00a0 Sri Lankan history. JVP founder leader Rohana Wijeweera challenged the results of the referendum in the Colombo District Court, putting the government led by J.R. Jayewardene in an awkward situation. The JVP became a thorn in the side of the UNP."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
