    "date_str": "2026-08-27",
    "description": "Is Nanda Malini to blame for the 1988-89 bloodbath?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Is-Nanda-Malini-to-blame-for-the-1988-89-bloodbath/172-348955",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787813434.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-27-dailymirrorlk-3c26cc20-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "943b1d27546fcd4c0ca42cbbb0ed39b6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1186,
    "chunk_text": "Meanwhile, the UNP government conducted a referendum to extend the term of\u00a0 Parliament by six more years in December, 1982. It was described by the Opposition parties and independent poll observers as the most rigged poll in\u00a0 Sri Lankan history. JVP founder leader Rohana Wijeweera challenged the results of the referendum in the Colombo District Court, putting the government led by J.R. Jayewardene in an awkward situation. The JVP became a thorn in the side of the UNP.\n\nIn short, the UNP government and its vigilante groups pushed a party\u00a0 peacefully engaged in democratic politics to take up arms, and then killed tens of thousands to crack down on them. Nanda Malini\u2019s songs had no say in triggering this process. She entered the scene when the groundwork for the bloodbath was being laid by the government. Her song \u201cYadamin Benda Vilangu Laa\u201d explains the brutality of the state crackdown, indicating she was in a middle of a process and not at its beginning.\n\nTherefore, only those who want to justify the social injustice and the brutal oppression which prevailed in the eighties can put the blame on Nanda Malini and other artistes for the violent unrest of 1988-89."
  },
