    "chunk_id": "2026-08-27-dailymirrorlk-b040a421-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7355153c7a7a10e1a69f89783a5219c3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1206,
    "chunk_text": "Gregory Lake water pollution poses serious health risk, CEA finds\n\nWater in Nuwara Eliya\u2019s Gregory Lake has reached a level of pollution that could pose a serious risk to human health if consumed, according to findings presented by the Central Environmental Authority (CEA).\n\nNuwara Eliya District Coordinating Committee Chairman and Member of Parliament Manjula Suraweera Arachchi said the findings were presented by the CEA at a recent District Development Committee meeting.\n\nHe said Gregory Lake, which was originally designed during the British colonial period to receive water from several smaller reservoirs, is now severely polluted.\n\nAccording to the MP, the silting of the smaller reservoirs over the years has resulted in pesticides, fertilisers, other chemicals and waste from surrounding agricultural areas flowing into Gregory Lake and altering its water composition.\n\nCEA data showed that the phosphate level in the lake had reached 1.04 milligrams, compared with the normal level of 0.20 milligrams in water, Suraweera Arachchi said.\n\nHe warned that the deterioration in water quality required urgent attention to prevent further environmental damage and potential risks to public health."
  },
