    "chunk_id": "2026-08-27-dailymirrorlk-86202cfe-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "adf1f920dd0db9efcf51cd91aced7461",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1866,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Dalada Perahera makes Kandy a heaven on earth\n\nThe Kandy Esala Pageant is splendid and attractive because it is conducted at night with the participation of about one hundred elephants\n\nAs recorded in Maha Parinibbana Sutta, Buddha Himself has sanctioned the worship of His corporal remains. This is one of the most important sanctions the Enlightened One has made in the verge of His passing away.\u00a0 It is believed that bodily remains of Buddha, Pacceka Buddha and Arahants are worthy of being worshipped. Corporal remains of Buddha are worshipped by Buddhists in addition to Paribhogika Wastu- items used by the Enlightened One and Buddha images. According to Digha Nikaya, the bodily remains of Buddha are enshrined in sthupas and are worshipped. Four canine teeth of the Enlightened One have said to have been separately enshrined and worshipped. One canine tooth is enshrined in Sakra Bawana-the abode of the King of gods. The second and the third canine teeth are enshrined in Gandhara and Nagadeepa respectively. The fourth canine tooth was removed from funeral ashes by a monk and was handed over to the king of Kalinga. The last Indian ruler to possess the sacred tooth relic was King Guhasiva of Kalinga. Meanwhile there are records to the effect that the left canine tooth is in Kandy and the right canine tooth is enshrined in Somawahie Dagaba.\n\nKing Guhasiva, who was in war with the neighbouring state, decided to send the sacred tooth relic to Sri Lanka. It was brought to Sri Lanka by king Guhasiva\u2019s daughter princes Hemamala, hidden in her knotted hair. She was accompanied by her husband, prince Danta. This happened during the reign of King Kithsiri Mevan in the fourth century B.C. According to Mahavamsa Sri Dalada Perahera had been conducted in a noble way during the reign of King Parakramabahu the Great and King Pandita Parakramabahu."
  },
