    "md5": "842aa7251b501b0f77db738173b0bccc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1871,
    "chunk_text": "NPP MP seeks dismissal of BASL petition on 22nd Amendment\n\nColombo, August 27 (Daily Mirror) - National People\u2019s Power (NPP) Parliamentarian Lakshman Nipuna Arachchi has filed an intervenient petition in the Supreme Court seeking an order to dismiss the Special Determination petition filed by two office-bearers of the Bar Association of Sri Lanka (BASL) against the proposed Twenty-Second Amendment to the Constitution.\n\nBASL Treasurer T.M.S. Pasindu Silva and Assistant Secretary Lakitha Isiwara Wakishta Arachchi had earlier filed a Special Determination petition challenging the constitutionality of the Bill titled \u201cTwenty-Second Amendment to the Constitution\u201d, arguing that the proposed changes undermine the independence of the Judiciary and require approval by the People at a referendum.\n\nNipuna Arachchi requested the Supreme Court to permit him to intervene in the proceedings and participate through counsel in the hearing of the BASL petition challenging the proposed constitutional amendment.\n\nIn his petition, Nipuna Arachchi rejects several central positions advanced by the BASL petitioners, describing them as baseless, without foundation or untenable in law.\n\nAmong the issues challenged by him is the BASL\u2019s contention that there had been inadequate stakeholder consultation over the proposed amendment.\n\nHe further challenges the arguments relating to bypassing the Constitutional Council mechanism, the proposal for a referendum as a solution to an alleged conflict of interest involving the Supreme Court, and the request for the matter to be heard by a Full Court.\n\nAccording to his petition, the Twenty-Second Amendment Bill seeks to increase the retirement age of judges of the Supreme Court and Court of Appeal, make provision regarding the retirement date of the Chief Justice and increase the number of judges of the Court of Appeal."
  },
