    "md5": "50e36330332f3803472ebbfa0140e970",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1656,
    "chunk_text": "Enforced disappearances in Sri Lanka : Grieving families  of missing persons still  await closure\n\nFamilies of disappeared persons at a demonstration seeking justice for their loved ones\n\nGrieving families have been agitating on the streets, holding demonstrations with a unified voice to seek justice for their loved ones\n\nThe OMP was setup to investigate on enforced disappearances, provide relief to victims, and to investigate enforced disappearances\n\nOffice on Missing Persons has received a total of 42,000 complaints regarding missing persons\n\nFamilies of individuals subjected to enforced disappearances have been engaged in a lifelong struggle to unearth what happened to their loved ones during different conflict periods in Sri Lanka\u2019s history. From the 1971 JVP insurrections onwards to the end of the 30-year armed conflict to the recent prison riots, these families have been agitating on the streets, holding demonstrations with a unified voice to seek justice for their loved ones. As a result of these struggles and international interventions such as the Geneva UN Human Rights Council, the Office on Missing Persons (OMP) Act No. 14 of 2016 was passed in Parliament in 2016 and the office was subsequently established in 2018. At the time, enforced disappearances left a black mark on Sri Lanka. The OMP was setup to investigate on enforced disappearances, provide relief to victims, and to find out what happened to people who were subjected to enforced disappearances. Its main objective was to ensure the nonoccurrence of enforced disappearances in Sri Lanka. But to what extent this objective has been fulfilled remains a question."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-28-dailymirrorlk-736260a6",
