    "md5": "c0dbda981ee91d7c305a35e7ba59e084",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1667,
    "chunk_text": "Families of individuals subjected to enforced disappearances have been engaged in a lifelong struggle to unearth what happened to their loved ones during different conflict periods in Sri Lanka\u2019s history. From the 1971 JVP insurrections onwards to the end of the 30-year armed conflict to the recent prison riots, these families have been agitating on the streets, holding demonstrations with a unified voice to seek justice for their loved ones. As a result of these struggles and international interventions such as the Geneva UN Human Rights Council, the Office on Missing Persons (OMP) Act No. 14 of 2016 was passed in Parliament in 2016 and the office was subsequently established in 2018. At the time, enforced disappearances left a black mark on Sri Lanka. The OMP was setup to investigate on enforced disappearances, provide relief to victims, and to find out what happened to people who were subjected to enforced disappearances. Its main objective was to ensure the nonoccurrence of enforced disappearances in Sri Lanka. But to what extent this objective has been fulfilled remains a question.\n\nHe said that some of them had been complaints about the same person. \u201cOur main challenge was to filter these complaints. Back then we didn\u2019t have a proper database. During the 30-year conflict, some people didn\u2019t have identification cards, there were some who left their houses without any documents. During 1971, 1988/1989 technology wasn\u2019t quite advanced. All records were handwritten on chits and books. There were practical issues in identifying individuals due to spelling errors. So we passed an era of manually recording all details,\u201d he said."
  },
