    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "84eefa80c142680ba62a36d3eb41102d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1577,
    "chunk_text": "He said that some of them had been complaints about the same person. \u201cOur main challenge was to filter these complaints. Back then we didn\u2019t have a proper database. During the 30-year conflict, some people didn\u2019t have identification cards, there were some who left their houses without any documents. During 1971, 1988/1989 technology wasn\u2019t quite advanced. All records were handwritten on chits and books. There were practical issues in identifying individuals due to spelling errors. So we passed an era of manually recording all details,\u201d he said.\n\nHe further said that the incumbent government is currently reviewing how a \u2018revival payment\u2019 could be disbursed to support the immediate needs of these families and that the Justice Ministry is working in this regard. \u201cA sum of Rs. 375 million was provided to OMP to initiate preliminary proceedings. Therefore this government seems to be having a political willingness to resolve these issues. We have been given approval to recruit 69 officers to expedite investigations. These officers include lawyers, tracing experts, experts to provide psychosocial support. Therefore we are able to function as an independent body despite being a government institution. The revival payment has already been given to over 5700 individuals while nearly 2000 individuals have been recommended to receive the payment. They will receive it over the next one or two months. In future we plan to reduce the waiting lists and ensure that these families would be able to see some light at the end of the tunnel,\u201d he affirmed."
  },
