    "md5": "35e12485aed7be966abfc769709ef8b4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1680,
    "chunk_text": "He further said that the incumbent government is currently reviewing how a \u2018revival payment\u2019 could be disbursed to support the immediate needs of these families and that the Justice Ministry is working in this regard. \u201cA sum of Rs. 375 million was provided to OMP to initiate preliminary proceedings. Therefore this government seems to be having a political willingness to resolve these issues. We have been given approval to recruit 69 officers to expedite investigations. These officers include lawyers, tracing experts, experts to provide psychosocial support. Therefore we are able to function as an independent body despite being a government institution. The revival payment has already been given to over 5700 individuals while nearly 2000 individuals have been recommended to receive the payment. They will receive it over the next one or two months. In future we plan to reduce the waiting lists and ensure that these families would be able to see some light at the end of the tunnel,\u201d he affirmed.\n\nHe further said that in 2016 a change was introduced where families of the disappeared were able to opt for a Certificate of Absence (CoA). \u201cMany families were quite nervous about accepting a certificate of disappeared. They thought the minute they accept a certificate of disappeared the government would close the file and that they wouldn\u2019t trace the individual. The CoA is therefore an attempt to try and provide some of the services you could access similarly to when you receive a certificate of death. But it is not expansive as a certificate of death. Based on OMP recommendations, 335 families have now obtained a certificate of absence,\u201d he added."
