    "language": "en",
    "md5": "30bc1540d96b48babbe93c5df3756323",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1840,
    "chunk_text": "A spymaster par excellence, President Vladimir Putin is not unaware of US and NATO schemes to destabilise his country. Given Russia\u2019s strong defence ties with Iran, it is unlikely Moscow will heed any US warning\u2014whether about Ukraine or Iran.\n\nInterestingly, in the initial list of sanctions on 60 individuals, entities, and vessels for alleged involvement in trade with Iran, no Chinese financial institutions appeared, despite their role in financing Iran\u2019s oil trade. The omission was deliberate and reflected US caution. When asked, Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent replied, \u201cWhy would I want to blow up the global financial system?\u201d\n\nWashington\u2019s lack of political courage to sanction China for dealing with Iran has rendered the so\u2011called D\u2011Day sanction little more than a damp squib. The Trump administration\u2019s decision to impose sanctions on Iran was akin to its decision to wage war on Iran. Both strategies lack foresight. If the US sanctions China for its dealings with Iran, Beijing could retaliate through financial markets or by restricting exports of critical minerals, including rare earths.\n\nOn Tuesday, Iran dismissed the sanctions as \u201cgross lawlessness\u201d and expressed confidence that many countries would refuse to join the pressure campaign.\n\nThe story about Ratcliffe reading the riot act in Russia may be a red herring. The real purpose of the visit may have been backdoor diplomacy aimed at persuading Moscow to use its influence to push Iran toward a peace deal.\n\nThe visit coincides with heightened diplomatic activity to revive the Islamabad Memorandum of Understanding, signed in June. The deal collapsed within weeks, with the US imposing a blockade on vessels departing from or bound for Iranian ports, while Iran closed the Strait of Hormuz to ships unless they moved with its permission."
  },
