    "md5": "3a7bb3b6592a4d879acd123756966385",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1823,
    "chunk_text": "The visit coincides with heightened diplomatic activity to revive the Islamabad Memorandum of Understanding, signed in June. The deal collapsed within weeks, with the US imposing a blockade on vessels departing from or bound for Iranian ports, while Iran closed the Strait of Hormuz to ships unless they moved with its permission.\n\nHis visit came as differences between Iran\u2019s political leadership and the military, including the Revolutionary Guard, became more evident, with President Masoud Pezeshkian pushing for talks while the latter insisted on prosecuting the war to consolidate Iran\u2019s advantages. Pakistan\u2019s military said \u201csignificant progress\u201d had been made in Field Marshal Munir\u2019s efforts to resume peace talks.\n\nIran\u2019s political leadership expressed willingness to re\u2011enter the talks but voiced concern over the new round of sanctions. Pakistan assured Tehran that such concerns could be addressed during negotiations.\n\nOn Tuesday, Oman\u2019s Foreign Minister Badr Albusaidi was in Tehran to finalise a bilateral deal on the Strait of Hormuz. The deal includes the setting up of a \u201cjoint temporary navigational corridor\u201d, a plan to clear mines, and possibly a security fee. Yesterday, Qatari Prime Minister Sheikh Mohammed bin Abdulrahman bin Jassim Al Thani was expected in Tehran for talks on de\u2011escalating tensions and reviving dialogue between Iran and the US.\n\nMeanwhile, US diplomats evacuated from the region are returning to their embassies, a sign that Washington will not resume the war, which most Americans oppose. Amid these positive signs, Trump\u2019s unpredictability remains a concern. He raises hopes for peace, only to subvert them himself. Iran, meanwhile, would do well to heed President Pezeshkian\u2019s advice to resume talks now that it has gained a position of strength."
  },
