    "md5": "2a8e3e3678cb76620c3c75fdcbe92a09",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1905,
    "chunk_text": "Development focus should not be lost\n\nSri Lanka stands at a crucial point in its history. After years of economic difficulties, political uncertainty and declining public confidence, the country needs the\u00a0 government to\u00a0 provide stability, restore investor confidence and deliver meaningful improvements to the lives of its people. While accountability for past wrongdoing is important, the Government must ensure that its pursuit of those responsible does not overshadow the equally urgent task of national development.\n\nThere is no question that corruption, abuse of public funds and misuse of power must be addressed. Sri Lanka has suffered enormously from poor governance and decisions that placed political interests above the national interest. If credible evidence exists against individuals who have misused public resources, investigations should be carried out and justice should take its course. A functioning democracy cannot turn a blind eye to wrongdoing.\n\nHowever, accountability must not become the central measure of government performance. Citizens did not elect a government merely to investigate the past. They also expect it to create a better future.\n\nThe danger arises when political attention becomes excessively concentrated on previous governments and their alleged failures. Endless investigations, accusations and political arguments may satisfy short-term political expectations, but they do little to put food on people\u2019s tables, create employment or improve public services. Sri Lanka\u2019s economic recovery remains fragile. The country needs sustained investment, stronger exports, improved productivity and new employment opportunities. Small and medium-sized businesses need an environment in which they can grow. Farmers need reliable markets and modern agricultural support.\n\nThese challenges cannot wait while politicians remain occupied with yesterday\u2019s battles."
  },
