    "chunk_size_bytes": 1911,
    "chunk_text": "The danger arises when political attention becomes excessively concentrated on previous governments and their alleged failures. Endless investigations, accusations and political arguments may satisfy short-term political expectations, but they do little to put food on people\u2019s tables, create employment or improve public services. Sri Lanka\u2019s economic recovery remains fragile. The country needs sustained investment, stronger exports, improved productivity and new employment opportunities. Small and medium-sized businesses need an environment in which they can grow. Farmers need reliable markets and modern agricultural support.\n\nThese challenges cannot wait while politicians remain occupied with yesterday\u2019s battles.\n\nInfrastructure development must also remain a priority. Roads, public transport, energy, digital connectivity, water supply and urban facilities are essential to economic growth. Development should not be limited to Colombo or major cities. Rural communities must also benefit from investment and modernisation.\n\nAt the same time, education and healthcare require sustained investment. A country\u2019s greatest resource is its people, and no long-term development strategy can succeed without a healthy, educated and skilled population. Government policies should therefore focus not only on immediate economic indicators but also on building human capital for the next generation.\n\nNone of this means that the Government should abandon investigations into past wrongdoing. Quite the opposite. Those found guilty through due process should face appropriate legal consequences. But the process must be transparent and free from the perception that investigations are being used as political weapons. Justice also loses credibility when it appears selective. If accountability is the objective, the same standards must apply regardless of political affiliation, position or influence."
  },
