    "chunk_size_bytes": 1482,
    "chunk_text": "None of this means that the Government should abandon investigations into past wrongdoing. Quite the opposite. Those found guilty through due process should face appropriate legal consequences. But the process must be transparent and free from the perception that investigations are being used as political weapons. Justice also loses credibility when it appears selective. If accountability is the objective, the same standards must apply regardless of political affiliation, position or influence.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s people need to see progress in their everyday lives. They want reliable public services, affordable living costs, decent employment, better education, efficient transport and an economy capable of providing hope for their children. The country cannot afford a choice between accountability and development. It needs both. The past should be investigated where necessary, but the future must remain the Government\u2019s principal concern.\n\nSri Lanka has already lost valuable time to political conflict, economic mismanagement and instability. Another generation should not be asked to pay the price because the country remains trapped in arguments about the past.\n\nThe Government must therefore look backwards only long enough to learn the lessons and establish accountability, while looking forward with determination. Sri Lanka needs a government that can deliver justice without losing sight of progress. The past must be accounted for, but the future must be built."
