    "date_str": "2026-08-28",
    "description": "How Sri Lanka can turn resources into riches",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/breaking-news/How-Sri-Lanka-can-turn-resources-into-riches/108-348988",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1787894842.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-28-dailymirrorlk-8cef0931-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "76bd1e522748e71f2815cb45cff3b193",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1974,
    "chunk_text": "While Sri Lanka exports high quality garments, tea, and rubber, it often exports them with low value addition. The country ships raw materials and semi-processed goods, missing the opportunity to build a manufacturing ecosystem that could command Swiss like premiums.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s recovery requires a deliberate shift away from dependency on primary exports towards a value-added, export-oriented economy. The National Export Development Plan (NEDP) 2026\u20132030 provides a blueprint, targeting $36 billion in export revenue by focusing on sectors like auto components, rubber, minerals, and processed food.\n\nInstead of exporting raw tea or coconut, Sri Lanka must focus on branded, processed, and packaged goods. The government is pushing to turn the coconut and food industry into a $3 billion sector by encouraging higher-value production.\n\nPolicy consistency is non-negotiable. The recent economic collapse was triggered by a combination of monetary errors, tax cuts, and global shocks. Sri Lanka must maintain the fiscal discipline required by the IMF while ensuring that reforms such as modernizing land and labour laws are implemented to improve the business climate.\n\nTo emulate Switzerland\u2019s stability, Sri Lanka must rebuild the independence of its institutions. This means restoring the autonomy of the Central Bank, ensuring Parliament functions as a genuine check on executive power, and reverting to a professional, merit-based civil service. The \"absentee Finance Minister\" model and politically motivated policies must end.\n\nDespite high female literacy, female labour force participation in Sri Lanka is only 31.6%, significantly hampering productivity. Investing in childcare, flexible work arrangements, and digital infrastructure would mobilize this underutilized workforce. Furthermore, the country must leverage its educated youth for the digital economy and business process outsourcing (BPO) sector, rather than solely relying on physical exports."
  },
