    "md5": "b8e2473cd4f5ae842d7ef8344139d180",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1751,
    "chunk_text": "Despite high female literacy, female labour force participation in Sri Lanka is only 31.6%, significantly hampering productivity. Investing in childcare, flexible work arrangements, and digital infrastructure would mobilize this underutilized workforce. Furthermore, the country must leverage its educated youth for the digital economy and business process outsourcing (BPO) sector, rather than solely relying on physical exports.\n\nSwitzerland proves that natural resources are not a prerequisite for wealth. Conversely, Sri Lanka proves that natural resources are not sufficient for prosperity. The downfall of Sri Lanka stems not from a lack of resources or talent, Sri Lanka does have deeply rooted legal and financial frameworks, blending colonial history with modern evolution. Main reason is failure of good governance and a lack of strategic value creation. To overcome this situation, Sri Lanka must \"think like the Swiss.\"\n\nSri Lanka must adopt a Swiss mindset prioritising precision, stability, and value addition in every sector of the economy. This means shifting from reactive, short-term fixes to strategic, long-term thinking that builds resilience and global competitiveness.\n\nBut a national transformation of this scale cannot succeed without confronting the deep seated accountability deficits that have held the country back for decades. Weak governance, opaque decision-making, and a culture of impunity have eroded public trust and stifled progress.\n\nTo truly move forward, Sri Lanka must embed transparency, meritocracy, and rule of law at every level of public and private life. Only then can precision and stability take root and only then can the country add genuine value, not just for export markets, but for its own people."
