    "md5": "beb0bc26e75d483a4cfc2b3be70e043a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1537,
    "chunk_text": "Number affected by El Ni\u00f1o climbs to 84,546 as vegetable prices set to rise\n\nColombo, Aug. 28 (Daily Mirror) - The number of people affected by the worsening impact of El Ni\u00f1o conditions has increased to 84,546, raising concerns over a possible surge in vegetable prices in the coming days.\n\nAccording to the latest figures released by the Disaster Management Centre (DMC), 24,144 families have been affected by the weather conditions as of yesterday.\n\nThe number of affected people has risen from 81,868 recorded on Tuesday (25), indicating a further increase in the impact of the prevailing conditions.\n\nHowever, the number of affected families has declined from 25,628 reported on Tuesday.\n\nThe worsening weather conditions are also expected to affect vegetable supplies, with prices likely to increase sharply in the next few days.\n\nThe Northern, Eastern, North Central, Southern and North Western provinces are affected by the dry spell prevailing at the moment.\n\nFormer President of the Manning Market Traders Association S. M. Upasena told the Daily Mirror that vegetable prices could increase in the coming days. He said prices are somewhat stable now, but could soar during the coming days. \"The dry spell had resulted in reduction of water available for cultivation of vegetables. This could result in soaring of prices,\" he said.\n\nThe price of beans stood at Rs. 300 per kilo, cabbage at Rs. 150, carrots at Rs. 200, leeks at Rs. 150, and capsicum at Rs. 400. Upasena said these prices could increase during the coming days."
