    "md5": "9b050a2b02515d9c1239594f9ae47eb3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1904,
    "chunk_text": "Social media groups allegedly used to trade sexual content\n\nColombo, Aug. 28 (Daily Mirror) - Groups operating on Facebook and other social media platforms are allegedly being used to trade sexual videos and private photographs of women and children, raising serious concerns over online exploitation, the Women and Child Affairs Ministry said.\n\nThe Ministry said information about the alleged activities had been received in recent days, while social media activists have also published posts and videos drawing public attention to the issue.\n\nFollowing preliminary inquiries, the Ministry has referred relevant information and findings to the Sri Lanka Police Bureau for the Prevention of Abuse of Children and Women and the Computer Crime Investigation Division for further investigation.\n\nDuring examinations of the relevant groups, authorities reportedly found that fake accounts were being used to attempt to sell photographs and videos of people known to the individuals operating the accounts.\n\nIn some cases, attempts had allegedly been made to distribute and sell photographs and videos of female members of their own families.\n\nThe Ministry also said many of the groups were reportedly being operated from overseas, making the investigation and prevention of such activities more challenging.\n\nThe Ministry urged parents and guardians to closely monitor children's use of social media and pay attention to the people with whom they communicate and interact online.\n\nIt warned that women and children could become victims of online exploitation even within their own homes and called on parents and guardians to take appropriate measures to protect children.\n\nAnyone who believes a family member has become a victim of such an online operation, or is experiencing harmful or addictive behaviour associated with such activities, can seek assistance through the 1938 and 1929 toll-free helplines."
  },
