    "md5": "2da1d937cc6ab0289bfe6a82a3b8db5a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1486,
    "chunk_text": "245 coral species identified along Sri Lanka\u2019s Eastern coast\n\nColombo, Aug. 28 (Daily Mirror) - A total of 245 coral species have been identified along Sri Lanka\u2019s eastern coastline, highlighting the importance of protecting the country\u2019s diverse marine ecosystems.\n\nAgainst this backdrop, conservation and restoration efforts are being strengthened through the Coral Conservation Centre and Laboratory being established in Nilaveli, Trincomalee.\n\nThe facility is focusing particularly on the conservation of rare coral species, including Black Coral and Red Coral, while also carrying out programmes to artificially propagate them.\n\nThe laboratory is designed to recreate natural marine temperatures and environmental conditions required for coral cultivation. The corals produced through the process will subsequently be transplanted onto the seabed to support the restoration of coral reefs.\n\nDeputy Minister of Environment Anton Jayakody inspected the facility on August 25.\n\nConstruction of the centre is expected to be completed by the end of September. It is planned to eventually operate as a Coral Museum and Research Centre, providing opportunities for university students, researchers and the public to engage in marine research and learn about coral conservation.\n\nThe project is part of broader Government efforts to protect marine biodiversity, promote sustainable management of ocean resources and safeguard vulnerable ecosystems from the impacts of climate change."
  },
