    "chunk_size_bytes": 1355,
    "chunk_text": "Colombo Port rises to 20th among world\u2019s busiest container ports\n\nColombo, Aug. 28 (Daily Mirror) - The Port of Colombo has climbed to 20th place among the world\u2019s major container ports after recording an 11.9 per cent year-on-year increase in container handling during the first half of 2026, according to the latest Alphaliner figures.\n\nThe Sri Lanka Ports Authority (SLPA) said the port handled 4,444,034 twenty-foot equivalent units (TEUs) during the first six months of this year, compared with 3,972,053 TEUs during the corresponding period in 2025.\n\nThis represents an increase of 471,981 TEUs, or 11.9 per cent.\n\nThe latest performance has moved the Port of Colombo up from 26th place globally at the end of 2025 to 20th place in the first half of 2026, marking one of the most significant upward movements among major container ports worldwide.\n\nThe SLPA said the growth follows a record year in 2025, when the Port of Colombo handled approximately 8.3 million TEUs.\n\nThe sustained growth further strengthens Colombo\u2019s position as a major transshipment hub in the Indian Ocean, the SLPA said.\n\nTransshipment accounts for the overwhelming majority of Colombo\u2019s container throughput, with the port serving as a key link between international mainline and feeder networks connecting East Asia, South Asia, the Middle East, Africa and Europe."
