    "md5": "cd5e78f5d227dc31bb062d1fcad2ebe6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2016,
    "chunk_text": "India races to Nepal\u2019s aid after deadly floods, sends 62 tonnes of relief\n\nPRAGATIVADI - Less than 12 hours after the deadly flash floods hit Nepal on August 26, an Indian Air Force C-130J aircraft took off carrying 10 tonnes of humanitarian and disaster-relief supplies. The first consignment reached Kathmandu later that night.\n\nThe rapid response has reinforced India\u2019s growing reputation as a regional \u201cfirst responder\u201d during major humanitarian emergencies.\n\nIndia\u2019s initial shipment was followed by additional consignments containing essential supplies, including medicines and food packets.\n\nBy Thursday, the total quantity of relief material delivered by air had reached 47.5 tonnes. By Friday afternoon, India had sent a total of 62 tonnes of assistance to Nepal.\n\nThe speed of the response stood out as several other countries and international organisations were still arranging their assistance.\n\nPrime Minister Narendra Modi spoke with Nepal Prime Minister Balendra Shah on Wednesday, expressing condolences over the loss of life and assuring him that India was prepared to provide all possible humanitarian assistance.\n\nModi also said Indian teams were coordinating with Nepalese authorities on rescue and relief efforts.\n\nForeign Minister S Jaishankar subsequently announced the dispatch of the first 10-tonne consignment, saying that India stood with Nepal and its people.\n\nChina has coordinated emergency supplies and financial assistance, while South Korea, the United Kingdom, the European Union and the United States have also offered support.\n\nThe US has committed $500,000 along with a disaster-response adviser, while the UK has pledged \u00a35 million and emergency responders.\n\nInternational organisations, including the United Nations, World Health Organisation and World Bank, have also indicated support for Nepal\u2019s recovery efforts.\n\nThe World Bank has indicated that it could provide emergency assistance of up to 25 billion Nepalese rupees, equivalent to about $163.55 million."
