    "md5": "40fd2e3a8abeb8f396484de4544f6adb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1929,
    "chunk_text": "International organisations, including the United Nations, World Health Organisation and World Bank, have also indicated support for Nepal\u2019s recovery efforts.\n\nThe World Bank has indicated that it could provide emergency assistance of up to 25 billion Nepalese rupees, equivalent to about $163.55 million.\n\nThe disaster struck the Nepal-Tibet border region on August 26.\n\nInitial assessments indicate that a massive glacial collapse may have triggered a powerful torrent of ice, rock, mud and water through mountain river systems.\n\nThe resulting destruction affected villages, roads and bridges, leaving communities cut off and intensifying the humanitarian crisis.\n\nSatellite imagery and geological assessments have provided further clues about the sequence of events, although scientists are continuing to examine the precise causes and circumstances surrounding the disaster.\n\nBy Friday afternoon, 469 people had died in Nepal, while three deaths were reported in Tibet, taking the combined death toll to 472.\n\nNearly 1,000 people were reported missing in Nepal, while 558 remained unaccounted for in Tibet.\n\nThe figures are expected to change as rescue teams reach more affected areas and authorities continue search operations.\n\nIndia\u2019s response to Nepal follows a broader pattern of New Delhi stepping in quickly during major humanitarian crises.\n\nIn recent years, India has provided assistance during disasters and emergencies in several countries, including T\u00fcrkiye and Syria following the 2023 earthquakes, Myanmar after the 2024 disaster, Sri Lanka after the 2025 cyclone and Venezuela following the 2026 earthquake.\n\nThe approach reflects India\u2019s growing emphasis on humanitarian assistance and disaster relief as part of its regional and international engagement.\n\nFor Nepal, however, the immediate priority remains rescuing those still missing, reaching isolated communities and delivering essential supplies."
