    "md5": "fa96eefc059e391fa220993dad677c0d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1946,
    "chunk_text": "President pledges full rights, better life for Malayagam community\n\nColombo, Aug. 28 (Daily Mirror) - President Anura Kumara Dissanayake said the previous Cabinet had decided to remove obstacles faced by people who had left Sri Lanka during the war and were living in India, enabling them to return to the country.\n\nHe made these remarks today (28) while attending the ceremony to hand over the \u201cNavodaya Gama\u201d housing project to 50 families from Poonagala and Kabaragala who lost their homes in a landslide in 2023.\n\nThe families had been living in difficult and unsafe conditions for nearly three and a half years.\n\nThe President said the Government had assumed responsibility for ensuring a better life for every person born in Sri Lanka, contributing to its economy and ultimately becoming part of the country.\n\nHe said priority had been given to housing projects in the forthcoming Budget, while the Government expected to include provisions safeguarding the rights of the Malayagam community in future agreements with plantation companies.\n\nPresident Dissanayake also stressed the need to protect the central hills, which he described as the \u201cheart of the country\u201d, for future generations.\n\nHe said steps would be taken to introduce a Bill to Parliament by the end of this year or early next year to establish a Central Highlands Protection Authority by bringing together the powers of institutions and departments responsible for protecting the region under one authority.\n\nThe \u201cNavodaya Gama\u201d project, constructed under the Government\u2019s \u201cA Thriving Nation \u2013 A Beautiful Life\u201d policy, consists of 50 fully equipped houses, each measuring 550 square feet and built on 10-perch plots.\n\nEach house comprises two bedrooms, a living room, kitchen and bathroom, with approximately Rs. 3.2 million spent on each unit.\n\nEach family has also been provided with essential furniture and kitchen equipment worth around Rs. 175,000."
