    "chunk_size_bytes": 1730,
    "chunk_text": "Groundwater levels follow seasonal decline in dry-zone areas\n\nColombo, Aug. 29 (Daily Mirror) - Groundwater levels in several dry-zone areas of Sri Lanka are showing their usual seasonal decline, with the most significant reductions being recorded in Kalpitiya and the Jaffna Peninsula, according to the Water Resources Board.\n\nWater Resources Board Assistant General Manager (Research and Development), Ruwan Rajapaksha said groundwater levels generally decline during the drier months, with the reduction becoming more pronounced around August in areas that receive relatively low rainfall.\n\n\u201cIf we look at the climatic behaviour from December through March and April and then up to August, there is generally a decrease in groundwater levels around August,\u201d he said.\n\nAccording to available data, groundwater levels in some parts of the Jaffna Peninsula and Kalpitiya have currently fallen by around 70 to 80 per cent from their maximum seasonal levels. Rajapaksha clarified that this percentage refers to the reduction from the highest groundwater level recorded during the wet period, rather than an 80 per cent depletion of the total groundwater resource. \u201cFor example, a groundwater level that may be observed at around two metres during a high-water period could fall by approximately 1.5 metres during the dry period,\u201d he explained.\n\nThe decline in these areas is largely linked to lower rainfall, with similar reductions recurring in line with seasonal climatic patterns. Rajapaksha stressed that such fluctuations do not necessarily indicate that Sri Lanka\u2019s groundwater resources are being uniformly depleted. \u201cGroundwater levels naturally rise and fall in response to rainfall and dry periods,\u201d he said."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-28-dailymirrorlk-d5efa15e",
