    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9501fd9f8a231571c428e478253d75d9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1786,
    "chunk_text": "The decline in these areas is largely linked to lower rainfall, with similar reductions recurring in line with seasonal climatic patterns. Rajapaksha stressed that such fluctuations do not necessarily indicate that Sri Lanka\u2019s groundwater resources are being uniformly depleted. \u201cGroundwater levels naturally rise and fall in response to rainfall and dry periods,\u201d he said.\n\nThe Water Resources Board currently monitors groundwater levels across the country through a network of around 1,600 observation points, of which approximately 190 are equipped for real-time monitoring.\n\nManual groundwater measurements are generally carried out between March and April and again from June to September. However, because these measurements are taken periodically, they may not capture short-term changes or variations in groundwater behaviour at a high resolution.\n\nTo improve monitoring, the Water Resources Board has introduced a real-time monitoring pilot project in several areas, including Point Pedro in Jaffna, the Puttalam region, Mannar, Kumbukkan Oya and Maduru Oya, which allows groundwater levels in these locations to be observed more frequently and provides a clearer picture of short-term changes.\n\nMeanwhile, Rajapaksha warned that excessive and unregulated groundwater extraction could eventually lead to wider environmental and water-quality problems. This is of particular concern in Jaffna, where groundwater is the main source of water for many. The widespread construction of tube wells and pumping of large volumes of groundwater could place additional pressure, particularly if extraction exceeds the amount that can be naturally replenished. \u201cIf water is extracted unnecessarily and in excessive quantities, the balance of the system can be affected,\u201d he said."
