    "chunk_size_bytes": 1578,
    "chunk_text": "A minimum age limit for trishaw drivers\n\nIt was the same concern that necessitated a higher age limit in 2018 as well.\n\nThe government is considering to raise the age limit for three-wheeler drivers up to 40 years, according to Deputy Minister of Vocational Education Nalin Hewage. It must be reminded that successive governments have mooted this idea several times before on various grounds and abandoned it for various reasons.\n\nYet, the proposal for age restrictions for the trishaw drivers have not been static over the years. In 2016, the then Chairman of the National Council for Road Safety (NCRS) Dr. Sisira Kodagoda had said that legal age limit for Three-wheel drivers who provide public transport should be increased to 25.\u00a0 Two years later, in 2018, the Deputy Minister of Transport Minister Ashok Abesinghe told media that the Government was considering banning the issuing of trishaw driving licence to persons less than 35 years.\n\nNow the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) government is mulling to raise the eligibility age for trishaw driving license up to 40.\n\nThe reasons for the increasing the age limit for tuk-tuk drives has also changed over the years. Earlier it was the road safety concerns that prompted the authorities to impose restrictions on younger drivers, citing younger age as a major factor that contributes to more road accidents. Announcing the move to impose ban on drivers below the age of 25 in 2016, NCRS Chairman Dr. Kodagoda also had argued \u201cmany of the fatal accidents involving three-wheelers have been caused by drivers below 25.\u201d"
  },
