    "chunk_size_bytes": 1691,
    "chunk_text": "The reasons for the increasing the age limit for tuk-tuk drives has also changed over the years. Earlier it was the road safety concerns that prompted the authorities to impose restrictions on younger drivers, citing younger age as a major factor that contributes to more road accidents. Announcing the move to impose ban on drivers below the age of 25 in 2016, NCRS Chairman Dr. Kodagoda also had argued \u201cmany of the fatal accidents involving three-wheelers have been caused by drivers below 25.\u201d\n\nThis time, Deputy Minister Hewage attributes\u00a0 the move to the lack of interest on the part of the youth to seek vocational training, while pointing out that the age restriction would not be implemented immediately. However, Hewage did not mention anything about the common perception that trishaws are a major contributor to road accidents or the younger drivers are more prone to accidents.\n\nIn spite of the decision on the increase of the age limit on the grounds of road safety having public support, it does not seem to be logical when one goes into the details. This might be seen as a wise move mainly as\u00a0 three-wheelers in the country are invariably viewed as a nuisance and their\u00a0 drivers\u00a0 are considered as an undisciplined lot, which is not totally wrong. However, they are not the only category of undisciplined drivers on the roads.\n\nAccording to the statistics issued by the very NCRS, three-wheelers are not the vehicles\u00a0 involved in the greatest number of road accidents. Motorbikes top the lists of total road accidents as well as\u00a0 fatal accidents for the past several years. This factor has been confirmed by the statistics issued by the NCRS as well as the police."
