    "md5": "03fca7512881fd1b0d1341fd01f97df5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1922,
    "chunk_text": "The AI debate we are not having\n\nChina went from almost no high speed rail to the largest network on earth in twenty years.\n\nThere is a hard part to this, though, and I am not going to skip it. Some people here will lose their jobs\n\nStrip away the noise,\u00a0 and what has actually happened over the past three years is fairly simple. Computers have become able to do a particular kind of work that previously required a trained human. Not physical work. Not the arithmetic that calculators have handled for fifty years. The work in between. Reading a stack of documents and telling you what matters in them. Looking at a year of transactions and noticing the pattern. Drafting the contract, checking the ledger, building the forecast, writing the report.\n\nThat work has always been expensive, because it required someone educated, paid monthly, and difficult to replace. It is now becoming cheap. That is the entire event. Everything else is an argument about what it means. And the argument, so far, has been about loss. Ask almost anyone in San Francisco or Brussels what artificial intelligence means,\u00a0 and you will hear about the paralegal, the junior analyst, the graduate hire who was going to learn the trade by doing the tedious parts of it.\n\nThe worry is legitimate. Those people will lose work they depend on. But notice what the question assumes before it is even asked. It assumes somebody already had the job. Replacement requires an incumbent. And that is where this stops describing us. Twenty minutes from where I am writing this, there is a factory. Twenty two people, real customers, an order book into next quarter. There is no CFO, and there has never been one. Not because the founder is unsophisticated, because she can tell you the landed cost of every line she makes from memory and she is usually right. There is no CFO because a competent one costs more per year than the company clears in profit."
  },
