    "chunk_size_bytes": 1867,
    "chunk_text": "The worry is legitimate. Those people will lose work they depend on. But notice what the question assumes before it is even asked. It assumes somebody already had the job. Replacement requires an incumbent. And that is where this stops describing us. Twenty minutes from where I am writing this, there is a factory. Twenty two people, real customers, an order book into next quarter. There is no CFO, and there has never been one. Not because the founder is unsophisticated, because she can tell you the landed cost of every line she makes from memory and she is usually right. There is no CFO because a competent one costs more per year than the company clears in profit.\n\nThat absence has a cost, and it is not abstract. Companies like hers rarely die of weak demand. They die of a cash gap nobody saw coming from three weeks out, or a receivable that aged quietly, or a good order that turned out to be underwater once the currency moved. Each of those is the same failure, which is a failure to see far enough ahead to act. The same blindness follows them to the bank. A business whose numbers exist only as a feeling cannot be lent to, so the International Finance Corporation now puts the unmet credit need of small firms in developing economies at 5.7 trillion dollars a year.\n\nThat is not a shortage of capital. It is a shortage of information. Lenders who cannot price risk ask for collateral instead, which excludes precisely the firms with the most growth in them and the least land. Hold all of that in mind, and then ask her the question they are asking in San Francisco. What happens when a machine can do an analyst\u2019s work? She is not going to wonder what happens to hers. She is going to tell you she will finally have one. You cannot be replaced at a job nobody was doing. If this sounds like special pleading, look at what people actually believe."
