    "chunk_size_bytes": 2002,
    "chunk_text": "That is not a shortage of capital. It is a shortage of information. Lenders who cannot price risk ask for collateral instead, which excludes precisely the firms with the most growth in them and the least land. Hold all of that in mind, and then ask her the question they are asking in San Francisco. What happens when a machine can do an analyst\u2019s work? She is not going to wonder what happens to hers. She is going to tell you she will finally have one. You cannot be replaced at a job nobody was doing. If this sounds like special pleading, look at what people actually believe.\n\nA society that already built the institutions, filled the roles and staffed the professions experiences a cheap substitute as a threat to something it owns. A society still building them experiences it as a shortcut. China is the clearest case because it has done this before, and recently enough to remember. It went from almost no high speed rail to the largest network on earth in twenty years. It skipped credit cards entirely and went from cash to phones. When you have personally watched a missing thing arrive within your working life, a new capability does not read as a threat to the old arrangement. There was no old arrangement. That is closer to our position than San Francisco\u2019s is.\n\nThere is a hard part to this, though, and I am not going to skip it. Some people here will lose their jobs. The outsourcing industry across South Asia employs millions of people doing structured, rules based, document heavy work. That is precisely the work these systems are best at. The same is true of the bookkeeper who keeps the ledgers for eleven small firms, and the junior at the accounting practice, and the clerk entering invoices at a distributor. These are real jobs supporting real households, in a country with almost none of the safety net that Europe and America are relying on. Those people are owed time, retraining and a serious plan. They are not owed a lecture about how the economy comes out ahead."
