    "chunk_size_bytes": 1848,
    "chunk_text": "There is a hard part to this, though, and I am not going to skip it. Some people here will lose their jobs. The outsourcing industry across South Asia employs millions of people doing structured, rules based, document heavy work. That is precisely the work these systems are best at. The same is true of the bookkeeper who keeps the ledgers for eleven small firms, and the junior at the accounting practice, and the clerk entering invoices at a distributor. These are real jobs supporting real households, in a country with almost none of the safety net that Europe and America are relying on. Those people are owed time, retraining and a serious plan. They are not owed a lecture about how the economy comes out ahead.\n\nIntelligence can simply become an import, rented from two countries and priced in dollars, so that productivity rises here and the payment leaves. The best funded firms adopt first and pull further ahead, and the gap that closes between countries opens up inside them. And capability without institutions is only a better documented failure, because you can see your cash position perfectly and still lose the company to a dispute that takes six years to resolve. None of it substitutes for the port, the power cut, the exchange rate or the courts. What it does is remove one specific constraint on people who are already good at what they do, which was never the constraint anyone chose. It was arithmetic. So the replacement question is real, and it deserves a serious answer where it applies. It is simply not the first question. For most of the world, for most of the businesses and most of the people who will decide what this century actually looks like, the first question is what happens when a capability that was always out of reach becomes ordinary.\n\n81% of Sri Lankans Now Use AI Instead of Google \u2014 Is That Safe?"
