    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "cf26aba7b746364032de4d4d863fb0e2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1973,
    "chunk_text": "Economists spar over growth versus governance in Sri Lanka\u2019s recovery\n\nSri Lanka-Korea Business Council puts spotlight on \u2018Sri Lanka\u2019s Future - Forecast, Scenarios and Challenges\u2019\n\nThe tension between the pursuing headline economic growth and establishing genuine long-term stability took centre stage recently, as the leading economists and corporate leaders debated the trajectory of Sri Lanka\u2019s post-crisis recovery.\n\nHosted by the Sri Lanka-Korea Business Council of the Ceylon Chamber of Commerce, at Hilton Colombo Residences, the panel discussion titled \u2018Sri Lanka\u2019s Future \u2013 Forecast, Scenarios and Challenges\u2019 laid bare the deep divisions over the macroeconomic planning, debt rollover risks, international credit ratings and structural reforms required to prevent another sovereign default.\n\nBeneath the granular debate over the interest rates and credit ratings, one question kept resurfacing: whether growth alone can rescue Sri Lanka or whether it will take a deeper overhaul of governance and institutional accountability to make that growth durable.\n\nAs part of its flagship event series, the council organised the forum to provide a comprehensive analysis of Sri Lanka\u2019s economic outlook, in the context of the evolving domestic developments and an increasingly dynamic global and geopolitical landscape.\n\nThe event introduced a novel structure, featuring two comprehensive keynote presentations, followed by an interactive format that paired a primary panel of industry leaders alongside a dedicated reaction panel. This unique mechanism was specifically integrated to fine-tune the economic debate, heighten panel preparation and drive optimal policy conclusions.\n\nDelivering the welcome address, Sri Lanka-Korea Business Council President Shamil Mendis outlined the broader context of the gathering, reflecting on the council\u2019s ongoing initiatives to foster a growing partnership between the business communities of the two nations."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-30-dailymirrorlk-7ab73356",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-7ab73356",
    "date_str": "2026-08-30",
    "description": "Economists spar over growth versus governance in Sri Lanka\u2019s recovery",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Economists-spar-over-growth-versus-governance-in-Sri-Lankas-recovery/273-349095",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1788113204.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-30-dailymirrorlk-7ab73356-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a8aec66e5a701aef0f8e9b5b87324adb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1736,
    "chunk_text": "Delivering the welcome address, Sri Lanka-Korea Business Council President Shamil Mendis outlined the broader context of the gathering, reflecting on the council\u2019s ongoing initiatives to foster a growing partnership between the business communities of the two nations.\n\n\u201cSri Lanka is at an important stage in its economic journey. We have seen encouraging signs of recovery but at the same time, the path ahead will bring both opportunities and challenges. We have to maintain stability and we have to grow our economy at the same time,\u201d Mendis stated.\n\n\u201cFor the business community, looking towards the future is not only about understanding economic forecasts or predicting numbers. It is about asking what different scenarios could emerge, how they may affect the economy and businesses, what risks we need to be prepared for and where new opportunities may arise. This is exactly why platforms such as today\u2019s discussion are important,\u201d he added.\n\nMendis explained that the session was designed to draw out unvarnished assessments, reminding the participants that the true value of the discussion would be determined by not holding back on what they really feel.\n\nThe high-profile event drew senior diplomatic representation, including the Ambassador for Italy, Ambassador for T\u00fcrkiye and High Commissioner of Canada. Notably, it marked the final public appearance of Ambassador for the Republic of Korea Miyon Lee, who attended the session just hours before taking a flight to her next diplomatic posting.\n\n\u201cAmbassador Miyon Lee bids farewell to us as she completes her tenure in Sri Lanka. In fact, she takes a flight to her next posting tonight,\u201d Mendis said, thanking her for her support of the council\u2019s work."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-30-dailymirrorlk-7ab73356",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-7ab73356",
    "date_str": "2026-08-30",
    "description": "Economists spar over growth versus governance in Sri Lanka\u2019s recovery",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Economists-spar-over-growth-versus-governance-in-Sri-Lankas-recovery/273-349095",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1788113204.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-30-dailymirrorlk-7ab73356-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b91f56d01cc2c04e35294a8766d46bcd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1895,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cAmbassador Miyon Lee bids farewell to us as she completes her tenure in Sri Lanka. In fact, she takes a flight to her next posting tonight,\u201d Mendis said, thanking her for her support of the council\u2019s work.\n\nSetting the analytical tone in the first keynote presentation, Dr. de Mel drew a historical parallel between Sri Lanka\u2019s macroeconomic planning and Christopher Columbus\u2019 voyage across the Atlantic.\n\nIn the late 15th century, Columbus set off from Europe destined for India but a fundamental misunderstanding of his trajectory landed him in the Caribbean instead.\n\n\u201cIn the 1500s, all of Asia was known as the Indies. Christopher Columbus in 1490 or so set off from Europe to India but due to a navigation error, he landed in the Caribbean,\u201d de Mel observed.\n\n\u201cEven though he knew exactly where he wanted to get to, he wasn\u2019t quite sure of his trajectory; it turned out and ended up in quite a different place.\u201d\n\nDr. de Mel cautioned that Sri Lanka risks a similar catastrophic outcome by anchoring the national policy to the flawed economic projections published in the official government and International Monetary Fund (IMF) review documents.\n\nHe presented empirical evidence demonstrating that the official review documents have consistently published macroeconomic forecasts that diverge sharply from the ground reality. The official projections for the current account persistently anticipated severe deficits, yet the actual outcomes produced substantial surpluses.\n\nSimilarly, the official documents imputed foreign exchange rates climbing past Rs.460 and Rs.510 to the US dollar, even as the market dynamics held the rupee far stronger. Furthermore, while the yield curve predicted in the debt sustainability assessments assumed the borrowing costs around 300 basis points below the actual market yields, economic growth outperformed the official projections."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-30-dailymirrorlk-7ab73356",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-7ab73356",
    "date_str": "2026-08-30",
    "description": "Economists spar over growth versus governance in Sri Lanka\u2019s recovery",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Economists-spar-over-growth-versus-governance-in-Sri-Lankas-recovery/273-349095",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1788113204.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-30-dailymirrorlk-7ab73356-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "84932f8b1d0ccbbc0dee72c62a9e9320",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2020,
    "chunk_text": "Similarly, the official documents imputed foreign exchange rates climbing past Rs.460 and Rs.510 to the US dollar, even as the market dynamics held the rupee far stronger. Furthermore, while the yield curve predicted in the debt sustainability assessments assumed the borrowing costs around 300 basis points below the actual market yields, economic growth outperformed the official projections.\n\nThis navigation problem is most acutely visible in the Central Bank of Sri Lanka\u2019s management of inflation. Under the prevailing monetary framework, the Central Bank is legally bound to anchor headline inflation at 5 percent. However, for eight consecutive quarters, official inflation consistently undershot this target, often slipping into the deflationary territory.\n\nDr. de Mel argued that this persistent failure fundamentally alters the debt sustainability dynamics. In domestic debt management, inflation serves as an implicit mechanism that lowers the real burden of government interest payments. When the state pays elevated nominal yields while inflation drops to near zero, the real interest rates spike to historically volatile levels.\n\nThe direct consequence of this mismatch has been an enormous and regressive redistribution of wealth.\n\n\u201cWhen the government pays high interest and there is no inflation, what you call the real interest rates in Sri Lanka became amongst the highest in the region and also became extremely volatile,\u201d Dr. de Mel explained.\n\n\u201cWhen you have high interest rates and no inflation, the only people that benefit from it are people like us, who have large savings, can leverage and borrow and we make money for nothing. While all the people who are living at subsistence, are just managing on wages, are paying higher taxes\u201418 percent VAT\u2014so the government can collect that money and pay us the high real interest rates,\u201d he continued.\n\n\u201cThis is a racket in which people like us benefit enormously on the backs of those who see very little benefit from the economy.\u201d"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
