    "time_ut": 1788113204.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-30-dailymirrorlk-7ab73356-0004",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9056174869f95c202ddfb8d4a099e5b0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1658,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cWhen you have high interest rates and no inflation, the only people that benefit from it are people like us, who have large savings, can leverage and borrow and we make money for nothing. While all the people who are living at subsistence, are just managing on wages, are paying higher taxes\u201418 percent VAT\u2014so the government can collect that money and pay us the high real interest rates,\u201d he continued.\n\n\u201cThis is a racket in which people like us benefit enormously on the backs of those who see very little benefit from the economy.\u201d\n\n\u201cWe got it wrong on both ends. We had a sustained negative supply-side shock that we didn\u2019t respond to by reducing the interest rates, which we should have, to stimulate demand. But when we had a negative supply shock that\u2019s basically transient, we intervened by jacking up the interest rates 100 basis points,\u201d he noted.\n\n\u201cOnce you get into a gazetted agreement with the government to deliver a certain level of inflation, you have to meet that. There is no management discretion here. These are simple facts of accountability and competence. In the Bank of England, as in Sri Lanka, we\u2019ve set up a device that says if you miss it by a certain amount, you must go to Parliament and explain why you did it. We haven\u2019t been able to get Parliament to ask good enough questions to rectify the problem,\u201d Dr. de Mel pointed out.\n\n\u201cThe Central Bank failing for eight quarters in a row on a single target that it is legally mandated to do, tells you how seriously we must look at this problem of competence, because it is one of the most competent institutions we have and yet, there we have it.\u201d"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-30-dailymirrorlk-7ab73356",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-7ab73356",
    "date_str": "2026-08-30",
    "description": "Economists spar over growth versus governance in Sri Lanka\u2019s recovery",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Economists-spar-over-growth-versus-governance-in-Sri-Lankas-recovery/273-349095",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1788113204.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-30-dailymirrorlk-7ab73356-0005",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "eea4e2f18e5f3e2b803944b0b0712a50",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1875,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe Central Bank failing for eight quarters in a row on a single target that it is legally mandated to do, tells you how seriously we must look at this problem of competence, because it is one of the most competent institutions we have and yet, there we have it.\u201d\n\nUtilising the Harvard Atlas of Economic Complexity, Jafferjee contrasted Sri Lanka\u2019s stagnant product space with that of the regional peers. While nations like Vietnam expanded their exports from US $ 15 billion in 1998 to nearly US $ 450 billion by aggressively adding complex manufactured goods, machinery and electronics to their trade baskets, Sri Lanka\u2019s exports have remained narrowly confined to garments, tea and rubber. He stressed that economic complexity requires tacit knowledge, which is acquired either through foreign direct investment or by actively mobilising the skilled diaspora.\n\nJafferjee then presented the sobering demographic data, warning that Sri Lanka is facing a sharp demographic contraction that would hollow out its future workforce. The annual births have plummeted from roughly 370,000 in the post-conflict period to just 240,000 last year, creating an impending contraction across primary schooling, higher education and labour market.\n\nFurthermore, the census data reveals a missing generation between the ages of 25 and 35, where nearly 20 percent of the population cohort has emigrated, due to a lack of domestic economic opportunity.\n\nCompounding this problem is the chronic underutilisation of women in the workforce. Despite the females accounting for 52 percent of the population and outperforming the males in educational attainment, the female labour force participation remains stagnant at just 32 percent. Overall total factor productivity growth has remained negative, with millions of workers trapped in low-output agricultural and informal service jobs."
  },
