    "md5": "f8696a448b651454954f590772737099",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1972,
    "chunk_text": "Compounding this problem is the chronic underutilisation of women in the workforce. Despite the females accounting for 52 percent of the population and outperforming the males in educational attainment, the female labour force participation remains stagnant at just 32 percent. Overall total factor productivity growth has remained negative, with millions of workers trapped in low-output agricultural and informal service jobs.\n\nTo address these systemic productivity deficits, he proposed rationalising the national holiday calendar. He noted that Sri Lanka observes roughly 18 weekday public holidays alongside nine three-day weekends, creating substantial business downtime.\n\nHe proposed converting redundant public holidays into contractual leave days while raising worker compensation to incentivise output.\n\nFollowing the keynote presentations, Fernando opened the floor to the reaction panel, where a recurring frustration surfaced before the discussion even turned to specifics: the persistent gap between diagnosis and action.\n\nAlphonsus captured the exasperation, arguing that Sri Lanka\u2019s economic remedies have been common knowledge for decades without ever being enacted.\n\n\u201cWe\u2019ve heard this again and again and again for the last couple of decades. So, it\u2019s not that this is not known by everyone in the room,\u201d Alphonsus said.\n\n\u201cThe question I think we should direct our attention to during this conversation is why, despite knowing this, we haven\u2019t done anything serious about it and what we can do now.\u201d\n\nThowfeek echoed the sentiment, questioning why the widely-acknowledged structural reforms remain stalled more than three years after the crisis.\n\n\u201cWe all know that Sri Lanka has to export more. We all know that we need to open up; we need to become more competitive. But why has that not happened?\u201d she asked.\n\n\u201cThree and a half years later, we are saying this is the time to reform but why haven\u2019t these reforms taken place?\u201d"
  },
