    "md5": "685f06106d4dee286db56035965782ce",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1604,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cWe all know that Sri Lanka has to export more. We all know that we need to open up; we need to become more competitive. But why has that not happened?\u201d she asked.\n\n\u201cThree and a half years later, we are saying this is the time to reform but why haven\u2019t these reforms taken place?\u201d\n\n\u201cI think having low inflation is a good thing. High inflation is a double-edged sword. It\u2019s tempting to think of inflation as this tool through which you can inflate away your problems but that doesn\u2019t create long-term sustainability. So, I feel that if anything we should be looking at lower inflation targets,\u201d Mannakkara stated.\n\n\u201cHere, it\u2019s not about personal consumption; this is about business. Lower interest rates make investment feasible and that\u2019s what will drive dynamism in this economy.\u201d\n\nMannakkara argued that reducing the lending rates requires patience, pointing out that the borrowing costs have fallen by over 20 percentage points from their peak three years ago. He suggested that the authorities formally consider lowering the statutory inflation target to between 4 and 5 percent.\n\nJoining the discussion, Alphonsus observed that wage elasticity could provide policy flexibility, suggesting that in the absence of political will to reform the civil service, allowing inflation to compress real public sector wages might make state jobs less attractive and encourage voluntary exits. Mannakkara vehemently rejected this view, asserting that intentionally degrading public sector working conditions through inflation is an ineffective and lazy approach to state restructuring."
