    "chunk_text": "Joining the discussion, Alphonsus observed that wage elasticity could provide policy flexibility, suggesting that in the absence of political will to reform the civil service, allowing inflation to compress real public sector wages might make state jobs less attractive and encourage voluntary exits. Mannakkara vehemently rejected this view, asserting that intentionally degrading public sector working conditions through inflation is an ineffective and lazy approach to state restructuring.\n\nDr. de Mel agreed with the critique. He acknowledged that the Central Bank had failed to correctly read the transient nature of the supply shock at the time and that raising the interest rates in response, rather than easing them, had been the wrong call \u2014 a misjudgement, he said, that carried destructive knock-on effects across the broader economy.\n\nTurning to external debt, Jafferjee delivered a stark assessment of the refinancing wall awaiting Sri Lanka between 2028 and 2029, when the grace periods expire and commercial repayments resume. Jafferjee stressed that the sovereign credit ratings will exclusively determine whether the country can roll over its debt or face renewed default.\n\n\u201cThere are three people who will determine whether we will need a restructuring or not. They have names: Moody\u2019s, S&P and Fitch. They are rating agencies. Our destiny is in their hands,\u201d Jafferjee stated.\n\n\u201cS&P did a rating review and we are CCC+. Now if you take the secondary market yields of all these bonds, they\u2019re still trading at about 8.5 percent. If you go to the primary market, you\u2019re borrowing at 9 percent. The highest we have ever borrowed for international finance is about 7.4 percent.\u201d\n\nJafferjee explained that global capital markets operate on rollover assumptions rather than full capital recovery."
