    "chunk_size_bytes": 1830,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cS&P did a rating review and we are CCC+. Now if you take the secondary market yields of all these bonds, they\u2019re still trading at about 8.5 percent. If you go to the primary market, you\u2019re borrowing at 9 percent. The highest we have ever borrowed for international finance is about 7.4 percent.\u201d\n\nJafferjee explained that global capital markets operate on rollover assumptions rather than full capital recovery.\n\n\u201cWhen you are in CCC+, which is just one notch above selective default, you are on shaky grounds. If you look at our debt rollover challenges that are coming from 2028 to 2029, unless we significantly improve our rating, our ability to roll over our debt by borrowing in international markets is going to be very, very difficult.\u201d\n\nHe added that the elevated US interest rates, with the 10-year Treasury yield hovering near 4.6 percent, compound these severe refinancing hurdles.\n\nThis external vulnerability led Fernando to poll the reaction panel on whether Sri Lanka would inevitably need a subsequent IMF programme or face a second debt restructuring before 2031. The panellists dismissed the likelihood of another debt restructuring before 2031 but opinions diverged on what the future IMF engagement would look like.\n\nMannakkara questioned the motivation behind it, asking: \u201cAre you talking about an IMF programme that we are forced to go into or one that we desire to go into for governance reasons? Because they are two very different things.\u201d\n\nJafferjee drew a technical distinction between full conditional programmes and targeted financing facilities, noting: \u201cThere\u2019s a difference between an IMF programme and IMF financing. The Rapid Financing Instrument we did is a financing programme. I would advocate the same for financing from the IMF. I think a programme is quite unlikely.\u201d"
