    "md5": "09f7646dd9fe31d72e1c2e3302ab9f45",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1806,
    "chunk_text": "Jafferjee drew a technical distinction between full conditional programmes and targeted financing facilities, noting: \u201cThere\u2019s a difference between an IMF programme and IMF financing. The Rapid Financing Instrument we did is a financing programme. I would advocate the same for financing from the IMF. I think a programme is quite unlikely.\u201d\n\nHowever, Jafferjee warned that policy formulation has long been captured by concentrated cartels, creating a system run for the benefit of roughly 100 families.\n\n\u201cThe main determinant of productivity is competition. And I\u2019ve said this many times, that the country is governed for 100 families. This is a figurative statement, not a literal statement but all over the place when you look at how policies are framed, formed and implemented, it is for vested interests,\u201d Jafferjee stated.\n\n\u201cOur biggest challenge in Sri Lanka is how we are going to overcome the vested interests, who are very deep.\u201d\n\nJafferjee\u2019s earlier proposal to rationalise public holidays met immediate pushback during the panel discussion.\n\nThowfeek stated, \u201cI understand the economic argument on the holidays but as a true and proper Sri Lankan, I must vehemently oppose this idea of taking away our public holidays.\u201d\n\nThowfeek emphasised that discussions on productivity must account for severe household strain, reminding the panel that real wages have fallen by roughly 30 percent compared to the pre-crisis levels. The audience members pressed the point further from the floor, citing a World Economic Forum report on Sri Lanka\u2019s widening gender pay gap and the strain facing the public sector households earning below Rs.100,000 a month.\n\nDr. de Mel expanded this into a philosophical critique of modern economic policy, warning against confusing means with ends."
