    "chunk_size_bytes": 1802,
    "chunk_text": "Thowfeek emphasised that discussions on productivity must account for severe household strain, reminding the panel that real wages have fallen by roughly 30 percent compared to the pre-crisis levels. The audience members pressed the point further from the floor, citing a World Economic Forum report on Sri Lanka\u2019s widening gender pay gap and the strain facing the public sector households earning below Rs.100,000 a month.\n\nDr. de Mel expanded this into a philosophical critique of modern economic policy, warning against confusing means with ends.\n\n\u201cHolidays are good for better lives. We must do the productivity that Murtaza talks about and then figure out how to still live better and function better. Growth that doesn\u2019t make the lives of the majority better off ends up, like in lots of places, biting back, even on those who benefit from that kind of growth,\u201d he added.\n\nDr. de Mel also criticised the absence of official poverty tracking, pointing out that while Sri Lanka\u2019s poverty headcount doubled to nearly 30 percent during the crisis, the authorities have failed to publish systematic measures.\n\n\u201cWhat you fail to measure, you fail to focus on. All through the crisis we still have not published a poverty measure,\u201d Dr. de Mel stressed, echoing Jafferjee\u2019s earlier point on how deeply wage compression has cut into formal sector incomes.\n\nHe urged the corporate leaders to look beyond transactional lobbying and advocate for a national development plan focused on genuine human flourishing.\n\nThe debate subsequently shifted towards governance structures and capital requirements.\n\nNanayakkara questioned whether five-year democratic election cycles hinder long-term economic planning when compared to centralised Asian governance models that execute multi-decade visions."
