    "md5": "dd265ee4af5e7a07061556c7c4b8f1f9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1977,
    "chunk_text": "The debate subsequently shifted towards governance structures and capital requirements.\n\nNanayakkara questioned whether five-year democratic election cycles hinder long-term economic planning when compared to centralised Asian governance models that execute multi-decade visions.\n\nShe also disputed the premise of capital shortages, adding: \u201cI completely agree with Murtaza; we don\u2019t need capital. I\u2019m from the banking sector; there\u2019s a lot of liquidity out there and there\u2019s a lot of money, capital, which is not getting deployed where it should be deployed. We really do not need foreign capital coming in but we need that knowledge base coming in from foreign markets.\u201d\n\nMannakkara delivered a robust rebuttal, defending both foreign capital and democratic governance.\n\n\u201cWe are entering a new economy; it\u2019s really akin to the Industrial Revolution, where the growth is very capital intensive and we are seeing one of the largest phases of investment globally right now. To then imagine that Sri Lanka can somehow operate in an inter-economy ecosystem outside this global norm is crazy. So, we do need that,\u201d Mannakkara said.\n\n\u201cInternational capital also forces governance. With that capital comes knowledge anyway and international capital will help reduce interest rates domestically because of the liquidity that it brings.\u201d\n\nHe cautioned against authoritarian alternatives, adding: \u201cWhile it\u2019s tempting for us to rubbish the public sector and to question the value of democracy, I think these are really dangerous things to say. Because democracy is the ultimate safety valve. Yes, it\u2019s messy; yes, it\u2019s annoying but it\u2019s what gives us resilience.\u201d\n\nAs the forum concluded, the participants agreed that Sri Lanka must capitalise on its current stabilisation window before the debt service obligations escalate. Murtaza Jafferjee concluded by citing John F. Kennedy\u2019s adage: \u201cThe time to repair the roof is when the sun is shining.\u201d"
