    "description": "Regional leaders pledge to step up sustainable finance at UN SDG Investment Forum",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/Regional-leaders-pledge-to-step-up-sustainable-finance-at-UN-SDG-Investment-Forum/273-349098",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1788113802.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-30-dailymirrorlk-6636e8b2-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9cf5acaa52aaa025f0c1fa7f95dccc3f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1718,
    "chunk_text": "The programme then transitioned into a series of four technical deep dives, deliberately sequenced to build on one another rather than stand alone. Rebecca Kwee, Associate Director at LeapFrog Investments, opened with a case study on how impact-oriented capital is deployed across emerging Asia and what portfolio companies and local partners must demonstrate to attract it. Yixi (Wilson) Wei, Investment Manager Assistant and Chief Analyst of ESG at E Fund Management, followed with an additional case study from the investor\u2019s perspective.\n\nA facilitated Leadership Roundtable followed, bringing together investors, financial institutions, corporates and sustainability leaders to candidly discuss the barriers preventing greater capital mobilization across emerging markets.\n\nParticipants identified priority actions to strengthen collaboration, improve access to transition finance, and accelerate the development of investable projects across the Asia-Pacific region. The session concluded with participants submitting individual organizational commitments to advance sustainable finance within their institutions, with progress to be tracked through follow-up engagements over the coming year.\n\nThroughout the discussions, participants explored several priority themes shaping the future of sustainable finance, including transition finance instruments, blended finance, sustainable disclosure frameworks, internal carbon pricing and strategies for communicating sustainability in an evolving global investment landscape. These conversations reinforced that mobilizing capital at the scale required will depend on stronger partnerships between investors, businesses, policymakers, and development institutions."
  },
