    "md5": "a46d21e492bf8368f6e0e89f6a85abb3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1930,
    "chunk_text": "Colombo Port breaks into global top 20 amid Middle East shipping shake-up\n\nThe Port of Colombo has climbed to 20th place in the world\u2019s busiest container port rankings at the half-year mark of 2026, up from 26th at the end of last year, according to Alphaliner\u2019s mid-2026 top-30 global container port table.\n\nThe rise coincides with an 11.9 percent jump in the port\u2019s own first-half throughput. Colombo handled 4,444,034 TEUs between January and June 2026, up from 3,972,053 TEUs in the same period last year, Central Bank data show.\n\nTranshipment, which continues to dominate the port\u2019s business, accounted for 84.7 percent of total container volumes and grew 12.6 percent year-on-year, while domestic container traffic rose 8.3 percent. Among the port\u2019s terminals, Colombo International Container Terminals handled 1,642,647 TEUs, or 37 percent of the total.\n\nVessel traffic also strengthened. A total of 2,066 vessels called at the port in the first half, up 2.5 percent from 2,016 a year earlier, with break-bulk arrivals up 55.6 percent and calls for bunkering, ship repair and other value-added maritime services up 24.4 percent.\n\nColombo\u2019s growth rate outpaces nearly every port ranked above it in the Alphaliner table. Only India\u2019s Nhava Sheva, which grew 13.6 percent and climbed from 28th to 21st over the same period, matched the pace among Colombo\u2019s immediate neighbours in the rankings. The gains were not shared evenly across the region: Mundra, also in India, slipped from 24th to 26th despite modest growth of its own.\n\nPart of the reshuffle reflects turmoil far from South Asia. Jebel Ali, the Middle East\u2019s largest container port, fell from 10th to 32nd after the Strait of Hormuz disruption cut second-quarter volumes at the Dubai hub by more than 90 percent, while Abu Dhabi\u2019s Khalifa Seaport dropped out of the top 50 entirely. Both exits opened space in the rankings for ports below them."
