    "chunk_text": "Part of the reshuffle reflects turmoil far from South Asia. Jebel Ali, the Middle East\u2019s largest container port, fell from 10th to 32nd after the Strait of Hormuz disruption cut second-quarter volumes at the Dubai hub by more than 90 percent, while Abu Dhabi\u2019s Khalifa Seaport dropped out of the top 50 entirely. Both exits opened space in the rankings for ports below them.\n\nJohn Keells Holdings, whose consortium with India\u2019s Adani Ports operates the Colombo West International Terminal, said the terminal reached full capacity utilisation within its first year of operation.\n\nWhether the climb continues into the world\u2019s top 15, as the port has said it is working towards, is less certain on current numbers. Colombo\u2019s FY2025 volume of 8.3 million TEUs trails 15th-placed Xiamen\u2019s 12.5 million by more than four million TEUs, and even the nearest target, 19th-placed Ho Chi Minh City, holds a lead of roughly 600,000 TEUs. Projected forward at its current growth rate, Colombo would end 2026 near 9.3 million TEUs, enough to threaten 19th place, but short of the ground needed to break into the top 15 by year-end."
