    "chunk_size_bytes": 1640,
    "chunk_text": "The service, operated with a reconfigured widebody Boeing 787-8 Dreamliner, runs three times weekly year-round and introduces more than 100,000 low-fare seats annually on the corridor, establishing Sri Lanka\u2019s first low-cost direct link to Australia, though SriLankan Airlines already operates the route on a full-service basis. Jetstar\u2019s pitch is squarely on price, with promotional launch fares starting from Rs. 67,770.\n\nOf the inaugural flight, Zeglin said about 85 percent of passengers were flying point-to-point between Melbourne and Colombo, with the remaining 15 percent connecting beyond Melbourne\u2014roughly split between domestic Australia and New Zealand.\n\nOn whether Jetstar would add frequencies for the December\u2013February peak season, Zeglin was measured, noting the thrice-weekly service must first prove its business case amid global cost volatility.\n\n\u201cSince we started planning this, we\u2019ve had fuel go through the roof in terms of costs with the Middle East pieces,\u201d he said. \u201cWe definitely need to make sure we can cover those increased cost pressures on the route, but so far, loads are good, revenue is good, and we\u2019re very pleased with the performance.\u201d He added that peak-season demand \u201cis excellent, and we\u2019re really looking forward to that.\u201d\n\nTo build sustained leisure demand, Jetstar has partnered with tour operator Intrepid Travel, which brought organised tour groups on the inaugural service and ranks Sri Lanka as its fifth most popular destination for Australian travellers. The airline also hosted a delegation of Australian content creators and New Zealand journalists on the flight."
