    "md5": "22efc8ac707f184c4fae39b47a185886",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1676,
    "chunk_text": "To build sustained leisure demand, Jetstar has partnered with tour operator Intrepid Travel, which brought organised tour groups on the inaugural service and ranks Sri Lanka as its fifth most popular destination for Australian travellers. The airline also hosted a delegation of Australian content creators and New Zealand journalists on the flight.\n\nQantas Group has previously used Jetstar\u2019s low-cost network to test emerging leisure routes ahead of a possible mainline entry, and a senior Qantas Group Asset Management executive, Rohan Garnett, was part of the delegation that travelled with Zeglin.\n\nThe bilateral significance of the connection was echoed by Australian High Commissioner to Sri Lanka Matthew Duckworth, who drew parallels to the historic World War II \u201cDouble Sunrise\u201d flights operated between Perth and Koggala Lake by Qantas and the Royal Australian Air Force, noting that as both nations approach the 80th anniversary of formal diplomatic ties next year, direct air links serve as an important bridge for trade, education and two-way tourism.\n\nMJF Leisure Chair and Tourism Alliance Co-Chair Malik J. Fernando wrote in a social media post that direct services mark a structural shift for the industry.\n\n\u2018\u2018Beyond the headlines, this is about reducing our historic overreliance on Middle East transit hubs, diversifying our source markets, and attracting the kind of direct-flight traveller who spends more once they\u2019re here,\u201d Fernando wrote. \u201cSecuring these routes is only half the job\u2014it now falls to our industry to support them with sustained, targeted marketing so airlines have the confidence to stay, and ideally go year-round.\u201d"
