    "chunk_text": "\u2018\u2018Beyond the headlines, this is about reducing our historic overreliance on Middle East transit hubs, diversifying our source markets, and attracting the kind of direct-flight traveller who spends more once they\u2019re here,\u201d Fernando wrote. \u201cSecuring these routes is only half the job\u2014it now falls to our industry to support them with sustained, targeted marketing so airlines have the confidence to stay, and ideally go year-round.\u201d\n\nZeglin remarked\u00a0 that Jetstar is open to joint promotional activities with Sri Lankan tourism authorities to build on the route\u2019s early momentum. SLTPB already runs a similar tie-up with Emirates and several other airlines under a memorandum of understanding first signed in 2022 and renewed through 2027, covering joint destination marketing and familiarisation trips for travel trade and media.\n\nA senior Jetstar and Qantas delegation\u2014including Zeglin and Garnett, alongside Hayleys Advantis Deputy Managing Director Shano Sabar, whose company serves as Jetstar\u2019s General Sales Agent (GSA) in Sri Lanka\u2014also met Minister of Ports and Civil Aviation Anura Karunatilaka, Ministry Secretary W.W.S. Mangala, and Civil Aviation Authority of Sri Lanka (CAASL) Director General and CEO Capt. Daminda Rambukwella, to discuss passenger facilitation, terminal handling capacity and potential frequency growth.\n\nCapt. Rambukwella also proposed that Jetstar evaluate future operations to Mattala Rajapaksa International Airport (MRIA) to leverage the country\u2019s regional airport network. (NF)"
