    "chunk_size_bytes": 1491,
    "chunk_text": "Engine displacement tax regime distorts automotive market, restricting hybrid choices and favouring EVs\n\nJB Securities highlights systemic bias against 2.0-litre Japanese hybrids despite proven secondary market reliability\n\nElectric passenger vehicle registrations remain historically elevated, driven heavily by Chinese manufacturer BYD\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s engine displacement-based automotive taxation framework is creating severe market distortions, effectively penalising globally acclaimed, highly efficient hybrid vehicles while disproportionately favouring electric vehicle-first architectures, according to leading equities and research firm JB Securities.\n\nThe current fiscal structure restricts consumer choice across the domestic market, systematically shutting out world-renowned fuel-efficient models with established secondary market reliability while accelerating the influx of newer battery-electric and extended-range powertrains.\n\nJB Securities Chief Executive Officer Murtaza Jafferjee pointed out that despite widespread consumer demand for proven Japanese hybrid engineering, Sri Lankan buyers face restricted access to benchmark models due to punitive duty structures on internal combustion engines exceeding 1,500cc. Total hybrid vehicle registrations declined to 2,288 units in July from 3,023 units in June, with sports utility vehicles accounting for virtually all hybrid volume, led by Toyota, Honda, and BYD\u2019s expanding presence through the Sealion and Denza ranges."
