    "md5": "d7adf6b9af91ad9ad2b58be9f05d8acf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1881,
    "chunk_text": "JB Securities Chief Executive Officer Murtaza Jafferjee pointed out that despite widespread consumer demand for proven Japanese hybrid engineering, Sri Lankan buyers face restricted access to benchmark models due to punitive duty structures on internal combustion engines exceeding 1,500cc. Total hybrid vehicle registrations declined to 2,288 units in July from 3,023 units in June, with sports utility vehicles accounting for virtually all hybrid volume, led by Toyota, Honda, and BYD\u2019s expanding presence through the Sealion and Denza ranges.\n\nThis regulatory disparity has profound implications for secondary market economics and long-term vehicle residual values. Traditionally, Japanese full-hybrid powertrains from manufacturers like Toyota and Honda have demonstrated unmatched secondary market value retention in Sri Lanka, supported by extensive parts availability, modular battery repairs, and trusted long-term durability. In contrast, battery electric vehicles and complex plug-in powertrains often face steeper depreciation curves in second-hand markets. This vulnerability is largely driven by consumer concerns over long-term battery degradation, expensive out-of-warranty traction battery replacements, and the rapid pace of technological obsolescence from fast-iterating automotive manufacturers.\n\nDespite these secondary market considerations, the existing fiscal bias has underpinned strong demand for electric mobility solutions. In the four-wheeler passenger segment, pure electric motor car registrations reached 992 units in July, with Chinese electric vehicle giant BYD maintaining an overwhelming dominance by accounting for 801 units, while BAW and Wuling followed distantly with 63 and 53 units, respectively. In the electric SUV space, 433 units were registered, with BYD securing 314 of those deliveries, primarily through its Atto crossover lineup."
