    "chunk_text": "Despite these secondary market considerations, the existing fiscal bias has underpinned strong demand for electric mobility solutions. In the four-wheeler passenger segment, pure electric motor car registrations reached 992 units in July, with Chinese electric vehicle giant BYD maintaining an overwhelming dominance by accounting for 801 units, while BAW and Wuling followed distantly with 63 and 53 units, respectively. In the electric SUV space, 433 units were registered, with BYD securing 314 of those deliveries, primarily through its Atto crossover lineup.\n\nAutomotive industry analysts argue that if Sri Lanka aims to foster genuine decarbonisation without introducing structural distortions or asset-value risks for consumers, the government must transition from an outdated engine capacity tax formula to a holistic emissions-based or lifecycle carbon taxation system. Under an equitable fiscal regime, cutting-edge 2.0-litre hybrid powertrains\u2014which offer proven efficiency and robust resale value without relying on national grid charging infrastructure\u2014could compete fairly against full-electric models on the showroom floor. (NF)"
