    "md5": "7cdcf10acd1e7ef60a95f0f5921f3ac4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1907,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka crosses 1.5mn tourist arrival milestone, eyes 2.5mn and 2027 breakthrough\n\nCumulative arrivals cross 1.51mn by late August; SLTDA chief confident of topping 2.5mn on winter surge\n\nStrategic pivot sets 2027 as breakthrough year, prioritising revenue yields and sustainable, experience-based tourism over volume\n\nInfrastructure push and niche product development underway with a special focus on Northern and Eastern provinces ahead of April global campaign\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s cumulative tourist arrivals crossed the 1.5 million mark this year, reaching 1,517,000 by the evening of August 27, newly appointed Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority (SLTDA) Chairman Suranjith Wevita said, even as arrivals remain around 2 percent below last year\u2019s levels amid fallout from the ongoing conflict in West Asia.\n\nSpeaking to Mirror Business, Wevita said he expects the country to comfortably surpass the 2.5 million arrivals mark by the end of the year. The final four months, coinciding with the start of the peak winter season, are expected to drive the bulk of the growth needed to hit that target. Reaching 2.5 million would mean adding close to one million arrivals over that period. While the monthly average recorded so far this year is approximately 190,000, arrivals typically move closer to the 300,000 mark during the peak months of November and December.\n\nWevita noted that while broader European inbound travel has faced headwinds from the escalating West Asia conflict, the downturn was particularly visible in the Netherlands during July and August\u2014the traditional peak summer holiday period for Dutch travellers. Heightened apprehension over flights transiting Middle Eastern airspace, alongside flight rerouting and suspensions by major European carriers such as KLM and Air France since January, has driven up travel times and costs across key corridors connecting Europe to Sri Lanka."
