    "chunk_text": "Wevita noted that while broader European inbound travel has faced headwinds from the escalating West Asia conflict, the downturn was particularly visible in the Netherlands during July and August\u2014the traditional peak summer holiday period for Dutch travellers. Heightened apprehension over flights transiting Middle Eastern airspace, alongside flight rerouting and suspensions by major European carriers such as KLM and Air France since January, has driven up travel times and costs across key corridors connecting Europe to Sri Lanka.\n\nLooking beyond this year, Wevita described 2027 as a potential breakthrough year for Sri Lankan tourism, driven by a strategic pivot from merely chasing arrival numbers to actively maximizing revenue yields. To realize this shift, he noted the SLTDA will concentrate heavily on developing essential infrastructure and laying the structural groundwork required to support premium, experience-based, and sustainably branded offerings. He said this push builds on an interim marketing campaign already under way, ahead of a full-scale global promotional rollout scheduled to launch next April.\n\nA central plank of that revenue-driven strategy, Wevita said, is broadening tourism income beyond the traditional southern coastal belt and Colombo, with the Northern and Eastern provinces a particular focus under his leadership. He said feasibility studies for a US$ 100 million World Bank-backed project have now been completed, alongside similar groundwork by other multilateral agencies, paving the way for Sri Lanka to roll out new experience-based tourism products targeting niche market segments as early as next year.\n\nThe push aligns with World Bank\u2019s wider engagement in the two provinces, including its REVIVE programme covering Jaffna, Pasikuda, Trincomalee and Arugam Bay under the government\u2019s Tourism Strategic Plan 2026-2030, and a separate US$ 100 million loan facility the Cabinet approved in August for broader regional development."
