    "language": "en",
    "md5": "191f7d41ca902230d1851a00a5fc6323",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1784,
    "chunk_text": "We cannot afford to import our health security\n\nSri Lanka has learnt many painful lessons from depending too heavily on the outside world. Fuel shortages taught us about energy security. Food shortages reminded us about agricultural security. The economic crisis showed us the dangers of depending on foreign exchange to pay for almost everything we need.\n\nBut there is another area where Sri Lanka remains heavily dependent on imports - medicines.\n\nAround 85 percent of Sri Lanka\u2019s pharmaceutical requirements are still met through imports. The local pharmaceutical market was estimated at around US$750 million in 2025, meaning hundreds of millions of dollars are spent every year bringing medicines into the country.\n\nIndia is naturally one of our most important suppliers. Its pharmaceutical industry is enormous, competitive and capable of producing medicines at prices Sri Lanka often cannot match locally.\n\nThere is nothing wrong with buying medicines from India. But the question we should be asking is whether 85 percent dependence is sensible for a country of 22 million people.\n\nThe answer should be no. The issue becomes particularly serious when there are failures in the international supply chain. A medicine may be inexpensive and readily available, but if quality assurance fails somewhere between manufacturing, procurement, shipping and distribution, the consequences can be serious.\n\nIn 2023, the National Medicines Regulatory Authority admitted that several imported medicines had failed quality tests after being brought into the country under registration waivers. Among them was prednisolone acetate ophthalmic suspension. The product was withdrawn after samples failed sterility testing, while hospitals reported cases of eye inflammation following its use."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-31-dailymirrorlk-03746860",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-03746860",
