    "description": "We cannot afford to import our health security",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/We-cannot-afford-to-import-our-health-security/172-349110",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1788116975.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-31-dailymirrorlk-03746860-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6f8ffc81ec86ab19ed1ed60ca3493617",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1852,
    "chunk_text": "In 2023, the National Medicines Regulatory Authority admitted that several imported medicines had failed quality tests after being brought into the country under registration waivers. Among them was prednisolone acetate ophthalmic suspension. The product was withdrawn after samples failed sterility testing, while hospitals reported cases of eye inflammation following its use.\n\nWhy are we not producing more of our own essential medicines?\n\nThe country already has the beginnings of a pharmaceutical manufacturing industry. The State Pharmaceuticals Manufacturing Corporation has experience producing medicines, while private sector manufacturers have also entered the market. What is missing is scale, investment, research and a long term national strategy.\n\nThe government should treat pharmaceutical manufacturing as a strategic industry rather than simply another manufacturing sector.\n\nSri Lanka spends significant foreign exchange importing medicines. Part of that money could be redirected towards modern manufacturing facilities, pharmaceutical research and training scientists, pharmacists, engineers and technicians. Sri Lanka does not have to manufacture every medicine in the world. We should identify medicines that are used in large quantities and can realistically be produced locally. The goal should not simply be import substitution.\n\nThe goal should eventually be exports. Sri Lanka sits in the middle of a region with hundreds of millions of potential consumers. South Asia has a massive demand for affordable generic medicines. There are already encouraging signs. In 2026, Zydus Lifesciences and Sunshine Healthcare announced a joint venture involving an investment of more than US$20 million to establish a pharmaceutical manufacturing facility in Sri Lanka. This is precisely the type of investment Sri Lanka should encourage."
  },
