    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e565bb30d40d9357bcfbb11a09d3311d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1140,
    "chunk_text": "The goal should eventually be exports. Sri Lanka sits in the middle of a region with hundreds of millions of potential consumers. South Asia has a massive demand for affordable generic medicines. There are already encouraging signs. In 2026, Zydus Lifesciences and Sunshine Healthcare announced a joint venture involving an investment of more than US$20 million to establish a pharmaceutical manufacturing facility in Sri Lanka. This is precisely the type of investment Sri Lanka should encourage.\n\nWe therefore need both stronger regulation and stronger local production. Medicine is not just another import. It is a matter of national security. A country that cannot guarantee a reliable supply of essential medicines during a foreign exchange crisis, international emergency or disruption to global supply chains remains vulnerable.\n\nSri Lanka should move from being a country that largely buys medicines from the region to a country that makes medicines for the region.\n\nOur people deserve medicines that are affordable, accessible and, above all, safe.\n\nHealth security should not begin at the port when a shipment of medicine arrives."
