    "chunk_text": "A resilient constitutional democracy requires a Parliament with genuine oversight powers rather than a rubber stamp; independent commissions protected from executive interference; a professional public service and police accountable to law rather than political patronage; a free and independent media capable of exposing abuses of power; an independent legal profession; and a vibrant civil society willing to challenge those in authority. There is one final dimension Sri Lanka cannot afford to ignore: power-sharing. A state that concentrates authority in one office is not only vulnerable to authoritarianism. It is also vulnerable to majoritarianism. In a deeply plural society, unchecked central power can become power exercised in the interests of the majority community at the expense of minorities.\n\nMeaningful devolution and power-sharing are therefore not concessions to particular communities. They are constitutional safeguards, essential for national unity, equality and lasting peace.\n\nDemocracy gives us the right to choose who governs us. Constitutionalism ensures that those we choose cannot govern us without limits. Sri Lanka has learned this lesson repeatedly. The tragedy would be to learn it yet again and still refuse to act."
