    "md5": "8ac6fa8ba15b134e095a46e96d25f9d6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1791,
    "chunk_text": "Maha 2026/2027: Why delaying cultivation until December may be safer choice\n\nFarmers could begin land preparation and planting following the first significant rainfall. But if the subsequent rainfall fails, the reservoirs may not contain sufficient water to sustain the crops through their critical growth stages. This could result in widespread crop losses after farmers have already invested heavily in land preparation, seed, fertiliser, labour and other inputs\n\nDelaying Maha cultivation until November or December should not be seen as a failure to cultivate. It should be viewed as a prudent water-management strategy \u2013 effectively an insurance policy against an uncertain climate year\n\nIn water management, a timely warning can be worth far more than a post-disaster response. Sri Lanka is now facing a situation where decisions taken in the coming weeks could determine whether farmers have adequate water to complete the 2026/27 Maha season.\n\nThe Irrigation Department has already urged farmers and the public to use irrigation and drinking water carefully as the country faces a heightened risk of drought and rising temperatures associated with the expected El Ni\u00f1o pattern. With reservoir storage falling rapidly, the timing of Maha cultivation deserves careful reconsideration.\n\nThe Pacific Ocean is currently exceptionally warm. According to NOAA, the Ni\u00f1o 3.4 region has recorded a very high positive sea-surface temperature anomaly, while subsurface waters are also significantly warmer than average. The International Research Institute (IRI) has indicated the possibility of a very strong El Ni\u00f1o developing during October-December 2026. Sri Lanka\u2019s Department of Meteorology has also indicated that El Ni\u00f1o conditions could continue into the first quarter of 2027."
  },
