    "md5": "b50a23924c5434e9df9a531059e4216f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1982,
    "chunk_text": "The Pacific Ocean is currently exceptionally warm. According to NOAA, the Ni\u00f1o 3.4 region has recorded a very high positive sea-surface temperature anomaly, while subsurface waters are also significantly warmer than average. The International Research Institute (IRI) has indicated the possibility of a very strong El Ni\u00f1o developing during October-December 2026. Sri Lanka\u2019s Department of Meteorology has also indicated that El Ni\u00f1o conditions could continue into the first quarter of 2027.\n\nThe important point is that this is not a normal year. There is considerable uncertainty over the behaviour of both the Second Inter-Monsoon (SIM) and Northeast Monsoon (NEM).\n\nThe situation is particularly worrying because reservoir storage has fallen substantially from the levels seen at the end of 2025. Most of the reservoirs that were overflowing in December 2025 are have fallen to about 40% of full capacity level.\n\nAccording to reports published on 26 August, storage in 74 major reservoirs managed by the Irrigation Department stood at about 1,182,385 acre-feet, or 1,465 million cubic metres, equivalent to only about 35% of their combined gross capacity of 3,114,407 acre-feet (3,843 million cubic metres). Reservoirs in the Ampara District had particularly low storage, at around 22%, while Senanayake Samudraya was reported to have only about 18%.\n\nThe Irrigation Department operates or oversees around 340 irrigation schemes covering approximately 306,000 hectares, representing a major share of Sri Lanka\u2019s irrigated agriculture.\n\nAt the end of July, the Department reported that only around 40% of the capacity of the major reservoirs remained. This represents a sharp decline from early June, when storage was around 63%.\n\nThis rapid depletion should be treated as a serious warning.\n\nStatus of Water Storage as of 14 August 2026 in Mahaweli Reservoirs\n\nThe Mahaweli system is equally important, as its reservoirs support both irrigation and hydropower generation."
  },
