    "chunk_size_bytes": 1985,
    "chunk_text": "This rapid depletion should be treated as a serious warning.\n\nStatus of Water Storage as of 14 August 2026 in Mahaweli Reservoirs\n\nThe Mahaweli system is equally important, as its reservoirs support both irrigation and hydropower generation.\n\nAs of 14 August 2026, Victoria contained about 494 million cubic metres, equivalent to around 60% of its gross capacity, while Randenigala stood at 55%. Moragahakanda had 61%, Kotmale 94% and Rantambe 77%.\n\nMore worrying for the North Central Province is the declining storage in downstream reservoirs such as Kalawewa, which had only 33 million cubic metres, or 32% of capacity. Nachchaduwa was at 37%, Minneriya 39% and Kaudulla only 28%.\n\nThese reservoirs cannot be considered as isolated water bodies. Water released through upstream regulating reservoirs such as Kotmale, Victoria and Randenigala in the Mahaweli system has to serve several downstream reservoirs and irrigation systems. Therefore, the apparent availability of water in a few upstream reservoirs does not mean that the same quantity is readily available for the entire cultivation area, particularly when many downstream reservoirs are already at relatively low storage levels.\n\nMinor village irrigation systems, comprising schemes with command areas of less than 80 hectares, are managed by the Department of Agrarian Development. The command area of these minor tanks across the island extends over roughly 185,000 hectares.\n\nDue to the prolonged drought, many of these tanks now have little or no water, creating tremendous hardships for rural communities. Domestic and agricultural wells located close to the minor reservoirs provide water for highland cultivation and drinking purposes. Villagers also depend heavily on these tanks for bathing, livestock and other domestic needs.\n\nThe declining water levels in these small tanks are therefore not merely an agricultural concern. They are directly affecting the day-to-day lives and livelihoods of rural communities."
