    "chunk_text": "Due to the prolonged drought, many of these tanks now have little or no water, creating tremendous hardships for rural communities. Domestic and agricultural wells located close to the minor reservoirs provide water for highland cultivation and drinking purposes. Villagers also depend heavily on these tanks for bathing, livestock and other domestic needs.\n\nThe declining water levels in these small tanks are therefore not merely an agricultural concern. They are directly affecting the day-to-day lives and livelihoods of rural communities.\n\nThese are welcome measures. However, an important question now arises: should Maha cultivation be started in October based largely on the expectation of substantial rainfall in November?\n\nFarmers could begin land preparation and planting following the first significant rainfall. But if the subsequent rainfall fails, the reservoirs may not contain sufficient water to sustain the crops through their critical growth stages. This could result in widespread crop losses after farmers have already invested heavily in land preparation, seed, fertiliser, labour and other inputs.\n\nNo meteorological agency can guarantee that the Southwest Monsoon (SWM) and Northeast Monsoon (NEM) will deliver normal rainfall this year. Therefore, cultivation decisions should not be based solely on the expectation of heavy rainfall in November.\n\nGiven the declining storage levels in many reservoirs, a more cautious and flexible approach to the timing of Maha cultivation may be necessary. The Government should closely monitor rainfall, reservoir storage and short-term weather forecasts before making final decisions on the extent and timing of cultivation.\n\nMy recommendation, based on long experience in irrigation water management, is to delay the main Maha cultivation program until November or, preferably, early December, depending on rainfall and reservoir storage."
