    "chunk_text": "My recommendation, based on long experience in irrigation water management, is to delay the main Maha cultivation program until November or, preferably, early December, depending on rainfall and reservoir storage.\n\nthe likely water available for the remainder of the Maha season.\n\nIf storage remains inadequate, cultivation can be restricted to areas where water availability is reasonably assured. If storage improves sufficiently, farmers could be encouraged to cultivate shorter-duration rice varieties.\n\nA December planting does not necessarily threaten the following Yala season. With appropriate short-aged varieties, harvesting could be completed by around March, allowing Yala cultivation to commence as early as April 2027 where water availability permits.\n\nThe temptation in agriculture is always to respond to the first rainfall. But rainfall at the beginning of the Maha season does not guarantee rainfall throughout the crop cycle.\n\nStarting cultivation over large areas simply because substantial November rainfall is expected could create a dangerous situation. If the expected rainfall does not materialise, farmers will face the consequences while the authorities will have limited water to rescue already-established crops.\n\nThe current reservoir situation therefore calls for water-first, rainfall-second planning.\n\nSri Lanka has experienced years when reservoirs were overflowing and water was abundant. The present situation is very different. With strong El Ni\u00f1o conditions possible, rapidly declining reservoir storage and uncertainty over the coming monsoon, caution is essential.\n\nDelaying Maha cultivation until November or December should not be seen as a failure to cultivate. It should be viewed as a prudent water-management strategy \u2013 effectively an insurance policy against an uncertain climate year."
