    "description": "ADB\u2019s $ 12.5 m loan to fund 300,000 smart meters to modernise power grid",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/business/ADB-s-12-5-m-loan-to-fund-300-000-smart-meters-to-modernise-power-grid/34-796593",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1788137401.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-31-dailyftlk-6a053a0f-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8a88e4cd7a337165a36ede66a8ebb2e7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1728,
    "chunk_text": "ADB\u2019s $ 12.5 m loan to fund 300,000 smart meters to modernise power grid\n\nThe Cabinet of Ministers has approved a $ 12.5 million smart-meter rollout aimed at modernising the country\u2019s electricity distribution network and introducing Time-of-Use (TOU) billing, using the remaining proceeds of a $ 15 million Asian Development Bank (ADB) loan.\n\nCabinet Spokesman and Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa said the project will see 300,000 smart meters installed over a two-year period by Electricity Distribution Lanka Ltd., as part of the country\u2019s broader smart-grid modernisation program.\n\nOn 21 October 2024, the Cabinet of Ministers originally approved the $ 15 million ADB facility, as financing for complementary projects to strengthen Sri Lanka\u2019s renewable energy programme. Thereafter, the Cabinet meeting held on 3 September 2025 approved the use of $ 2.5 million from the loan to prepare the detailed design and procurement documents for the Maha Oya Pumped Storage Power Plant Development Project.\n\nThis led to the proposal to implement a project to install 300,000 smart-meters with a TOU billing system as part of the core program for modernising the country\u2019s smart grids, utilising the remaining $ 12.5 million.\n\nThe two-year implementation period will make the scale of deployment a key test, with the initiative requiring the installation of an average of about 150,000 smart meters a year.\n\nThe move creates the potential to encourage electricity users to shift discretionary use away from peak periods, reducing pressure on the grid and improving utilisation of available generation capacity.\n\nThe project also fits in with the Government\u2019s wider push to integrate renewable energy into the national grid."
  },
