    "chunk_size_bytes": 1819,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka closely monitors Iranian-flagged vessels near EEZ\n\nColombo, Aug. 31 (Daily Mirror) - The Sri Lankan Government is closely monitoring several Iranian-flagged vessels anchored off the country\u2019s coast near the Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ), while authorities have clarified that the ships remain outside Sri Lanka\u2019s territorial waters and are therefore not subject to intervention under the country\u2019s jurisdiction.\n\nThe spokesman for the Sri Lanka Navy said the Navy and Sri Lanka Air Force are maintaining close surveillance of the vessels. However, he stressed that the ships are located outside Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereign territorial waters, meaning the Government is neither responsible for them nor providing port facilities or other assistance.\n\nAccording to maritime tracking service TankerTrackers, nearly 20 Iranian fuel tankers, including around 13 Iranian-flagged vessels, are currently gathered off Sri Lanka\u2019s coast. Several other vessels have reportedly been identified as part of a so-called \u201cshadow fleet\u201d allegedly used to circumvent international sanctions.\n\nThe vessels are reportedly empty and have been unable to return to Iranian ports amid an ongoing U.S. naval blockade affecting Iranian ports.\n\nThe presence of the vessels has attracted increased attention amid concerns over maritime security, particularly following the sinking of the Irish-flagged Dena vessel approximately 19 nautical miles off Sri Lanka\u2019s coast in March.\n\nCommander Chaminda Walakuluge said that foreign vessels operating beyond the country\u2019s territorial waters are entitled to navigate international sea lanes in accordance with international maritime law.\n\nHe said the vessels in question remain beyond the 12-nautical-mile territorial limit and within areas where Sri Lanka has limited jurisdiction."
  },
