    "chunk_size_bytes": 1563,
    "chunk_text": "Commander Chaminda Walakuluge said that foreign vessels operating beyond the country\u2019s territorial waters are entitled to navigate international sea lanes in accordance with international maritime law.\n\nHe said the vessels in question remain beyond the 12-nautical-mile territorial limit and within areas where Sri Lanka has limited jurisdiction.\n\nDespite having no legal basis to arbitrarily stop or obstruct the vessels, the Sri Lanka Navy continues to maintain 24-hour Maritime Domain Awareness and surveillance across the country's maritime zones using naval patrol craft, the spokesman said.\n\nThe spokesman also said that Sri Lanka's enforcement powers in the contiguous zone are limited to specific matters, including customs, fiscal, immigration and environmental regulations, as well as certain unlawful activities such as illicit ship-to-ship transfers. He said no illegal activity or regulatory violation involving the vessels has so far been detected or reported.\n\nSri Lankan authorities have also clarified the country's position regarding international sanctions. Commander Walakuluge said Sri Lanka is legally bound by sanctions imposed by the United Nations, but unilateral sanctions imposed by individual countries do not automatically create a legal obligation for Sri Lanka to take action against foreign vessels.\n\nIn the absence of a violation of UN sanctions or any unlawful activity occurring within Sri Lankan jurisdiction, the spokesman said there is currently no legal requirement or basis for Sri Lanka to intervene against the vessels."
