    "md5": "18a9eb3f85efcf6a2125cb273ced7465",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1699,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka ranks third globally for most public holidays\n\nColombo, Aug. 31 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka has the third-highest number of public holidays of any country in the world, according to a new analysis from the Pew Research Center, behind only Myanmar and Bangladesh.\n\nThe report, published February 12, found that Sri Lanka will observe 25 public holidays in 2026, well above the global median of 13 across the 190 UN member states surveyed. Myanmar topped the list with 30 holidays, followed by Bangladesh with 29. Cambodia, Iran and Lebanon also passed 20.\n\nAt the other end, Switzerland recognizes just one federal holiday, though its cantons observe at least eight each. Bosnia and Herzegovina and Uruguay were close behind, with four and five holidays respectively.\n\nPew researchers attributed Sri Lanka's high count mainly to its practice of declaring every full moon day, or \"Poya,\" a public holiday. As a predominantly Buddhist nation with a calendar built around lunar cycles, Sri Lanka is grouped with Myanmar as one of two Buddhist-majority countries with many holidays tied to the lunar calendar, though Myanmar's total comes from a broader mix of Buddhist, ethnic and state holidays rather than the same every-full-moon rule. Countries with large Buddhist or Muslim populations, the researchers noted, tend to observe more public holidays overall.\n\nThe findings draw on data from Time and Date's global calendar database, checked against national labour codes, government notices and regional news reports.\n\nPew counted only nationally recognized, non-working public holidays, leaving out optional days, half-days and anything observed only at the state, provincial or local level."
  },
