    "md5": "9354249f74ed40c1db645bf9776eaf00",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1770,
    "chunk_text": "The findings draw on data from Time and Date's global calendar database, checked against national labour codes, government notices and regional news reports.\n\nPew counted only nationally recognized, non-working public holidays, leaving out optional days, half-days and anything observed only at the state, provincial or local level.\n\nDeepavali coincides with the festival as celebrated in India, Nepal and other countries with large Hindu communities. Vesak, marking the Buddha's birth, enlightenment and passing, is also a public holiday in Myanmar, Thailand and Cambodia, though the exact date can shift by a day depending on which lunar calendar a country follows.\n\nIt is the Poya days that set Sri Lanka apart. Most Buddhist-majority countries mark Vesak, but few besides Sri Lanka treat every full moon as a nonworking holiday, which is the main reason its total climbs so far above what most other nations, Buddhist-majority or otherwise, observe.\n\nNine long weekends already on the 2027 calendar for Sri Lanka\n\nNext year's calendar is already locked in, and it rewards planning ahead. The Public Administration, Provincial Councils and Local Government Ministry gazetted the 2027 schedule this month under the Holidays Act No. 29 of 1971, listing 25 public and bank holidays, matching Sri Lanka's count in the Pew analysis for 2026.\n\nNine of those holidays fall on a Friday or a Monday, stretching an ordinary weekend into three days off. It happens twice in January alone, on the 15th and the 22nd, and again in March, May, June, August, October and December.\n\nThe best-placed cluster comes in May. The Eid ul-Adha (Hajj Festival) lands on a Monday and Vesak spills into Wednesday and Thursday, leaving just two working days in the whole third week of the month."
