    "time_ut": 1788169201.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-31-dailymirrorlk-be12eced-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "53f29c291462e90cb7d2476a55fd5a81",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1436,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka faces heightened Maha season risks from El Ni\u00f1o, high costs\n\nSri Lanka is facing an elevated risk of disruption to food production during the upcoming Maha cultivation season due to the combined effects of strengthening El Ni\u00f1o conditions, rising input costs and the possibility of heavy rainfall, according to a new United Nations report.\n\nThe Asia-Pacific regional assessment has placed Sri Lanka among countries of **\u201celevated concern\u201d**, warning that above-average rainfall is possible from October with the onset of the Second Inter-Monsoon.\n\nWhile increased rainfall could help replenish reservoirs and improve water availability for agriculture, the report cautioned that excessive rain during the Maha planting period could trigger flooding and waterlogging, while also disrupting and delaying cultivation activities.\n\nThe report further warned that elevated fuel and fertilizer costs could compound the impact on agricultural production and food security.\n\nDiesel and petrol prices in Sri Lanka remain around **44% and 41% higher**, respectively, than their levels before the Middle East crisis. The country also remains vulnerable to fluctuations in imported fertilizer costs and international wheat prices.\n\nThe assessment was jointly prepared by the **Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO), World Food Programme (WFP), UN Women, World Meteorological Organization (WMO)** and other UN humanitarian agencies."
  },
