    "chunk_size_bytes": 1949,
    "chunk_text": "As governor sees stability people waiting for Godot?\n\nColombo, Aug. 31 (Daily mirror) - Stability Is Real. Recovery Is Not Complete. The Next Battle Is Investment, Exports, Jobs and Prosperity.\n\nSri Lanka has come a long way from the economic abyss of 2022. The queues have disappeared. Foreign exchange is available. Inflation has been brought under control. Government revenue has strengthened. Fiscal balances have improved. Debt restructuring has provided breathing space. Economic growth has returned.\n\nGovernor was therefore justified last week in highlighting the turnaround and stating that the economy has recovered to almost the size it was before the crisis.\n\nBut there is an important distinction that Sri Lanka must not ignore.\n\nThe economy has stabilized. That does not mean the country has fully recovered. There is a fundamental difference between stopping an economic collapse and rebuilding national prosperity.\n\nIf recovery means ending fuel queues, restoring foreign-exchange availability, controlling inflation and bringing order back to public finances, Sri Lanka has achieved an impressive turnaround.\n\nBut recovery means restoring household purchasing power, creating quality employment, attracting large-scale investment, expanding industries, accelerating exports and giving young Sri Lankans confidence to build their futures at home, then the job is far from finished.\n\nThe World Bank itself has described Sri Lanka\u2019s recovery as \u201cuneven and incomplete\u201d, noting that poverty remained significantly elevated and that many households had still not regained livelihoods lost during the crisis.\n\nThis distinction matters. An economy is not merely a balance sheet at the Central Bank.\n\nIt is the factory owner deciding whether to expand. It is the entrepreneur deciding whether to invest. It is the exporter fighting to win another international market. It is the graduate deciding whether to stay in Sri Lanka or leave."
  },
  {
