    "language": "en",
    "md5": "bd74760382be27b58eabfb423b99e228",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1995,
    "chunk_text": "It is the factory owner deciding whether to expand. It is the entrepreneur deciding whether to invest. It is the exporter fighting to win another international market. It is the graduate deciding whether to stay in Sri Lanka or leave.\n\nThis is perhaps the biggest misconception surrounding the recovery. Lower inflation is unquestionably good news. But lower inflation does not mean that prices have returned to where they were before the crisis. It simply means that prices are increasing more slowly. The damage caused by the earlier explosion in living costs does not disappear because today\u2019s inflation rate is lower.\n\nThe World Bank noted that food prices more than doubled between 2021 and 2024, while real wages remained below their 2019 levels.\n\nThat is why ordinary Sri Lankans may hear impressive macroeconomic statistics while still asking a simple question:\n\nIf the economy has recovered, why doesn\u2019t my household feel recovered? That is not cynicism. It is economics. The ultimate measure of recovery must be purchasing power, not simply inflation.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s debt restructuring was essential. Without it, sustainable recovery would have been virtually impossible. But restructuring debt does not make debt disappear. The country still carries a heavy debt burden and substantial future financing obligations. Sri Lanka therefore cannot return to the economic model that repeatedly produced vulnerability.\n\nOtherwise, Sri Lanka will have treated the symptoms of the crisis without curing the disease that caused it.\n\nSri Lanka now urgently needs productive domestic and foreign capital\u2014not merely speculative property development or consumption-driven investment.\n\nEvery unnecessary approval, contradictory regulation, bureaucratic delay and unpredictable tax decision has an economic cost.\n\nInternational investors have choices. Vietnam wants their money. India wants it. Bangladesh wants it. Indonesia wants it. Malaysia wants it. African economies increasingly want it."
  },
