    "chunk_size_bytes": 1949,
    "chunk_text": "Every unnecessary approval, contradictory regulation, bureaucratic delay and unpredictable tax decision has an economic cost.\n\nInternational investors have choices. Vietnam wants their money. India wants it. Bangladesh wants it. Indonesia wants it. Malaysia wants it. African economies increasingly want it.\n\nInvestors do not wait for countries. Countries compete for investors.\n\nThat means Sri Lanka needs a genuine one-stop investment mechanism with real decision-making authority . Not another office that merely redirects investors from ministry to ministry.\n\nAn investor should not need political connections simply to navigate bureaucracy.\n\nForeign Direct Investment must become one of Sri Lanka\u2019s principal engines of growth.\n\nAnd this is where the country\u2019s sovereign credit rating matters.\n\nS&P Global Ratings affirmed Sri Lanka\u2019s long- and short-term sovereign ratings at CCC+/C with a stable outlook in July. Although S&P simultaneously upgraded its country-risk assessment for Sri Lanka from \u201cvery high risk\u201d to \u201chigh risk\u201d, CCC+ remains deep within speculative-grade territory.\n\nThis is not merely a Treasury problem. It is an investment problem. A weak sovereign rating affects how international lenders, investment committees and global corporations assess country risk. It can raise financing costs, increase required returns and make otherwise attractive projects harder to finance.\n\nSri Lanka therefore needs to treat improvement of its sovereign rating as a national investment priority.\n\nFiscal discipline, debt reduction, stronger reserves, credible institutions, policy consistency and structural reform are not simply IMF requirements. They are the foundations upon which investor confidence is built.\n\nToday, some of leading companies carry stronger credit ratings / profiles than the sovereign itself. That is both a tribute to the resilience of our private sector and a warning about the state of our economy."
