    "chunk_size_bytes": 1397,
    "chunk_text": "Today, some of leading companies carry stronger credit ratings / profiles than the sovereign itself. That is both a tribute to the resilience of our private sector and a warning about the state of our economy.\n\nA weak sovereign rating becomes an invisible tax on every credible Sri Lankan company seeking international capital. Higher borrowing costs, restricted financing and lost investment opportunities.\n\nSri Lanka cannot build a globally competitive economy while its strongest companies are forced to carry the burden of sovereign weakness. Fixing the sovereign rating is not about borrowing more it is about allowing the entire economy to breathe, invest and grow.\n\nOur companies should not have to pay the price for the sovereign\u2019s weaker credit standing. Rebuilding Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereign rating is therefore not merely a government financing issue; it is fundamental to unlocking private-sector growth, attracting FDI and restoring the country\u2019s competitiveness in glo\n\nWe were spending foreign exchange faster than we were sustainably earning it.\n\nTourism and worker remittances are enormously important. But a modern economy cannot depend indefinitely on tourism, remittances and external borrowing to finance its ambitions.\n\nThe country must aggressively expand higher-value export oriented manufacturing. And economic diplomacy must become part of that national export strategy."
