    "chunk_size_bytes": 1794,
    "chunk_text": "CSE opens the week higher as late buying lifts both indices\n\nThe Colombo Stock Exchange began the week on a positive note on Monday, 31 August 2026, with both benchmark indices closing marginally higher despite some intraday selling pressure.\n\nThe All Share Price Index gained 22.78 points, or 0.11%, to close at 21,338.69, while the S&P Sri Lanka 20 Index advanced by 4.35 points, or 0.07%, to end the session at 6,009.68.\n\nThe ASPI opened firmly above the 21,350 level and strengthened during early trading before gradually losing momentum through the middle of the session. Selling pressure pushed the index close to the 21,300 level during afternoon trading, although renewed buying interest towards the close helped the market recover and finish in positive territory. The S&P SL20 followed a similar pattern, surrendering its early gains before staging a late recovery.\n\nTotal market turnover remained relatively subdued at LKR 1.01 billion, with approximately 50.61 million shares changing hands during the session.\n\nThe Capital Goods sector dominated market activity, generating approximately LKR 300.64 million in turnover from 7.88 million shares. Sierra Cables recorded the highest turnover within the sector at approximately LKR 107.72 million.\n\nCrossing transactions contributed approximately LKR 106.20 million, representing around 10% of total equity turnover. The largest crossing was recorded in Access Engineering, where 1.00 million shares changed hands at LKR 75.00, generating turnover of LKR 75.00 million. Sierra Cables also recorded a crossing worth approximately LKR 31.20 million involving 850,000 shares.\n\nForeign investors remained net buyers for the session, recording a net foreign inflow of LKR 18.68 million, providing modest support to overall market sentiment."
