    "md5": "e47c43540add698671a8ee0cf613dfd3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1785,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka among \u2018Elevated Concern\u2019 countries for El Nino and Middle East crisis snapshot\n\nSri Lanka has been listed within the Tier 2 Elevated Concern Group countries for El Nino and Middle East crisis as of August 2026 snapshot jointly issued by OCHA, FAO, WFP, UN Women, and the WMO.\n\nLowest concern Tier 3 group includes countries such as India, Bangladesh, Thailand, Vietnam, Cambodia. High concern Tier 1 countries include Philippines and Myanmar.\n\nThe snapshot is structured in three parts and covers the period of September to November 2026. The three parts are climate risk outlook; convergence of pressures and from risk to action.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, as per the latest snapshot, the wetter signal becomes particularly important from October 2026 onwards, as it coincides with the Second Inter-Monsoon (October\u2013November), typically the wettest period of the year across the country, followed by the Northeast Monsoon from December to February.\n\nA substantial proportion of Sri Lanka\u2019s annual rainfall is received during these latter parts of the year, and the Second Inter-Monsoon is particularly important because rainfall is widespread across much of the island and can be locally intense. As with Pakistan, the evolution of the Indian Ocean Dipole, particularly if it strengthens into a positive phase, will be important in determining wetter conditions.\n\nThe convergence risk is particularly relevant as the Maha season begins. Diesel and gasoline remain around 44% and 41% above pre- crisis levels, respectively, while headline CPI has increased by around 6%t (WFP, 2026b). Sri Lanka is also exposed to fertiliser imports and\n\ninternational wheat markets, while elevated fertiliser prices during 2026 increased pressure on agricultural production (World Bank, 2026f)."
  },
