    "chunk_size_bytes": 1864,
    "chunk_text": "Three judges face bribery complaint over X-Press Pearl\n\nColombo, Sep. 1 (Daily Mirror) - A complaint has been lodged with the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption (CIABOC) against three Supreme Court judges over allegations that their actions resulted in nearly US$999 million in compensation that was due from the X-Press Pearl shipping company and its local representatives not being recovered.\n\nDr Chamal Sanjeewa, President of the Medical and Civil Rights Doctors\u2019 Association, submitted the written complaint to the Director General of CIABOC yesterday, calling for an independent investigation into the conduct of the judges in relation to the X-Press Pearl compensation case.\n\nThe complaint also raises questions over whether the loss of the compensation was connected to the implementation of the 22nd Amendment to the Constitution. Dr Sanjeewa has also filed a petition before the Supreme Court challenging matters relating to the constitutional amendment.\n\nAccording to the complaint, a five-judge Supreme Court bench headed by the Chief Justice delivered judgements in cases SC/FR/168/2021, SC/FR/176/2021, SC/FR/184/2021 and SC/FR/277/2021 and ordered the shipping company and its representatives to pay US$250 million by September 24, 2025.\n\nHowever, the complaint alleges that only US$1 million had been paid, leaving approximately US$249 million of that particular court-ordered amount outstanding.\n\nThe complaint alleges that although the petitioners sought action for contempt of court over the alleged failure to comply with the order, no such action was taken against the company by the three judges concerned.\n\nIt also alleges that Justice Yasantha Kodagoda, who had been the senior-most judge of the previous bench and was involved in preparing the judgement, was removed from the case without a valid legal reason."
