    "md5": "99924223001bfec34f6baf5c8bf4c98c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1875,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lankan-Origin Family Among Australians Missing in Nepal Floods\n\nABC - A Sri Lankan-origin Australian couple and their two young sons are among dozens of Australians still missing after catastrophic floods devastated areas along the Nepal-Tibet border, according to Australian media reports.\n\nChandrasekaran Muralitharan, 47, his wife, Shylaja Muralitharan, 42, and their sons Ashwin, 14, and Aarush, 11, were travelling with a Himalayan Glacier Adventures group when contact with them was lost.\n\nThe family lives in northwestern Sydney. Chandrasekaran is originally from Kopay in Jaffna, while he and his wife are first-generation Sri Lankan migrants to Australia, according to reports from Sri Lankan and Australian media. Their two sons were born into the Australian Tamil diaspora.\n\nThe family was on a pilgrimage to the Mount Kailash region when the floods struck on August 26.\n\nRelatives said their last contact with the family came on the morning of the disaster. They were preparing to have breakfast before travelling toward the border for immigration formalities, ABC News reported.\n\nTheir family was unable to reach them after that call as floods and landslides destroyed roads, electricity supplies and communications across large parts of the affected region.\n\nThe Muralitharans were travelling with a tour group that included 15 Australians. Members of the group were believed to have been near Rasuwagadhi, close to the Nepal-China border, when the disaster struck.\n\nAustralian authorities said Monday that 43 Australians remained unaccounted for following the floods. Many were pilgrims travelling through Nepal toward Tibet to visit Mount Kailash.\n\nThe wider disaster has become one of the deadliest Himalayan floods in recent years.\n\nFor relatives of the Muralitharan family, however, attention remains focused on finding the four missing family members."
