    "md5": "15c55c5b9cdb9891d681696f26bc5e9e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1809,
    "chunk_text": "KIST continues to expand sustainable Aloe Vera cultivation in Kilinochchi\n\nKIST, a brand with a heritage of over 70 years marketed by Cargills Food and Beverage Limited, has marked significant milestone in its Aloe Vera cultivation initiative with participating farmers delivering their first harvest to the KIST fruit processing facility in Kilinochchi.\n\nThe Aloe Vera cultivation programme was introduced through a contract farming model to promote sustainable cultivation and create long-term livelihood opportunities for farming communities in Kilinochchi. Implemented with the support of Deutsche Gesellschaft f\u00fcr Internationale Zusammenarbeit (GIZ) GmbH, the initiative focuses on empowering 100 women farmers by providing access to essential cultivation equipment, technical guidance and agricultural extension services. Farmers are also supported through financial assistance facilitated by Cargills Bank, with corporate guarantees from Cargills Food and Beverage Limited enabling access to financing without traditional collateral requirements.\n\nAs part of this initiative, KIST purchases harvested Aloe Vera at a pre-agreed price, ensuring a clear commercial pathway for farmers. The delivery of the first harvest reflects the successful implementation of this farming model. With continuous supervision and technical support from the company, the average yield per Aloe Vera leaf has increased from approximately 350 grams to 500 grams, demonstrating improved cultivation efficiency and farming practices.\n\nAloe Vera is widely recognized for its cosmetic and medicinal properties, with increasing consumer demand driven by its natural health benefits. The initiative supports the development of a consistent local supply of Aloe Vera in Sri Lanka while strengthening local agricultural production."
