    "chunk_text": "Salesforce deepens Sri Lanka push with key John Keells partnership\n\nFrom left: Salesforce Country Sales Head Sri Lanka Pablo Tachil, John Keells Holdings EVP and Group CIO Ramesh Shanmuganathan, Salesforce Managing Director India and Sri Lanka Mankiran Chowhan and John Keells Holdings Senior Vice President and Head of IT Retail Sector Osanda Warnekulasooriya\n\nPartners Keells Supermarket and JKCG Auto to drive AI-led customer engagement and automotive lifecycle management\n\nRolls out 4-pillar strategy spanning digital skills, start-ups, partner network and enterprise transformation\n\nGlobal customer relationship management leader Salesforce has signalled an aggressive expansion into Sri Lanka, anchoring its market push with landmark digital transformation partnerships across two prominent subsidiaries of John Keells Holdings PLC (JKH).\n\nThese strategic developments and corporate roadmaps were shared at a press conference held in Colombo yesterday, in concurrence with the company\u2019s inaugural Salesforce Innovation Day in Sri Lanka.\n\nThe newly announced enterprise collaborations involve Keells Supermarket and John Keells CG Auto (JKCG Auto), the authorised distributor for the BYD passenger vehicles in Sri Lanka.\n\nAddressing the media personnel and industry leaders, Salesforce Managing Director for India and Sri Lanka Mankiran Chouhan highlighted that Sri Lanka\u2019s macroeconomic recovery and digital trajectory present an ideal environment for technological expansion.\n\n\u201cToday we\u2019re in Sri Lanka and we\u2019re really excited about the opportunity,\u201d Chouhan stated.\n\n\u201cI think where you are as a country with regards to your digital ambitions\u2014whether it\u2019s the 15 billion mindset in the economy by 2030 or your 200,000 plus digital skilled labour that you\u2019re looking to create or the fact that you have a consistent year-on-year growth of 5 percent plus in the economy\u2014the momentum is right for us to partner with you in your country to help the businesses in Sri Lanka grow.\u201d"
