    "chunk_size_bytes": 1632,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka tourism reset targets reforms, investment and promotion\n\nSri Lanka tourism yesterday launched two World Bank-supported consultancy assignments to develop a national tourism strategy and global promotional campaign road map, as the country seeks to attract higher-spending visitors and generate greater economic returns from the sector.\n\nThe National Tourism Strategic Planning Consultancy for 2026-2031 will prepare a five-year framework covering tourism demand and supply, investment requirements, regulatory and institutional reforms, skills development, marketing and implementation.\n\nThe assignment was awarded to a joint venture between Spain\u2019s Aninver Development Partners and Sri Lanka\u2019s EML Consultants PLC.\n\nA separate Global Destination Communication Campaign Road Map Consultancy will develop a five-year international promotional strategy using market intelligence, destination analysis and global benchmarks. It was awarded to US-based Skift Inc. and Sri Lanka\u2019s MTI Consulting.\n\nBoth assignments, launched yesterday, are supported by the World Bank\u2019s Grant Facility for Project Preparation.\n\nForeign Affairs, Foreign Employment and Tourism Deputy Minister Prof. Ruwan Ranasinghe said the initiatives should help shift Sri Lanka away from pursuing the visitor numbers alone.\n\n\u201cWe are no longer interested only in volumes. We are a small island nation with sensitive ecological settings, a small population and fragile cultural practices and heritage,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cFor an island of this size and type, I think five million tourists would be the maximum. Now, however, we need to focus on value.\u201d"
  },
