    "chunk_text": "\u201cWe are no longer interested only in volumes. We are a small island nation with sensitive ecological settings, a small population and fragile cultural practices and heritage,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cFor an island of this size and type, I think five million tourists would be the maximum. Now, however, we need to focus on value.\u201d\n\nWorld Bank Lead Private Sector Specialist for South Asia Natasha Kapil said better data would be critical as Sri Lanka\u2019s tourism markets evolve.\n\n\u201cWe have witnessed a major shift in the types of tourists arriving on the island,\u201d she said.\n\n\u201cOnce we have gathered and analysed more intelligence and data, we will be able to develop much more targeted marketing approaches for the higher-spending visitor segments in Colombo and the other major tourism areas.\u201d\n\nThe assignments will complement the World Bank\u2019s wider tourism programme, including the proposed US $ 77 million Thrive Colombo operation, which aims to position the commercial capital as a 48-to-72-hour regional getaway.\n\nProf. Ranasinghe urged the consultants and authorities to ensure the recommendations are implemented.\n\n\u201cWe do not need just another report. We already have hundreds of them,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cWe need to implement these proposals on the ground and begin delivering results.\u201d"
