    "md5": "516bdcbfdd9bfe9e8901a980d376a5ba",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1876,
    "chunk_text": "Inflation overshoots CB band despite easing fuel prices\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s Colombo inflation accelerated to 8.0 percent in August, moving above the Central Bank\u2019s upper tolerance margin, as the food prices rose sharply and the transport costs remained the largest source of the annual price pressure.\n\nHeadline inflation, measured by the Colombo Consumer Price Index (CCPI), increased from 7.3 percent in July, recording its sixth consecutive monthly acceleration since February, the data released by the Census and Statistics Department showed.\n\nThe August reading exceeded the upper margin of the Central Bank\u2019s 5 percent inflation target, which allows a deviation of two percentage points on either side.\n\nHowever, compliance with the target is assessed using the quarterly average inflation, while a formal breach occurs only when inflation remains outside the specified range for two consecutive quarters.\n\nFood inflation climbed to 8.5 percent in August, from 6.3 percent in July, overtaking non-food inflation, which eased marginally to 7.7 percent, from 7.8 percent.Central Bank Governor Dr. Nandalal Weerasinghe said in a mid-August interview with Bloomberg\u2019s The Asia Trade that inflation was expected to decline towards the 5 percent target later this year and into early next year, provided the oil prices remained around the Central Bank\u2019s baseline assumption of US $ 80 a barrel.\n\nThe outlook could face pressure if food inflation remains elevated or the global oil prices rise above that assumption. While the lower domestic fuel prices helped contain the monthly inflation in August, transport remained the single largest contributor to the annual increase.\n\nDespite the acceleration in the headline rate, the price growth during August was relatively contained. The CCPI rose 0.28 percent month-on-month, compared with an increase of 0.24 percent in July."
  },
