    "chunk_size_bytes": 1959,
    "chunk_text": "The outlook could face pressure if food inflation remains elevated or the global oil prices rise above that assumption. While the lower domestic fuel prices helped contain the monthly inflation in August, transport remained the single largest contributor to the annual increase.\n\nDespite the acceleration in the headline rate, the price growth during August was relatively contained. The CCPI rose 0.28 percent month-on-month, compared with an increase of 0.24 percent in July.\n\nMilk powder made the largest positive food contribution during the month, adding 0.09 percentage points to the index. The higher prices for the dried fish, fresh fruit, ice cream and green chillies each contributed 0.05 percentage points.\n\nThese increases were partly offset by the lower prices for the sea fish, vegetables, limes, rice and red onions. The sea fish and vegetables reduced the monthly index by 0.14 and 0.10 percentage points, respectively.\n\nThe non-food items contributed 0.07 percentage points to the monthly increase. The higher costs for the household equipment and maintenance, restaurants and hotels, clothing, healthcare and communication were partly offset by the lower housing and transport costs.\n\nThe reductions in the petrol, diesel and liquefied petroleum gas prices helped moderate the monthly increase. The average petrol price declined to Rs.414 a litre in August, from Rs.419 in July, while diesel fell to Rs.382, from Rs.388.25. The average price of a 12.5-kilogramme gas cylinder dropped to Rs.4,590, from Rs.4,726.25.\n\nOn a year-on-year basis, however, the non-food items remained the dominant source of inflation, contributing 5.20 percentage points to the overall rate. Transport alone contributed 2.45 percentage points, mainly reflecting the higher fuel prices compared with August 2025.\n\nHousing, water, electricity, gas and other fuels contributed another 1.14 percentage points, while restaurants and hotels added 0.72 percentage points."
  },
