    "md5": "751c7be3aa370e4a8f6c04e1d523e4c5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1914,
    "chunk_text": "Apparel, tea declines pull Sri Lanka\u2019s July merchandise exports lower\n\nServices exports expanded an estimated 3.8% to US $ 351.92mn during month\n\nCombined merchandise and services exports for July stood at US $ 1.64bn\n\nCumulative analysis shows merchandise exports rose 5.1% to US $ 8.19bn during January-July, while services exports grew at a faster 7% to US $ 2.30bn\n\nThe lower earnings from the apparel, tea, seafood and coconut-based products outweighed the strong growth in electronics and spices, pulling the merchandise exports down 1.3 percent year-on-year (YoY) to US $ 1.29 billion in July.\n\nThe services exports, meanwhile, expanded an estimated 3.8 percent to US $ 351.92 million during the month, led by the information and communication technology (ICT) and business process management (BPM) services, according to the provisional data released by the Export Development Board (EDB).\n\nThe EDB estimated the combined merchandise and services exports at US $ 1.64 billion in July.\n\nThe apparel and textile export earnings, the country\u2019s largest merchandise export category, fell 8.8 percent YoY to US $ 437.59 million. The apparel shipments to all three major markets declined, with the exports to the United States falling 6.3 percent, the European Union 8.6 percent and United Kingdom 5.7 percent. The tea export earnings dropped 17.2 percent to US $ 116.73 million, reflecting a 27 percent contraction in bulk tea and a 10.1 percent decline in tea packets. The sector was hit by a 47.9 percent fall in shipments to the Middle East. The tea exports to the United Arab Emirates declined 57.5 percent, while the shipments to Iran and Iraq plunged 73.1 percent and 78.5 percent, respectively.\n\nThe seafood exports recorded the steepest sectoral decline, falling 60.3 percent to US $ 15.58 million. The frozen fish exports dropped 54.5 percent, while the fresh fish shipments contracted 81.2 percent."
  },
