    "md5": "666e3e592a3b6bd07dd357e932c7594d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1916,
    "chunk_text": "The seafood exports recorded the steepest sectoral decline, falling 60.3 percent to US $ 15.58 million. The frozen fish exports dropped 54.5 percent, while the fresh fish shipments contracted 81.2 percent.\n\nThese declines were partly offset by a near doubling of the electrical and electronic component exports to US $ 76.34 million. The insulated wire and cable shipments surged 150.2 percent, while the exports of boilers, piston engines, pumps and vacuum pumps rose to US $ 5.87 million, from US$ 0.21 million a year earlier.\n\nThe spice and concentrate exports increased 96 percent to US $ 72.14 million, driven by a more than fivefold increase in pepper shipments.\n\nThe rubber and rubber-based product exports rose 3.2 percent to US $ 87.24 million. The industrial and surgical glove exports increased 19 percent, while the shipments of gaskets, washers, seals and other hard-rubber products grew 47.6 percent.\n\nServices provided further support, with the ICT and BPM exports estimated at US $ 143.24 million in July.\n\nDespite the weaker monthly merchandise performance, the combined exports increased 5.5 percent YoY to US $ 10.49 billion during the first seven months of 2026.\n\nThe merchandise exports rose 5.1 percent to US $ 8.19 billion during January-July, while the services exports grew at a faster 7 percent to US $ 2.30 billion.\n\nThe growth during the period was supported by a 57.7 percent increase in the electrical and electronic component exports to US $ 377.91 million. The ICT/BPM exports rose 14.5 percent to US $ 1.02 billion, while the food and beverage exports grew 19.2 percent to US $ 405.22 million.\n\nThe United States remained Sri Lanka\u2019s largest single export destination, accounting for about 22 percent of the merchandise exports. The shipments to the market declined 0.6 percent to US $ 250.87 million in July and fell 1.1 percent to US $ 1.71 billion during the seven-month period."
