    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9d845392264b2589847bb990a5250fcc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1876,
    "chunk_text": "Promises alone would not defeat racism\n\nGiven several recent developments, it is timely that President Anura Kumara Dissanayake firmly stated his government would not tolerate racism from any ethnic group\u2014Sinhalese, Tamil, or Muslim.\n\nDuring the National Milad-un-Nabi or Holy Prophet\u2019s Birthday celebration in Negombo on August 26, the President said his government would not allow another ethnic or religious conflict to break out in Sri Lanka. He made this remark against the backdrop of some individuals and groups making malicious statements against some communities in mainstream media, social media and even in Parliament.\n\nAn ugly situation emerged several weeks ago where Tamil and Muslim social media activists traded religious insults against each other after a Muslim man in the east had kicked a dog that had urinated in his garden. Similar to the dog Aloka that accompanied a group of Buddhist monks on a Walk for Peace across the United States and Sri Lanka last year and this year, this dog had accompanied a group of Hindu pilgrims on the way from Jaffna to Kataragama, and was named by them \u201cSubramani.\u201d\n\nThe assault on the dog incensed some Hindu devotees and resulted in an ugly exchange of religious insults between some sections of the two communities on social media. Ultimately, a Sri Lankan Tamil youth was arrested in Saudi Arabia on charges of blasphemy and was given five years prison sentence and a huge fine of 3 million Saudi Riyals (approximately LKR 260 million).\n\nThen came the controversy over the uniform meant for\u00a0 female health assistants at hospitals, following nine Muslim health assistants attached to the Kalmunai North Base-Hospital who wanted to cover their heads and legs along with the standard state-prescribed uniform. This led to another wave of religious insults between\u00a0 Tamil (Hindu) and Muslim social media users."
  },
