    "md5": "0b8db2fced514d227120f3c4f3df4833",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1986,
    "chunk_text": "Then came the controversy over the uniform meant for\u00a0 female health assistants at hospitals, following nine Muslim health assistants attached to the Kalmunai North Base-Hospital who wanted to cover their heads and legs along with the standard state-prescribed uniform. This led to another wave of religious insults between\u00a0 Tamil (Hindu) and Muslim social media users.\n\nSubsequently,\u00a0 a person called Sangeeth Jayasekara from Boralesgamuwa claimed during a press conference that there was an LTTE training camp in the Kannagipuram area of Kilinochchi. However, before this could\u00a0 instill fear and suspicion among the Majority community towards Tamils, the authorities revealed that the claim was only a hoax, and the person who made this statement was arrested.\n\nIn another incident, Transport Minister Bimal Rathnayake accused Samagi Jana Balawegaya (SJB) MP Dayasiri Jayasekara of racism, claiming that the latter wanted police clearance for the appointees of the Wakf Board, the institution that governs the mosques in the country. He asked whether Jayasekara views all Muslims in the country as terrorists.\n\nThe viciousness displayed in\u00a0 social media posts and the follow up comments in connection with these incidents is a vivid testament that we are enjoying just an illusion of peace, and not\u00a0 real peace. The communal hatred in our society is a phenomenon that prevails like a ball pressed underwater.\n\nWith regard to the issue of the health assistants\u2019 uniform, Health and Media Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa told parliament that the concerned uniform was prescribed by a circular issued in 2019 and employees have to adhere to it until it is changed. This raises the question whether the authorities did not know about the Muslims\u2019 concerns about women\u2019s attire, even after it has been a major topic in Sri Lanka for the past two decades. This seems to be a result of absence of proper ethnic representation at the decision-making entities, including the Cabinet."
