    "md5": "ef4c577b83d9011bdfdf8a9d072ac421",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1302,
    "chunk_text": "Consumers deserve truth\n\nAloe vera is a medicinal plant long researched as a solution for gastritis and digestion. So, when brands market \u201cAloe Vera Yoghurt\u201d as a health drink, the public naturally assumes it is good for adults, especially diabetics and gastric patients.\n\nI checked the label of Aloe Vera Yoghurt, produced by one of the country\u2019s oldest conglomerates. It contains *10.8g of sugar per 100ml*. Another popular brand starting with the letter \u2018S\u2019 has similar sugar levels. Taste it and you will know why: it is very, very sweet. Clearly designed to attract children, not to heal adults.\n\nHere is the bigger deception:\u00a0 The most important ingredient \u2014 how much actual Aloe Vera is in 100ml \u2014 is NOT mentioned anywhere on the label and the sugar content is printed in unreadable, tiny letters, deliberately hiding the truth from consumers.\n\nIf this is truly a \u201chealth drink\u201d, then where is the \u201cDiabetic Aloe Vera Drink\u201d version with less sugar for adults who need it? Why are the makers of these products misleading the public by hiding behind the \u201caloe vera\u201d name while selling sugary yoghurt?\n\n1. Make it mandatory to declare Aloe Vera content per 100ml.\n\n3. Stop brands from using \u201chealth\u201d claims to deceive the gullible public with acidity health issues."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
