    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e10bfe23423067e989d2cf8bf1acbf22",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1687,
    "chunk_text": "Quality of life:  Is it the state\u2019s responsibility?\n\nThe state cannot take responsibility for the happiness of its citizens\n\nThe title of the NPP manifesto, \u201ca Thriving Nation, a Beautiful Life,\u201d suggests that those running the present government believe that the state has a responsibility to ensure a higher quality of life for the citizens. This issue was raised in a recent public discussion, in implicit criticism of my advocacy of reducing taxes and promoting exports as against actions to ensure a \u201crich life.\u201d\n\nThe state cannot take responsibility for the happiness of its citizens. Its responsibility is to preserve the basic conditions needed for citizens to achieve their own happiness. Reduce the barriers, but do not seek to ensure the outcome.\n\nThe events leading up to the economic crisis on 2021-22 are illustrative. Thousands came to the streets to protest the Gotabaya government\u2019s failure to ensure the supply of necessities such as cooking fuel, electricity, fuel for transport, and fertiliser and related items for farmers. They were not fighting for happiness (though a minority did raise related slogans), they were protesting the government\u2019s failure to continue the supply of the basics needed to achieve their own versions of happiness, or at least to reduce their unhappiness.\u00a0 What is a higher quality of life?\n\nIs one man\u2019s or woman\u2019s happiness the same as that of another? Anyone from a normal family who has organised a family trip knows the answer. If it is so difficult to ensure that everyone\u2019s happiness is optimised in a small group, how can the politico-bureaucratic machine known as the state do it for a much larger population?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
