    "md5": "2b28abc64c8f0894c8fb1a8bb8d3c54c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1807,
    "chunk_text": "Is one man\u2019s or woman\u2019s happiness the same as that of another? Anyone from a normal family who has organised a family trip knows the answer. If it is so difficult to ensure that everyone\u2019s happiness is optimised in a small group, how can the politico-bureaucratic machine known as the state do it for a much larger population?\n\nHappiness is subjective; to each his own. It is foolish to average these desires. De gustibus non disputandum est is an ancient aphorism, meaning \u201cin matters of taste, there can be no disputes.\u201d Personal likes and choices are different for everyone. You cannot prove a personal preference is right or wrong, so arguing about it is futile.\n\nSo, the ceiling is happiness but defining it and reaching for it is an individual\u2019s responsibility, with a little help from their friends. The state can ensure that we have a firm footing as we reach for the stars (or the ceiling). When there is no electricity, when there is no water in the tap, when transport is crippled and roads are blocked, when there is no fuel to cook with, one is unable to reach for the ceiling. One must spend all one\u2019s\u00a0 energy trying to stop falling through the floor.\u00a0 That which we cannot provide for ourselves\n\nThe floor that fell apart in 2021-22 was made of those elements mentioned above, especially for urban dwellers who powered the Aragalaya. According to the 2024 census 82.1 percent of urbanites use LPG gas for cooking (up 53 percent from 2012). While firewood is still common in rural and estate areas, gas use has been growing very fast from low bases in 2012-24: 202 percent increase to 35.1 percent of rural households and a 313 percent increase to 15.3 percent of estate households. No wonder the urban areas erupted against President Gotabaya\u2019s mismanagement of the economy."
  },
