    "md5": "4ab6da3a6e6c42b70d12a4e32a2c4c83",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1691,
    "chunk_text": "The floor that fell apart in 2021-22 was made of those elements mentioned above, especially for urban dwellers who powered the Aragalaya. According to the 2024 census 82.1 percent of urbanites use LPG gas for cooking (up 53 percent from 2012). While firewood is still common in rural and estate areas, gas use has been growing very fast from low bases in 2012-24: 202 percent increase to 35.1 percent of rural households and a 313 percent increase to 15.3 percent of estate households. No wonder the urban areas erupted against President Gotabaya\u2019s mismanagement of the economy.\n\nThere are other things we cannot provide for ourselves. Credentials that tell others that we have certain competencies are one example. It is possible to become an autodidact like Martin Wickramasinghe. But knowing is not enough for most people. There must be some respected body certifying that one has the required knowledge or skills. In modern societies it is necessary for the state to provide us with the means to establish that we are who we claim to be, to authenticate our identities. If any of these hard or soft infrastructures are taken away, our lives will be poorer; our striving for happiness will be made harder.\u00a0 That which we must get for ourselves\n\nSuppose that one person is made happy by travel and another by the quiet life. If the basics are in place, the wanderer can achieve happiness by earning enough money to travel. The person wanting the quiet life will have less need for money and can trade that off for greater non-working time. One may be a film buff and will need to have the time and money for enjoying films. Another would make different choices regarding time and money."
  },
