    "md5": "dac92cba215ccba6a83a62f6a55df682",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1591,
    "chunk_text": "Suppose that one person is made happy by travel and another by the quiet life. If the basics are in place, the wanderer can achieve happiness by earning enough money to travel. The person wanting the quiet life will have less need for money and can trade that off for greater non-working time. One may be a film buff and will need to have the time and money for enjoying films. Another would make different choices regarding time and money.\n\nAn interview about the new book \u201cThe time tax,\u201d began thus: one of the biggest problems in US politics is that the very people who Democrats, liberals and socialists think should be the most supportive of big government programmes intended to help them are often the least excited \u2014 and\u00a0 often vote for the party that wants to cut government spending.\n\nWhen I was informally conversing with President Gotabaya\u2019s advisors back in 2022 on how to face the onslaught of the coming crisis, we agreed that the poorest would bear the brunt and the government had to provide a safety net. The debate was whether it would be modelled on the pre-1978 rationing scheme where the authorities would decide what food items would be given out, or whether it should be cash transfers direct to the beneficiaries\u2019 bank accounts. I was pleased we went with the latter option which is the Aswesuma programme currently in effect.\n\nAswesuma recognises that people were affected by a crisis they did not cause and provides relief. But how that money is to be spent is left to the recipients. The floor is provided, but reaching the ceiling is up to individuals"
