    "md5": "56f2d48233b4de6c08d3c0116346aae6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1762,
    "chunk_text": "Japan, pacifist since 1947, is now vigorously militarising\n\nRising threats from China and President Donald Trump\u2019s reluctance to be the sole guarantor of security have pushed Japan towards militarisation\n\nMuch of Japan\u2019s defence spending so far has gone on missiles capable of striking targets more than 1,000 km away\n\nJapan was sworn to pacifism after the devastating impact that World War II had on it. Its 1947 constitution had Article 9, through which it explicitly abjured war. It gave up its sovereign right to wage war and also to maintain war potential. Post-war Japan handed over responsibility for its defence to the United States, the occupying power turned ally.\n\nBut there was no remorse because Japan\u2019s rather unflattering image, through the 1930s till 1945 as an expansionist power, had been shed. Freed from the burden of building its defences, post-war Japan spent the money and time thus saved to build itself up as a technological and trading world power with single-minded devotion.\n\nHowever, this is becoming history now. Japan has begun to arm itself vigorously and enter defence alliances in response to mounting threats from China and North Korea, coupled with US President Donald Trump\u2019s reluctance to be the guarantor of security to America\u2019s allies.\n\nRealisation had dawned as early as 1954, after the Korean War in which China and the United Nations, led by the US, clashed. It was at that time that Japan created the \u201cSelf-Defence Force\u201d (SDF). But still, the SDF was not meant to be deployed in offensive actions. Much later, in the 1990s, Japanese troops got involved in UN peacekeeping work.\n\nHowever, the major change came under Prime Minister Shinzo Abe (in power from 2005 to 2006 and again from 2012 to 2016). In"
