    "md5": "3bc6b13fe2caad7a630e8fe5c26d1155",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1853,
    "chunk_text": "Realisation had dawned as early as 1954, after the Korean War in which China and the United Nations, led by the US, clashed. It was at that time that Japan created the \u201cSelf-Defence Force\u201d (SDF). But still, the SDF was not meant to be deployed in offensive actions. Much later, in the 1990s, Japanese troops got involved in UN peacekeeping work.\n\nHowever, the major change came under Prime Minister Shinzo Abe (in power from 2005 to 2006 and again from 2012 to 2016). In\n\nA key reason for Japan\u2019s re-armament was the rising threat from China, which was aggressively claiming territories and building up a gigantic military. North Korea also posed a threat, armed as it was with missiles and nuclear weapons. Abe also desired that Japan should contribute to regional/international security rather than remain\n\nThere is another reason, which is unstated, and that is US President Donald Trump\u2019s demand that allies should not expect the US to finance and provide defence 100%. He has asked at least his European allies to raise their defence expenditure to 5% of their GDP. Japan, a strong US ally, has hiked up its defence expenditure appreciably. Its estimated defence expenditure is US$ 63.5 billion, which will be about 1.9% of its GDP.\n\nHigher defence spending, counterstrike capabilities, and manufacturing arms for export are among the steps envisaged. But the pacifist Article 9 of the constitution remains.\n\nIn an interesting development, in August 2026, Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi portrayed Japan\u2019s accelerating military buildup not only as a means to protect the country, but as a route\n\nThe latest defence White Paper portrays arms production as a support to economic growth, Reuters reported.\u00a0 The White Paper called for harnessing technology, funding startups and using more private sector-made components in weapons production."
