    "md5": "db7a9c38db2ac29c87db36c8e499914b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1795,
    "chunk_text": "The latest defence White Paper portrays arms production as a support to economic growth, Reuters reported.\u00a0 The White Paper called for harnessing technology, funding startups and using more private sector-made components in weapons production.\n\nTies between Japan and China sank to their lowest level in years, after Takaichi said in November 2025 that Japan could deploy its Self-Defence Forces if any Chinese attack on Taiwan also threatened Japan\u2019s survival.\n\nMuch of Japan\u2019s defence spending so far has gone on missiles capable of striking targets more than 1,000 km away. A significant share of any further budget increase is expected to go toward drones and other unmanned weapons of the kind deployed extensively by Ukraine in its war with Russia.\n\nAccording to \u201cDefence Security Asia, \u201d Japan has crossed one of the most politically sensitive and strategically consequential thresholds in its post-war defence posture by deploying its first domestically developed long-range stand-off missiles to frontline units.\n\nThe move places Japan within a rapidly expanding group of Indo-Pacific powers capable of striking hostile naval forces, missile bases and invasion corridors far beyond their own coastlines.\n\nThe Japan Ground Self-Defence Force formally redesignated its upgraded Type-12 missile as the Type-25 Surface-to-Ship Guided Missile, or 25SSM, while its island-defence hypersonic weapon was renamed the Type-25 Hyper Velocity Gliding Projectile, or 25HGP.\n\nTogether, the two systems represent the first fully domestically developed Japanese stand-off missiles to enter frontline service, giving Japan a new ability to threaten hostile warships, invasion fleets and selected land targets from distances previously unavailable to Japanese forces, Defence Security Asia said."
