    "md5": "7b0d00c9455c3fb72579730fb3904fea",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1644,
    "chunk_text": "Together, the two systems represent the first fully domestically developed Japanese stand-off missiles to enter frontline service, giving Japan a new ability to threaten hostile warships, invasion fleets and selected land targets from distances previously unavailable to Japanese forces, Defence Security Asia said.\n\nJapan\u2019s defeat in World War II led to a pacifist constitution\n\nTherefore, for the first time since the end of the Second World War, Japan is constructing an indigenous counterstrike architecture capable of influencing the military balance across the East China Sea, Taiwan Strait and wider western Pacific.\n\nThe Type-25 Surface-to-Ship Missile entered operational service with the 5th Surface-to-Ship Missile Regiment at Camp Kengun in Kumamoto Prefecture on Kyushu, placing Japan\u2019s new missile capability directly opposite the East China Sea and Taiwan approaches, Defence Security Asia said.\n\nDeveloped by Mitsubishi Heavy Industries, the 25SSM is mounted on a highly mobile truck-based launcher designed to disperse rapidly across Japan\u2019s southwestern islands and complicate enemy targeting. The launcher can be moved between roads, hidden sites and pre-prepared firing positions.\n\nJapanese planners increasingly believe that static air bases and command facilities could be rapidly neutralised during the opening hours of a regional conflict involving Taiwan or the East China Sea.\n\nFor that reason, mobile missile batteries now occupy a central place within Japan\u2019s emerging \u201ccounterstrike capability\u201d strategy, which seeks to hold adversary ships and missile sites at risk from beyond enemy air-defence range."
