    "chunk_size_bytes": 1809,
    "chunk_text": "Digital Era Global South should unite on common platform to bargain with Big Tech companies \u2013 Ranga Kalansooriya\n\n\u201cThe old systems are collapsing, but new systems are not emerging at the speed of those collapses.\u201d\n\n\u201cWe have a huge vacuum because the new information ecosystem has no borders, but our legal systems still operate within geographic boundaries.\u201d\n\n\u201cIn this changing environment, how are we going to sustain the fourth estate, which is of paramount importance in preserving a healthy information ecosystem?\u201d\n\nRanga Kalansooriya, PhD, the Asia Regional Advisor of International Media Support (IMS) for the past 17 years, has compiled his book \u2018The Youthquake\u2019, drawing on experiences from five countries \u2013 Sri Lanka, Bangladesh, Nepal, Myanmar and Indonesia. He examines how networked citizens fight for accountability, transparency and good governance without waiting for legacy media. The book is scheduled to be launched on Monday September 07 at the BMICH.\n\nRanga has gained international media development experience in both Asia and Africa. He is also a member of the Oxford Media Policy Summer Institute and holds Sri Lanka\u2019s first Ph.D. in Journalism. A former Director General of the Department of Government Information, Sri Lanka, and also of the Sri Lanka Press Institute, Ranga served in a diplomatic posting in Malaysia and also functioned as the spokesman for the Foreign Minister of Sri Lanka.\n\nIn an interview with Daily Mirror, he shares his insights on the future of democracy in the new information era and the need to secure the rightful place for legacy media to contain harmful content.\n\nThere is a concept called the fifth estate. The fifth column of the new democracy \u2013 the system where internet-connected citizens are challenging the establishments."
  },
