    "md5": "569f4830a2303e825089dff5a3e67374",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1756,
    "chunk_text": "In an interview with Daily Mirror, he shares his insights on the future of democracy in the new information era and the need to secure the rightful place for legacy media to contain harmful content.\n\nThere is a concept called the fifth estate. The fifth column of the new democracy \u2013 the system where internet-connected citizens are challenging the establishments.\n\nWhen I was listening to him, it triggered something in me that was really related to Aragalaya and also the Indian farmers\u2019 protest one year back. Then, after 2023, I was invited to Oxford Summer Institute again as a host person and a member of the organising team. Then, I continued to meet William Dutton and listen to him.\n\nI thought of validating his argument in Asia. Every year in Asia, we notice it. In 2024, we saw it in Bangladesh and, in 2025, in Nepal. In between, we had Indonesia and many other ongoing struggles. So, I thought of validating this argument of the fifth estate through these youth protest movements. That is how it started.\n\nQThe protests took place in different countries but the contexts were different. What is your analysis about it?\n\nI take your point, but if you go into deep analysis, the trigger was different. In Sri Lanka, there was an economic collapse. In Bangladesh, it was education reforms. In Nepal, there was a ban on social media. In Indonesia, it was Indonesian President Joko Widodo (Jokowi) bringing new laws to bring his son into politics or as a mayor. If you go into deep analysis of the contextual situations, all are the same. It comes down to accountability, corruption and wrong democratic systems or bad governance.\n\nThings were developing. Even in Sri Lanka, if you take the period between 2015 and 2017, things were developing."
