    "md5": "fabe02b6f921442eb4be3d6ed0aded48",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1737,
    "chunk_text": "I take your point, but if you go into deep analysis, the trigger was different. In Sri Lanka, there was an economic collapse. In Bangladesh, it was education reforms. In Nepal, there was a ban on social media. In Indonesia, it was Indonesian President Joko Widodo (Jokowi) bringing new laws to bring his son into politics or as a mayor. If you go into deep analysis of the contextual situations, all are the same. It comes down to accountability, corruption and wrong democratic systems or bad governance.\n\nThings were developing. Even in Sri Lanka, if you take the period between 2015 and 2017, things were developing.\n\nQCan traditional democratic institutions cope with the speed and amplification of political dissent on social media between elections?\n\nThere are two sides of the coin. One side is that checks and balances are now being done by citizens in a way that legacy media could not do. That was because legacy media was captured, politicised or commercialised. So, according to most of the protesters or these youths, legacy media was part of the problem.\n\nIn Bangladesh, they attacked the legacy media. In Nepal, they attacked the legacy media. They burned down legacy media offices.\n\nIn Sri Lanka also, the protesters encroached on legacy media newsrooms. They made their own demands. As we have seen, the good side of the coin is that citizens are taking checks and balances into their own hands and doing it now. If you look at the recent Ambalangoda police station incident, see how the citizens and social media users did the checks and balances against the law and order system in the country.\n\nThey were very strong in their opinion. The other side of the coin is, as you said, that the narrative can be manipulated."
