    "chunk_text": "In Sri Lanka also, the protesters encroached on legacy media newsrooms. They made their own demands. As we have seen, the good side of the coin is that citizens are taking checks and balances into their own hands and doing it now. If you look at the recent Ambalangoda police station incident, see how the citizens and social media users did the checks and balances against the law and order system in the country.\n\nThey were very strong in their opinion. The other side of the coin is, as you said, that the narrative can be manipulated.\n\nThe complexity is getting even deeper with more issues. The third factor is that all these platforms are not registered or within the geographical perimeters of the nation state.\n\nSo, who is responsible? Who is accountable? So, these are the issues. These are the dynamics.\n\nAs you very correctly said, the old systems are collapsing, but new systems are not emerging at the speed of those collapses.\n\nThat\u2019s a very good question because now the norms of democracy are being challenged.\n\nThe very institutions of the democratic system are being challenged. Challenged by citizens who are demanding more transparency and accountability.\n\nAs you said, the systems are not adapting to those new dynamics. We have a huge vacuum here because the new information ecosystem has no borders. But our systems are taking decisions according to geographic or legal perimeters \u2013 legal borders. Those two are not in parallel. Those are not compatible with each other. We are in a paradox.\u00a0 If you ask me what would be the future of democracy, I think we need new thinking, a new narrative. We need a lot of research, a lot of thinking, a lot of inputs for new policymaking in governance, given this new information ecosystem landscape. I don\u2019t have answers to that, but the answers are as complex as they are in the question.\n\nQHave the youth protests in Nepal, Bangladesh and Sri Lanka achieved what they initially demanded?"
