    "md5": "edb36b29cf9c1f0bd9b2fd8c7445d1b3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1839,
    "chunk_text": "As you said, the systems are not adapting to those new dynamics. We have a huge vacuum here because the new information ecosystem has no borders. But our systems are taking decisions according to geographic or legal perimeters \u2013 legal borders. Those two are not in parallel. Those are not compatible with each other. We are in a paradox.\u00a0 If you ask me what would be the future of democracy, I think we need new thinking, a new narrative. We need a lot of research, a lot of thinking, a lot of inputs for new policymaking in governance, given this new information ecosystem landscape. I don\u2019t have answers to that, but the answers are as complex as they are in the question.\n\nQHave the youth protests in Nepal, Bangladesh and Sri Lanka achieved what they initially demanded?\n\nThat is why I call it Youthquake. So, it could be a negative impression. For me, yes, it\u2019s a negative impression because we are still waiting to see the real change that we wanted in the systems.\n\nIn this digital era of widespread misinformation and disinformation, do you think greater media literacy and resilience among young people and society at large have become essential?\n\nWe call it harmful content \u2013 whether it\u2019s hate speech, disinformation or whatever you call it. That\u2019s why one of my arguments in my book is to say that the harmful content issue needs multiple solutions.\n\nOne solution is, of course, media literacy. You have to educate the user, the recipient, the audience, and also platforms and government. The most important factor, as my argument here, is good journalism. The fourth estate has to be strengthened further.\n\nThe problem is that the Fourth Estate is now facing multiple challenges. It is facing economic challenges because digital platforms are encroaching on local advertising markets. Business is affected by that."
