    "md5": "57877015e8103fd8790c927af6817b3c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1951,
    "chunk_text": "The problem is that the Fourth Estate is now facing multiple challenges. It is facing economic challenges because digital platforms are encroaching on local advertising markets. Business is affected by that.\n\nQCan you cite some examples from different countries in the world?\n\nThe main challenge is how to economically sustain the fourth estate. The problem is the economy. Journalists are not getting paid. Investigative journalism is not getting funded. Therefore, media houses cannot run.\n\nThe technology cannot be updated because of commercial issues.\u00a0 \u00a0Given that context, in some Asian countries, more than 50% of local advertising revenue is being captured by digital platforms based outside the country. All the money goes out of the country. Right. Some countries have taken certain steps now.\n\nFor example, in Indonesia, they are working with the Indonesian government. Work is in progress with the Press Council to find a mechanism to at least create a separate media fund. The Editors Guild of South Africa has gone to the Fair Competition Commission asking for its share from the local digital platforms.\n\nEurope and Canada have taken steps. These are individual case studies that we can strike.\u00a0 \u00a0But I don\u2019t think these individual efforts will bring the necessary results unless we come together as the Global South to create a common bargaining platform. Common bargaining.\n\nQCould a handful of technology companies trigger or influence social unrest to serve their own political or geopolitical interests?\n\nThat\u2019s another conspiracy theory. But if you really analyse the Nepali, Bangladeshi and Sri Lankan issues, and also issues in Myanmar and Indonesia, the ground realities create the necessary fertile ground for unrest.\n\nWhen the unrest is expressed using digital platforms, then the algorithms start getting activated. We know these social media platforms make money out of conflicts because of algorithmic intervention."
