    "md5": "8278f5cc614db37f1fadb85a57ba99d1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1952,
    "chunk_text": "When the unrest is expressed using digital platforms, then the algorithms start getting activated. We know these social media platforms make money out of conflicts because of algorithmic intervention.\n\nThat\u2019s the argument. On the other hand, some external international forces can use this as a tool.\n\nThat\u2019s a conspiracy theory, but conspiracy theories can be applied in a practical way. So, you have a point. This is the danger I\u2019m seeing.\n\nThis is the danger I\u2019m seeing in giving your fourth estate to another country\u2019s platforms.\n\nQDo you think technology companies are less likely to intervene in the political affairs of large markets such as India, while smaller countries like Sri Lanka, Bangladesh and Nepal may be more vulnerable to such influence?\n\nI fully agree with the market size approach. It all happens, but I don\u2019t think even the Cockroach Party could be controlled through interference along with the tech platforms. It\u2019s not easy.\n\nCockroach Janata Party is the largest mobilisation ever of youths within 24 hours on one social media platform \u2013 22.8 million youth on an Instagram page within 24 hours. That is a huge mobilisation. I don\u2019t think even tech companies would interfere in that.\n\nBut you are correct, Big Tech will listen to big countries because of their big market. India itself is more than 20% of the market of the Big Tech companies. Small countries like Sri Lanka, the Maldives, Nepal and Timor-Leste \u2013 who will listen to us? That is why I make this argument. We need a Global South common bargaining platform.\n\nQHow realistic is it now actually to unite the countries in the Global South to achieve that?\n\nNow, India is very big in the AI discussion. They had this AI summit early this year. South Africa is becoming very strong. Brazil and Indonesia are also there.\n\nBut they are doing it in their own bubble. We should break this bubble. I need China in this Global South bargaining platform."
