    "md5": "50563c1b03a4fb2bf6bea0741110f2a2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1976,
    "chunk_text": "Now, India is very big in the AI discussion. They had this AI summit early this year. South Africa is becoming very strong. Brazil and Indonesia are also there.\n\nBut they are doing it in their own bubble. We should break this bubble. I need China in this Global South bargaining platform.\n\nWe need big players. Elephants in the Global South should come together and protect small animals like us. That is for, you know, to regulate all these platforms in a better way without compromising transparency and accountability. There are different dynamics. These companies are drawing huge profits. They are economic, commercially driven companies.\n\nBut they have a political responsibility as well. So, they need to be very careful about how you handle politics here. The main issue here is what you call the word Frankenstein.\n\nThis particular social media dynamic has become a Frankenstein where even the creator cannot take control of it now. It has gone out of control, even with AI. The creators themselves cannot control it anymore because, I think, the issue has become too big even for the tech platforms.\n\nSo, we need \u2013 I think that is what we are seeing in the European appeal centre, the newly established appeal centre in Dublin, Ireland, under the European Union\u2019s Digital Services Act. I think this is perhaps the best mechanism ever established as a regional effort by the European Union.\n\nBut even that mechanism has its own challenges. For example, the tech company took the matter to court. It was supposed to be an out-of-court settlement, but the company went to court. So, I think there is a huge mess here now.\n\nWe need common platforms and common bargaining platforms. Big Tech companies also need to be united, but they are not united at the moment.\n\nThey are trying something in Singapore, where a new grouping is emerging as a possible solution. We need this kind of mobilisation of the Asian technology community \u2013 what they call the ATC, I think."
